| 2026-08-26 | §2 — one error experience, and the response that must stay silent | **A platform-wide error architecture, not a prettier 404.** `classifyError` turns any failure into one of nine situations a reader can act on, `ErrorPanel` is the single branded state, and `ErrorState` — 13 call sites including **every `DataTable`** — now delegates to it, with the table forwarding the real error so it can say *which* failure rather than «حدث خطأ». **It invents no second protocol**: TD-3.8's envelope stays the contract and the server's `code` and `request_id` are carried through untouched. Each state shows what happened in Arabic, what to do next, a **stable public code** derived from the class rather than the message (`BA-403`, `BA-NET`) so it survives rewording, and the server's own code beside it. **Retry is offered only where retrying could work** — never on 403, 404 or 401, where a button that will keep refusing is worse than no button; 401 offers signing in instead. | **The regression this section most had to avoid is the anonymous `/auth/refresh` 401.** The SPA refreshes on every page load, so on an anonymous page 401 is the correct answer and **not a failure**; it is handled where it happens and never reaches the architecture — now pinned by a browser check asserting the startup shows no error and no envelope. **Two references, never one pretending to be the other:** a `request_id` exists only when the request reached the server, so when it did not, **none is fabricated** — a made-up id sends somebody hunting logs for a request that was never there. A local report reference is shown instead, deliberately unlike a `request_id` in shape, with the explanation shown **only** when the network genuinely failed, so a caller that merely had no error object is told nothing about the network. **24 rendered assertions** cover all nine classes, each proving no stack trace, SQL, path or exception text can appear; **9 unit tests** cover classification and references; **5/5 browser checks** cover what only a browser can — the offline call really rejecting as a `TypeError` with the network cut over CDP, and the edge really returning **429** once the brute-force zone is exhausted. Building it corrected two faults in the harness itself: reloading while offline never serves the SPA at all, so the browser's own error page was being tested; and `/auth/switch-role` sits in the 120 r/m general zone by design (R60.3), so hammering it proved nothing. **Nine per-screen «فشل» strings stay inline on purpose** — an action failure beside its controls must not replace the screen behind it. Frontend lint, typecheck, **776/776**, build; **21/21** guards; 858 documentation links. | None. |
