| 2026-08-20 | Two things refused rather than invented | **Whole-Level only.** In the other two modes the branch is carried by the target itself (§7 — a group IS at one branch), so a branch list has no meaning: the write refuses it and the action is not offered. Whether combining Groups or Circles across branches means anything is an **open Owner question**. **The counterpart is never guessed**: two branches' schedules are structurally independent, nothing identifies *the corresponding lesson*, so the platform does not cancel the other occurrence — the administrator does it explicitly, through the flow that already asks whether to tell people. | Concurrency is the Session's own `version` (TD-15), never a second mechanism. Audited as `session.audience`, because *who decided both branches attend* is a question somebody asks later and the override table keeps no history of its own. | See above. |
| 2026-08-20 | Two defects the slice walked into | **A fixture that bypasses the write path tests something the platform does not do.** The browser seed recorded a one-off cover with a raw `sessionStaff` insert; materialization correctly resynced future un-overridden occurrences from the schedule and replaced it, so the مؤطِّرة's calendar was rightly empty and two checks failed. The seed now goes through `overrideSession`, which marks the occurrence `overridden` and makes the protection predicate spare it. | **And the trash-coverage guard earned its keep**: the new roster read fetched branches without `deleted_at`, and the guard named the line. Constrained — safe as well as consistent, because `session_audience_branch` holds a RESTRICT foreign key, so a branch named by an override cannot be deleted at all. | See above. |
| 2026-08-19 | R91 — teaching staffing becomes effective-dated | **`CourseScheduleStaff` gains `effective_from` / `effective_until`** — inclusive calendar dates (TD-11), `NULL` open-ended at that end. The platform can now answer *who teaches this class today, who taught it in October, who will teach it next month* — four questions that had one answer while an assignment had no period. A temporary replacement is **three rows, two of them Safa's**, and one edit produces October→Safa, November→Amina, December→Safa with **no occurrence touched by hand**. | **`@@unique(schedule_id, user_id)` withdrawn**: it encoded *one person, one assignment*, true only while assignments had no period, and it refuses the case R91 exists for. Replaced by two INTERVAL invariants under a `FOR UPDATE` row lock (TD-15.2's pattern) — at most one **main** مؤطِّرة per date, no overlapping periods for one person — with assistants deliberately exempt from the first. `EXCLUDE USING gist` was rejected: it needs `btree_gist`, which §3.1's deployment does not install. | `20260819230000_r91_effective_staffing` |
| 2026-08-19 | The migration that fabricates nothing | Every pre-R91 row carries **two NULLs**, which spans the schedule's whole life — exactly what a time-blind row already meant, so **no backfill**. Deriving an `effective_from` from `anchor_date` was rejected in terms: it would assert an assignment date nobody recorded, and would differ from the schedule's real beginning for every schedule created after its anchor. | The compatibility proof is behavioural, not a comment: **all 1349 pre-existing integration tests passed unchanged** on the migrated schema before a single new one was written. | See above. |
