| 2026-08-12 | R69 | **The two hierarchies get their own navigation — and the audit found the MODEL and the AUTHORIZATION already correct.** Nothing in the intended structure needed a schema change or a rule change: a Level belongs to a Category and not a Branch (§7), may exist with no Administrative Group and take students directly (**R66**); a Subject is independent, needs no Circle, and is taught to the whole Level through `entire_level` when unsplit (§7, §4.4c). **The permissions asked for were already in the services** — Level, Category, Subject and the Level↔Subject pairing are Super-Admin **write** and **Admin-readable**, and a Teaching Group's **structure is Super Admin while its MEMBERSHIP is Admin** and branch-scoped (R43.3). **Not one column, service, policy or matrix row changed.** **What was actually wrong: two screens had no way in.** `مواد المستوى` and `حلقات المواد` worked, but their paths carried ids, so **no menu could link to them** — §14.1 listed Subject Organisation while nothing could reach it. The consequence is exactly what was reported in use: the only entry points were **row actions borrowed by unrelated screens**, so `المستويات` grew a Subject action and `مجموعات المستويات` grew another. A navigation defect, not a modelling one. **One node each, with the id as a QUERY parameter** — `/admin/level-subjects?level=` and `/admin/teaching-groups?level=&subject=` — the pattern §14.1 already uses for `/resources` (R43: *"a second path segment would be a navigation node §14.1 does not list"*). Each screen selects what it needs **in the page**; the parameter is a deep link. **The old paths redirect** with `location.replace`, so bookmarks survive and the old URL does not sit in history for Back to return to. Adding menu entries to the id-carrying paths was rejected: a menu entry cannot supply an id, which is *why* they had no node, and a "pick a Level first" landing page would be a third screen for a question the destination can ask itself. **§14.1 restructured along the two hierarchies.** Administration, in dependency order: **الفئات → المستويات → المواد → مواد المستوى**. `المستويات` **moved here**: curriculum structure, Super-Admin writes throughout, and R66 removed the last operational thing it did. **Its read endpoint stays Admin-accessible** — scheduling, the placement dialog and the groups screen feed selectors from it, and gating the data rather than the screen would break an Admin's daily work (the R61 `GET /admin/branches` rule again). الشؤون التعليمية: **مجموعات المستويات · حلقات المواد**, both operational. **Both duplicated Subject actions removed** — neither screen is about Subjects. **A test I wrote the same morning was superseded by the fuller audit**, and the rewrite says so rather than quietly flipping: *"Levels stays Admin-readable — it is operational"* was true about the DATA and wrong about the SCREEN, because an Admin reaches Levels from the approval queue and the groups screen, each with its own selector. **Verified end to end against the running stack, 21 assertions**: an Admin reads levels/categories/subjects/level-subjects and is refused creating a Level or assigning a Subject; a Super Admin may. **R66 direct Level enrolment** (no group, branch on the enrolment) and **grouped enrolment** (branch taken from the group) both work. **`entire_level` scheduling accepted with no Circle in existence.** **R43.3's split proved live**: an Admin is refused creating a circle (403) and permitted placing a student in one (201). Both new nodes ship, both legacy paths redirect, all four URLs survive a refresh, and no row action in either screen links to a Subject any more. | Two §14.1 nodes; `المستويات` to `administration`/Super Admin; `LEGACY_SUBJECT_PATHS` redirect; Level selectors added to both subject screens; two borrowed row actions deleted | None |
