| 2026-08-12 | R68 | **The identity-binding review item — the last open item of Revision 62.** §4.3 (R62.9) mandated the behaviour and specified no mechanism; R68 supplies one and nothing else. **The state is reached by the STAFF path**, which is why it was worth building rather than deferring: staff pre-provision a student against a Google address, a parent is linked to them — **permitted**, because R62.9 restricts linking to accounts with no `UserIdentity` and a pre-provisioned one has none — and the student then signs in. That is simply how an older student who had a parent acting for them starts acting for themselves. **Verified end to end by driving a real login through `resolveLogin`.** **`FamilyLink.identity_review_raised_at`** is stamped on every approved live link of that student inside the binding transaction. **Non-blocking is enforced, not promised**: nothing reads it for authorization, `resolveActingStudent` is untouched, and the stamp is an `updateMany` no branch of `bindIdentity` can fail on — proved by asserting the login succeeds, the links stay approved and live, and **`X-Active-Child-ID` still resolves while the item is outstanding**. A **timestamp, not a status**: the link's `status` is a decided value (TD-1), and overloading it would make *"is this link approved"* and *"has anyone looked at this"* one question. **A fourth derived queue type, one item per STUDENT**, grouping every stamped link — the decision is about the arrangement, and an approver asked about each parent separately would be asked the same question twice. **Approve** clears the stamp and touches nothing; **reject** soft-deletes the links, which is §4.3's revocation mechanism since Revision 16. `familylink.identity_review` joins TD-8 recording *what was decided*, beside the existing `familylink.revoke` recording *what it did* — one row would have had to mean both. **Two structural guards earned their keep, and both findings were real.** The **Trash-coverage guard** caught the revocation writing a tombstone with **no Trash snapshot** — a revoked link an administrator could never restore (§7/TD-5). The **deletedAt-proximity guard** then failed because the new branch had been nested inside the family-link *not-found* handler, pushing two deliberate tombstone reads out of their explaining context; the honest fix was **structural rather than an exemption** — an identity review is a first-class type and now resolves as a sibling branch, which also fixed a live bug the tests found first: a student id **is** a user id, so `wasUser > 0` was answering *"already decided"* before the new branch ran. **Also closed: a stale TASKS item.** *"A branch created after Levels exist cannot be deleted"* was fixed by R66 retiring TD-4.6d's backfill; measured against the running stack with 20 Levels present — the branch creates no groups and deletes cleanly. | `family_link.identity_review_raised_at` + partial index; the stamp inside §4.1b step 4b; a fourth `ApprovalType` through service, DTO, query enum, adapter, badge and filter; `familylink.identity_review` in TD-8; 10 new integration tests | `20260812090000_r68_identity_review` |
