| 2026-08-11 | R66.5 | **Approval places a student either way: a Group when the Level is subdivided, a Level + Branch when it is not.** No new revision — **R66.5 already specified this** (*"the placement it demands is now a Level and a branch, with a group only where one exists, so an approver is never blocked by a missing group"*); this implements it. **One resolver, two callers.** `enrolAtPlacement` takes a `PlacementInput` union and dispatches to `enrolInGroup` or `enrolInLevel`; `decide()` and `decideChildApplication` both hand it one rather than branching themselves, because this project has paid repeatedly for one rule with two implementations. **The union is the type, not a validator's afterthought**: a shape carrying both would have to decide which wins, and one carrying neither is the missing placement §4.1 refuses. **The boundary refuses each failure by name** — both shapes at once, and half of the second (`level_id` without `branch_id`) — rather than letting the service guess. Three HTTP cases pin it. **The audit row records WHICH shape was used**, because *placed in a group* and *placed directly in a Level* are different decisions and a null would not distinguish them from a group a projection happened to omit. **The dialog offers every Level again.** They were excluded for one release — the honest workaround while a group-less Level left the confirm button `disabled` and produced no network request at all. R66 removed the premise, so the filter goes and a **branch selector appears exactly when the chosen Level has no group**, defaulted to the branch the applicant asked for. The *"no assignable Level"* notice retires with the filter that made it necessary. **TD-4.6d's backfill is now retired in code as well as in the SRS**: creating a Branch no longer creates one group per group-less Level — a placement decision nobody made, and one that would have re-broken the model R66 establishes. `backfillFirstGroups` is removed rather than left dead. **Verified end to end against the running stack**: approve into a group-less Level (no group, branch on the enrolment, right Level) · approve into a subdivided one (group set, branch taken from it) · both shapes → 400 · half a placement → 400 · no placement → `ENROLLMENT_REQUIRED` · and the served bundle carries the branch selector with the obsolete notice gone. **999 backend tests across 55 files, 385 frontend, 14 guards.** | `PlacementInput` + `enrolAtPlacement`; `Decision.enrollments` → `{ userId, placement }`; `DecideInput.administrativeGroupId` → `placement`; both wire schemas gain `level_id`/`branch_id` with exactly-one refinements; `PlacementDialog` gains a branch selector; `backfillFirstGroups` removed | None |
| 2026-08-11 | R66 | **A student is enrolled in a Level; a Group is a subdivision.** Owner approved both decisions the audit put to them; the revision is drafted, applied and implemented. **The whole revision is one move: the branch goes onto the `Enrollment`.** A student's branch existed ONLY through their Administrative Group — `Enrollment → AdministrativeGroup.branch_id`, named identically by §5.2 and R43.3 and read at **13 code sites**, with R43.3 calling it *"a referent that does exist"* because a Level spans branches. That is exactly why the group could not be optional: an ungrouped student would have had no branch, taking Admin scoping, Teaching-Group placement, the library's own-branch ordering and `entire_level` audiences with them. The enrolment IS the operational assignment, so the branch belongs on it. **`administrative_group_id` becomes nullable** and nothing else structural changes. **Agreement is enforced, not intended:** the composite FK `(administrative_group_id, branch_id) → AdministrativeGroup(id, branch_id)` — the same device R43 already uses for `level_id` — and because PostgreSQL leaves a composite FK unenforced when any column is NULL, **the ungrouped case needs no exception** while the grouped case cannot drift. Proven by the `move` path, which had to update both columns together or the constraint refused it. **Three rules retire, each having existed only to guarantee a Level had a group:** **TD-4.6b** (`createLevel` created the Level *and* its first group — which is why the form asked for a branch a Level never had), **TD-4.6d** (the bootstrap backfill), and **`LAST_GROUP_IN_LEVEL`**. `ENROLMENTS_EXIST` is untouched — it is the rule that actually protects people, and an EMPTY last group may now go. **`levelsWithoutGroups` survives as a report rather than an invariant.** **`enrolInLevel` is new** and is deliberately a second function rather than a nullable parameter on `enrolInGroup`: the two validate genuinely different things — one starts from a group and takes the Level and branch FROM it, which the composite FK then proves; the other is given both and must check them itself, including the caller's branch scope. Everything they share stays shared (§4.4b sex eligibility, BR-21's explained refusal, consent re-evaluation, the audit row). **The Subject side is untouched, as instructed** — §7 already said *"a Subject with no Teaching Groups in a Level is taught to the entire Level"*, so four of the six statements in the intended model were already the specification and needed no text. **Terminology unchanged**: the entity names encode §20 rule 22's organisation-versus-delivery distinction. **Migration is TD-6b expand → backfill → contract**, every value derived from data that already exists, and it **fails loudly rather than relaxing the column** if any row cannot derive a branch. **The 18 group-less Levels stop being a defect** and become ordinary Levels nobody has subdivided. **Tests: 995 backend green across 55 files.** ~100 failures were worked through, and the split matters — most were fixtures that had to name a branch, but ten encoded rules this revision RETIRES and were **rewritten to the new rule rather than deleted**, keeping the original intent where it survives: the last-group refusal became "an empty last group may go, and the Level stays enrollable", and TD-4.6b's block became "a Level is created alone" plus the two cases the revision exists for — a direct enrolment, and BR-21 still refusing a second placement. `createLevelSchema` gained `.strict()` so a client sending `branch_id` is **refused rather than silently ignored**. | `enrollment.branch_id` NOT NULL + composite FK + index; `administrative_group_id` nullable; `administrative_group_id_branch_id_key`; `enrolInLevel`; 13 branch reads moved off the join; Level create drops the branch end to end | `20260811210000_r66_enrollment_branch` |
| 2026-08-11 | UI fixes + deletion/deploy audits | **Four items fixed, three audited to a decision boundary.** **(5) Arabic-Indic digits removed platform-wide, and a 14th CI guard added so they cannot return.** They were mine, from the previous slice. **Arabic text, Western numerals**: every displayed number is also typed into a form, copied into a message or read down a telephone; reference codes (R62.6) are Latin-alphanumeric by construction; and `<input type="number">` and `type="date"` emit Western digits whatever we do, so converting elsewhere guarantees two renderings of one value. `check-western-digits.sh` catches the characters **and** the conversion idiom, and deliberately exempts comments, tests (a fixture named «فوج ١» is user DATA, not our formatting) and `file-types.ts` (a map converting Arabic-Indic digits INTO Western on the way in — the rule applied, not broken). **(4) The approval button produced no request because the DATA violates an invariant, not because the button is broken.** Traced UI → validation → mutation: `complete` requires a `groupId`, a Level with no live Administrative Group can never supply one, so the confirm button was `disabled` and no request was ever made. **18 of 20 live Levels have no group.** TD-4.6b makes `createLevel` produce a first group precisely so this cannot arise; these Levels entered another way (seeds/fixtures). Fixed at the UI so an approver is never left pressing a dead control: **unassignable Levels are excluded from the list rather than offered and then refused**, and when that empties the list the screen says why and links to the Groups screen. **The underlying data state and its interaction with item 1 are reported, not silently patched.** **(2) «＋ تسجيل طفل» removed from the account menu** — the personal section is one click away and carries the action with the context that explains it; a second entry point to one page is a second thing to keep in step. `/profile/register-child` is unchanged. **(7) `.field--choice` had NO CSS rule at all** and inherited `.field`'s `display: grid`, which stacks a control above its label — that is what put «يوم كامل (بدون توقيت)» out of line. One rule fixes **five** usages: a centred flex row, direction-agnostic so RTL needs no separate rule, with the input at its intrinsic size. **(6) Dates:** the shared formatter now prints Western digits; the native-input limitation stands as documented (the placeholder is the user agent's). **Audited to a decision boundary and NOT implemented — see the report:** item 1 (Level creation's branch coupling) conflicts with **TD-4.6b's atomic Level+first-group transaction**, which exists so nobody can be admitted to a Level with no group; removing the branch legitimises exactly the state that broke item 4, so it needs a revision rather than a form edit. Item 3 (per-child branch and category) needs **no migration** — `child_application` already carries `requested_branch_id` and `requested_category_id` per row since R64 — only the two forms and the registration validator treat them as request-level. Items 8 and 9 are audits by request. | 14th CI guard `check-western-digits.sh`; `format-date.ts` and `children.tsx` lose the digit conversion; `PlacementDialog` filters unassignable Levels; `.field--choice` gains its rule; `UserMenu` loses the duplicate | None |
| 2026-08-11 | UI audits + R64.7 evidence | **Eight product items; seven implemented, one blocked on a decision already on record.** **(1) The child section became ONE component** (`components/registration/children.tsx`) composed by `/register` and `/profile/register-child`. The two had diverged twice already — R62 unified the service and left the forms separate, R64 found the parent-facing one collecting no branch and no stage, and R65's personal page still had no repeatable section, so a parent of three submitted three requests from one page while the other took them in one. Fields, add/remove, the cap and the validation now live once; what stays with each page is the *request-level* answer (`/register` asks one branch and stage for the family, the personal page per submission). 9 tests, written against the shared module **and again through the personal page**, because the defect was never a wrong rule — it was the same rule implemented twice. **(2) الفئة — the adult option is NOT supported by the model, and the fix is blocked.** Traced submission → approval → identity → role: approval creates a `User` with **no `preProvisionedEmail` and no `UserIdentity`**, an approved `FamilyLink` to the requester, and `ConsentRecord`s whose `granted_by_user_id` is **the requester**. So choosing الكبار produces an account the adult **can never log into** (§2.1: adults hold their own Google-authenticated accounts), permanently readable by another adult, with consent recorded as given by that other adult (§4.1a: an adult consents for themselves). R62.9's guard fires only on `match_existing_user_id`; the create path has none, because for a genuine minor there is nothing to guard. **The Owner's three future cases are already served by adult self-registration** (§4.1b), which mints the Google identity and takes the person's own consent — the family mechanism exists for people who *cannot* hold a login. **The option cannot be removed without R64.7's decision**: R27 made the three Categories renameable generic rows, so nothing distinguishes the adult one structurally and filtering by the name «الكبار» would hardcode reference data the SRS expects to be renamed. Recorded, not guessed at. **(3) `.datatable__toolbar` aligns to `start`, not `end`.** Bottom-aligning the boxes lined up whatever each field happened to end with, and the search box carries a hint (TD-10's two-character floor) while the selects do not — so its input sat higher than theirs. Every field is label-then-control, so aligning the tops aligns the controls and a hint hangs below its own field. One rule, every toolbar on the platform. **(4) الشؤون التعليمية ordered general → specific** along R43's dependency chain: الفئات → المستويات → المواد → مجموعات المستويات. **And a §20 rule 22 conflation fixed:** the nav node read «الحلقات» — a *Teaching* Group, a Subject's circle inside a Level — while `/admin/groups` manages the **administrative** division OF a Level. Renamed «مجموعات المستويات». The last two rungs of the Owner's hierarchy (إسناد مادة لمستوى, حلقات المواد) are **parameterised screens, not menu nodes** — they cannot be linked without an id, §14.1 lists them under Levels, and the Levels table's subject count is the way in; adding nav entries would be invented navigation. **No authorization change.** **(5) Dates read in Arabic.** `lib/format-date.ts` — one formatter, month names from the catalogue the calendar already uses — replaces three inconsistent renderings (`2026-06-12`, `slice(0, 10)`, and a private helper inside one card) at every `<time>` on the platform. **The boundary is stated rather than hidden:** `<input type="date">` renders its placeholder in the **user agent's** locale and `lang`/`dir`/CSS cannot change it; only abandoning the native control could, at the cost of the platform picker and its keyboard and screen-reader behaviour. So `DateField` keeps the input and makes the rest Arabic — `lang="ar-MA"`, a hint naming the order, and the chosen date echoed underneath. **Stored and transmitted values untouched** (TD-11). **(6) Hero.** Title and lede replaced with the association's **own** words — its motto and its stated fields of work and aim — rather than a written-for-the-web paraphrase. `logo-large.png` was **colour type 2: fully opaque**, which is the background that showed inside the circular panel; converted to RGBA by a **border flood fill** (not a global colour key, so white *inside* the artwork survives) with feathered edges, then cropped 552×480 → 390×444 because the artwork sat off-centre with ~100px of dead canvas on one side and none on the other. The badge now uses `object-fit: contain` at full size: it fills the circle and **cannot** distort, since the logo is portrait and the badge square. **(7) The mission section is removed** from the landing page; its `landing.mission*` strings are **kept**, because it was removed *for now* and an unused key costs nothing while a deleted one has to be rewritten. **(8) Footer:** `landing.footerCity` removed, key and all — the branch list carries each premises' real address, so one hardcoded city was redundant and goes stale the day a branch opens elsewhere. **The sticky footer is a LAYOUT**: `#root` is a flex column of `min-height: 100dvh` and the page body takes the slack. On a long page the body is already taller, nothing stretches, and the footer follows the content exactly as before — no threshold, no margin, no `position: fixed`. `dvh` so collapsing mobile chrome does not tuck it under a toolbar. **Verified against what nginx serves**: the repeatable control, the toolbar's `align-items:start`, the four menu paths in dependency order, the Arabic format hint, the new hero copy with the old gone, the logo served as RGBA at 390×444 with `object-fit:contain`, the city line absent, and the `100dvh` frame. | New `components/registration/children.tsx` + `lib/format-date.ts`; `register.tsx` and `register-child.tsx` compose both; `admin-modules.ts` academic order; `admin.nav.groups` relabelled; `DateField` gains a hint and an Arabic echo; `#root` flex frame; `logo-large.png` RGBA + cropped | None |
