ut of the *calendar* but an association must be able to enrol before opening day. Written on the **applicant only**, never copied onto the child. **Verified live end-to-end**, not assumed: two registrations into two different branches; the queue showed each requested branch; `?branch_id=` returned exactly 1 and 1 with `meta.total` following the filter; the family-link item showed *(none)* unfiltered and was excluded when filtering; missing/unknown/soft-deleted branch and a stray `level_id` all returned **400**; approving reported `records_updated: 2` and left **0 role rows and 0 enrolments** — the branch is a request, and approval still grants no placement. Fixtures removed afterwards | New CI guard `check-migration-order.sh` (12 guards); `BranchSelector` promoted to §14.3's shared registry | `20260801194116_r39_user_intended_branch` — nullable FK, `ON DELETE RESTRICT`, partial index over pending rows |
| 2026-08-02 | SRS-R39b | **Two pre-existing defects found while implementing R39, both fixed, neither introduced by it.** **(1) A fresh `prisma migrate deploy` FAILED — the first production deployment would have broken.** `20260729060000_r36_1_display_name_not_blank` adds a CHECK on `public_display_name`; `20260729150624_r36_1_public_display_name` adds that column, **nine hours later in filename order**, and Prisma applies in filename order. Every existing database was fine because the two were applied in the order they were *authored* and `_prisma_migrations` recorded both as done — so the break was invisible to every developer and to CI, and would have surfaced **exactly once**, at §19.1 step 5 against an empty database. Found only because Prisma's shadow-database replay refused to create the next migration; **proven by running `migrate deploy` against a freshly created database**, before and after. Repaired by making both migrations **idempotent and order-independent** rather than renumbering one — a directory name is recorded in `_prisma_migrations`, so renaming orphans the row on every database that has already applied it. The two recorded checksums were re-computed in place (plain `sha256` of the file, validated against an *untouched* migration first) rather than resetting a developer database. New guard `check-migration-order.sh`, **proven by restoring the original defect**, names both the offending migration and the one that should have come first. **(2) TD-15.3 first-wins on approvals was never actually enforced.** `decide()` read the row `WHERE account_status = 'pending'` and then updated it; under `READ COMMITTED` two concurrent approvals **both** read `pending` — neither sees the other's uncommitted write — so both succeeded, writing **two `user.approve` audit rows for one decision**. The existing test caught it about **one run in five** and had been passing on timing luck since the queue was written; an R39 fixture change shifted the timing enough to surface it. Fixed with the `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` row lock the branch, room, group and roster services already use (§16.2 sanctioned raw-SQL exception (a)), on both the user and the family-link paths. **Verified 10/10 runs**, from ~4/5. The general rule is now written down: *first-wins needs a lock or a conditional update, never a read followed by a write* | Both defects documented in `architecture/database.md` and `architecture/backend.md` | See above |
