| 2026-08-04 | DOCS-13 | **`/compact` recommendations became evidence-based.** Document Owner correction: the previous recommendation was justified by *four process turns have accumulated context* — message-count reasoning wearing an efficiency argument, which is exactly what the policy exists to prevent. **The test is now a throughput estimate:** *would the next slice go faster from a compacted context than from this one?* — answered by separating **live** context (files the next slice will edit, fixture and test shapes it will copy, decisions in flight) from **spent** context (closed decisions, finished process discussion, superseded approaches, already recorded durably elsewhere). **The load-bearing insight: spent context is inert, not harmful.** Its presence is not a reason to compact, so the recommendation turns on the *live* portion only — recommend when the next slice's working set is largely disjoint from what is loaded, and **do not** when the loaded implementation context is what the next slice will build on, because a summary compresses that too and the re-reads it forces are a certain cost against a speculative saving. **The default is silence:** a wrong compaction costs a re-derivation of everything the summary dropped, paid in full immediately, while a conversation carrying inert history costs almost nothing — when the two are close, keep implementing. **A recommendation must now name three things or not be made:** what is spent and where it now lives, what the next slice concretely needs, and whether that need survives a summary. Message count, turn count and elapsed time are explicitly not evidence. **Applied immediately to the pending recommendation, which did not survive the new test and was withdrawn:** the process turns are spent, but the live context — the `teaching-group` HTTP fixture, `dto.ts`, the OpenAPI generator region, the controller shape — is precisely what Course Schedules and Sessions will copy, and it would not survive a summary intact. |
