| 2026-08-05 | M3b-14 | **TD-3.12 Sessions — the individual occurrence, mounted. TD-3.12 is now COMPLETE.** Five operations: `PATCH /sessions/{id}`, `POST /{id}/cancel`, `POST /{id}/restore`, `POST /{id}/content`, `DELETE /{id}/content/{contentId}`. **One verb per TD-1 transition, and `PATCH` is not one of them:** `status` is **refused** on the edit endpoint rather than dropped, because a transition carries obligations a field assignment cannot — a cancellation must state a reason (blank and whitespace both refused) and records the audience size **while it is still answerable**, and a restore is refused once the date has passed. Accepting `status` would give the state machine a second entrance with none of that attached. `schedule_id` is refused for the neighbouring reason: moving an occurrence to another schedule detaches it from the recurrence that explains it, leaving a row no schedule edit will ever reach again. **`overridden` means *a human decided about this occurrence*, not *differs from the schedule*** (R43.4) — set by any override including a no-op one, proven by a test that PATCHes nothing but the version and asserts the flag; inferring it from a difference would silently un-protect a session whose schedule later moved to match it. **`staff` supplied REPLACES the occurrence's snapshot, omitted leaves it untouched**, so an empty array is a real instruction (*this session has no staff*) and deliberately not the same as omission — the absent-stays-absent spread in the controller is what keeps the two apart. **Restore refused after the date** (`SESSION_IN_PAST`): a class that already did not happen cannot be asserted back onto the timetable, which would be a false historical record rather than a recoverable mistake. **Unlinking content NEVER deletes the file** — the content is a §4.9 library item with its own lifecycle, and removing it from one session's materials must not remove it from every other session, screen and download URL; the link row tombstones (TD