| 2026-08-06 | M3b-46 | **Upload `NOT_FOUND` traced to an unmounted route; Events and Schedules were soft-deleting without reaching the Trash.** **The upload failure was not in the service at all.** The envelope was `NOT_FOUND` with an **empty `details`**, and nothing in `content.service.ts` can produce that — every refusal it raises carries a `reason`. A nonsense path returned the byte-identical envelope, and the running image was timestamped **before** the commit that added the routes: `POST /uploads/initiate` was never on that router, so the request fell through to the `notFound` middleware (`request-context.ts:54`). Fixed by rebuilding the container. **The 401 that looked like proof the route existed was not**: `guarded.use(authenticate)` runs before route matching, so an unmounted guarded path answers `401` for a request with no token and `404` for one with a token — the first probe measured the middleware, not the mount. **A second, real defect surfaced underneath it.** With the route live, every upload still failed: the screen offered **every Subject in the platform**, while the server accepts only Subjects the Level actually teaches (R43) — and this database has **no `LevelSubject` rows at all**, so no choice could have succeeded. The Subject selector is now **the Level's**, loaded from `GET /admin/levels/{id}/subjects`; a stale selection is cleared when the Level changes, and a Level teaching nothing says so and names the screen that fixes it rather than failing at upload. **`upload.http.integration.test.ts` pins the mount as a property** — the service tests call `initiateUpload` directly and *no number of them* could have caught this, because whether `app.ts` wires it is a different question; one assertion now states that a bare `NOT_FOUND` from this endpoint is impossible by construction. **Deleting an Event or a Course Schedule wrote a tombstone and an audit row and NO `Trash` snapshot** — the record vanished from every screen and appeared on the one screen built to report deletions as nothing at all, and §4.10's restore runbook reads a snapshot that was never written. Both now snapshot, and both capture what their cascade destroys: an Event's four **scope joins are hard-deleted**, so the snapshot is the only surviving record that it ever reached anyone; a schedule carries its `CourseScheduleStaff` (without which a restored class is one nobody teaches) and the ids of the occurrences it removed. **Why it went unnoticed generalises, and is recorded rather than just fixed:** each of those services had a passing test for *"the row is soft-deleted"*, and that assertion is **true of a half-implemented delete** — soft deletion is two obligations, hide the row and record what was hidden, and a test for the first cannot see the absence of the second. So the new guard is **structural**: it reads the service sources and names any module that writes a `deletedAt` tombstone without calling `trash.snapshot`, with `enrollment.service.ts` as the one documented exception (un-enrolment ends a membership; TD-5 keeps the grades and the student). `Event` and `EducationalContent` join the Trash's blocked map with their cascade reasons. **Reported as working-as-designed, not fixed:** the الحلقات and الفئات refusals are correct guards — §4.4b forbids leaving a Level with no group, TD-5 refuses a Category while Levels reference it — and **both confirm dialogs already state those exact rules before the click**. Backend **861 passing across 47 files** (14 new). | The upload works; deletions are findable again | Two: the unmounted route, and the platform-wide Subject list |
