| 2026-08-06 | M3b-44 | **TD-3.5 storage — the upload pipeline, the presigned mint, and content replace/delete (SRS Revision 53 applied).** The five routes TD-3.5 specifies were **specified and never mounted**; `app.ts` had no `/uploads` at all, which is why the library's preview dialog has been returning *not available* since it shipped. **The browser PUTs straight to MinIO** through a presigned URL and the file never passes through the API — on a 4 GB VPS with mobile users, proxying a 100 MB upload through the container is not an option (§2.3) — so validation splits in two, and the split is not an implementation detail. **`/initiate` decides everything decidable before a byte moves:** the §4.9 branch scope, TD-9's whitelist, TD-9's cap, and the per-user quota. A teacher on a phone connection must learn they cannot publish Globally **before** uploading eighty megabytes. **`/complete` decides only what the object itself can answer** — a **ranged GET of `bytes=0-511`** for magic bytes and a **HEAD** for size (§4.9 Revision 8), never a full read — and a mismatch deletes the object, creates no row, and answers **409** `VALIDATION_FAILED`, which is the one place in TD-3.8's catalog where a code's status varies and the catalog says so itself. **`upload_id` is a signed ticket, not a table.** §7 defines no pending-upload entity, and inventing one would have been a schema decision the specification never took plus a table that can disagree with the bucket; `upload.gc` (TD-7) instead reaps **objects** older than 48 h that no content row claims, which is the thing that actually needs collecting. The ticket **binds every authorization decision taken at phase one** — without that, a Teacher could initiate inside their branch and complete into the Global scope, making the first check decorative — while title and description travel in the body, because no authorization turns on them and a 2,000-character description would have made a URL path segment a few kilobytes. Its key is derived from `JWT_SIGNING_KEY` by **HKDF under its own label**, giving TD-13's separation between token classes without a configuration variable TD-13 does not list. **A Teacher's branch scope resolves through `CourseScheduleStaff`** (§4.4c), not through their role assignment — the single definition of a teacher's reach, and reading the role scope would have been a second, quieter answer to the same question. **The mint is where three rules meet:** TD-12 freshness re-reads `account_status` and the role assignment from the database on every request, so a Teacher suspended mid-session loses a private recording **at once**; the §4.9 tiers are applied through **the same predicate `GET /library` uses**, because the rule that decides what a person may see in a list is the rule that decides what they may open; and §4.3's child context narrows the private tier to **that one child**, unlike the browsing surface — the two genuinely differ, and they differ in the direction that is safe. **The quota is 30/hour in PostgreSQL inside the initiating transaction** under a row lock (TD-4.12, TD-15.2), proven by a test where two concurrent initiations at the boundary admit exactly one. **Revision 53** gives replacement and deletion the contract TD-9 and TD-5 always assumed: **replacement extends `/uploads/initiate`** via `replaces_content_id` rather than becoming a route, because a replacement *is* an upload and a second route would be the same flow written twice — a **new key with a new hash segment**, the old object quarantined, `version` incremented, never an overwrite; **`DELETE /content/{id}`** is its own route because it moves no bytes in, and it **moves the object to quarantine rather than destroying it**, so BR-15's window means for a recording what it means everywhere else. **Video is refused**, and that is deliberate: TD-9's whitelist names no video type and §4.9 excludes it entirely, so accepting it is an Owner decision and a revision — recorded as a divergence from the brief rather than quietly widened. Backend **842 integration across 45 files** (23 new) and **156 unit across 14** (23 new); 12/12 guards; OpenAPI **71 paths / 94 operations**. | Uploads work end to end, and private content can finally be opened | Video uploads refused per TD-9 — Owner decision recorded |
