| 2026-08-05 | M3b-36 | **Recurrence edit scopes decided and specified before any code — `SRS-PROPOSAL-R50.md`.** Document Owner instruction: editing one occurrence of a recurring class must ask **which occurrences the change applies to** — *this session only* · *this and all future* · *all sessions* — for **every operation that can reach a series**, not only edits. **The audit found two of the three already built**, and this is why the proposal changes so little: *this session only* is `PATCH /sessions/{id}`, which sets `overridden` — R43.4's *a human decided about this occurrence* — and is thereafter protected by R43.6; *all sessions* is `PATCH /admin/course-schedules/{id}`, which already rewrites future un-overridden sessions and reports `resynced` and `protected_sessions`. **Only *this and all future* had no mechanism**, and the reason is one missing column: `RecurringCourseSchedule` carries `anchor_date` — where the recurrence starts counting — and **no end date at all**, so the model can say when a series begins and cannot say when it stops. **The Owner specified the split as the mechanism, and the proposal records why it is the right one rather than merely the instructed one.** An exception model — per-occurrence overrides of a rule — is the other common answer and is **explicitly rejected**: the platform already materializes every occurrence as a real `Session` (TD-4.6c) and already marks the ones a human touched, so a second way to say *this occurrence differs* would be **two representations of one fact**, the §20 rule 22 hazard; and §4.4 computes conflicts **against materialized sessions, never against rules**, which a rule-carrying-exceptions would defeat. A split, by contrast, is expressible in entities that already exist — two ordinary schedules — so **conflicts, rosters, the calendar and the Session page keep working with no knowledge that a split happened.** **One schema change proposed:** `effective_until` (nullable calendar date, TD-11), where **`NULL` means open-ended** — the value of every schedule today. With `anchor_date` it bounds one series, and materialization gains exactly one condition. **No new endpoint:** the scope travels as a parameter on the existing schedule edit, since a second route returning the same resource is duplication rather than separation (R45). **Four implementation traps recorded in the proposal rather than left to be rediscovered:** `horizonFor()` is already an upper bound and `effective_until` is a second, so the earlier wins; the condition belongs in `expandSchedule()` **and nowhere else**, or two expansions will disagree; the split must be one transaction, because a half-split leaves a gap in the timetable; and `CourseScheduleStaff` must be copied to the successor, or the teacher silently disappears from every future session. **`docs/SRS.md` was NOT edited** — it is immutable to implementing agents per CLAUDE.md, so the exact §0, §4.4, TD-3.12, §7 and §18 wording is drafted for the Owner to paste. **Nothing is built against this yet, by instruction.** Also audited and reported: Events and Session management are **frontend-only** (every endpoint exists), while **TD-3.5's storage routes are specified but not mounted** — `app.ts` has no `/uploads` — so content upload needs that backend slice first. Tree clean at 6b0753d; no code changed in this entry. | Recurrence semantics are now a recorded product decision rather than an open question | None |
