nts outside its own `TAG` namespace — restoring only its own spare. It left the development database with **zero active Super Administrators**, locked out of its own back office, **with every test green**. Found because a verification probe could not mint a Super Admin token; recovered by hand-restoring two `user_branch_role` rows identified by a shared `deleted_at` and a null `deleted_by`, the fingerprint of an `updateMany` rather than of a person. **Three fixes, not one:** the test now borrows `account_status` instead of the role grant (a status is trivially restored and means nothing alone, whereas a revoked assignment is indistinguishable from a deliberate administrative act — which is why the damage was hard to see as damage); it restores in `finally`, so an assertion failing mid-test cannot leave the platform administrator-less; and `afterAll` now **asserts the platform is still administrable**, turning a silent outcome into a failing run. Recorded in `development/testing.md` beside the consent-version incident it rhymes with — *a fixture may create and destroy rows it owns; it may only ever borrow rows it does not.* **One redundancy removed rather than worked around:** a test asserting the adapter carries no published-name field tripped `check-display-identity.sh`, and the temptation was to exempt the line. The assertion was checking a literal this file wrote against an interface that does not declare the key — the compiler already guaranteed it — so the **guard is the enforcement and the test was the weaker copy**. Deleted, with a note saying why. Frontend **260 passing across 19 files**; backend 783 across 42; 12/12 guards; build clean. | Admin portal: every §14.1 People and Academic node now operable | None |
