## NEW J/K/L — canonical Rooms, Categories and Levels (2026-08-27)

The Owner's canonical reference data, established as the seed baseline **and**
reconciled into the already-initialized development database in place.

- **NEW K — Categories** are المرأة (1) / اليافعات (2) / الطفل (3). The display
  order had been scrambled to 5/4/3 by the R76 reorder suites; it is now the
  Owner's order.
- **NEW L — Levels** are each Category's own named sequence rather than a shared
  number scheme, and الطفل additionally carries the explicit **المستوى 0** — which
  was soft-deleted and has been **restored by id**, not recreated by name.
  `display_order` is now 1-based and contiguous within each Category (R76.6); it
  had drifted to 11–16 and 20–26.
- **NEW J — Rooms**: nine teaching rooms (capacity 10) and قاعة المحاضرات
  (capacity 100) at each of مقر تاركة and مقر أمرشيش — 20 in total. Rooms are
  matched to their Branch **through the Branch row by id**, never by position.
  The existing قاعة رئيسية at each branch is the same single large room and was
  **renamed in place**, keeping its id and anything booked against it.

`backend/scripts/reconcile-reference-data.ts` is the new, idempotent home for
this. It exists because the seeders lay a baseline once and then never touch
those tables again (`seed.initialized.*`), so a change to the canonical list does
not reach an installation that is already initialized. It follows the protocol
already approved for the Subjects — normalize in place, preserve ids and
relationships, never create a near-duplicate, and **skip only the ambiguous row
and report it** rather than stopping the batch.

`prisma/seed/production.ts` now lays the canonical names on a fresh install, so
a new deployment and a reconciled one agree.

**Not done, and awaiting an Owner decision:** the Owner supplied a *description*
for each Category and Level. Neither entity has a `description` column, and §7
defines both as carrying only `name` and `display_order` (plus
`gender_restriction`) — the seed says so in a comment, deliberately. Storing the
descriptions is a schema change against a normative §7 clause, so it is reported
rather than taken.

## §8 — every table shows the meaningful fields of what it manages (2026-08-27)

The Owner's rule: *a table must show all meaningful fields of the element it
represents* — while technical internals (ids, hashes, audit plumbing, timestamps
used only internally) stay out, and actions stay actions rather than becoming
data columns.

The audit found the recurring shape UX rule P already names: **the fact was on
the row and the screen was not showing it.**

- **الجدولة** gained التاريخ, المادة, المكان, الظهور and عدد المؤطِّرات.
- **حصص الجدول** gained المكان, الظهور and عدد المؤطِّرات — all three already on
  the occurrence DTO. The tier shown is **the occurrence's own** (R109): after a
  single-occurrence override it differs from the schedule's, and showing the
  schedule's would hide the override.
- **المستويات** gained المجموعات, المواد and الظهور الافتراضي; **الفئات** gained
  الظهور الافتراضي.
- **المجموعات الإدارية** gained المستفيدات — a new derived count, added to the
  service, the DTO and both wire-contract pins. Derived per request, never
  stored: a stored count drifts the moment an enrolment is made anywhere else.
- **طاقم التأطير** gained الفروع, which المستخدمون already showed. The cell was
  **extracted** into `components/admin/branch-scope-cell.tsx` rather than copied,
  so R24's rule — `branch_id: null` is *all branches*, never *no branch* — is
  stated once. Copying it is how an unscoped Super Admin ends up reading as
  having no access at all.

### A test that asserted a global fact about a shared table

`registration.integration.test.ts`'s SIGKILL case asserted
`consumedToken.count({ purpose: 'onboarding' }) === 0` — every other assertion
around it is scoped to the test's own fixture. It passed only because the suite
emptied the table in teardown, which is the P1.2 leak that was just removed. It
now asserts what the test actually claims: **the crash spent nothing**, i.e. the
count is unchanged from before the child was spawned.

## NEW C closed — طلبات الانضمام proved to reorder in a browser (2026-08-27)

The fix shipped earlier; what stayed owed was the proof, because the queue holds
pending registrations and a healthy development database has none — one pending
user cannot demonstrate a reorder.

`scripts/dev/browser/verify-approvals-sorting.sh` seeds three tagged applicants
of its own and clicks both sortable headers, 7/7. The fixture's design is the
point: the three names sort أ ب ج, and oldest-submitted-first they sort ج أ ب —
**neither the same list nor its reverse**, so a screen that dropped the sort
parameter and returned its default order could not satisfy both assertions. A
single-column check would have passed while proving nothing.

Two expectations in the first draft were wrong and the application was right:
oldest-first is ج أ ب, not ج ب أ. Corrected against what the screen returned
after confirming it matched the seeded timestamps.

Also added §8's rule to the UX architecture as **rule BA** — a table shows every
meaningful field of what it manages, with the exclusions (technical identity,
audit plumbing, actions-as-columns) and the two consequences already met: show
the row's own value rather than its parent's, and a field the row does not carry
is a service change rather than a column.

## NEW E — «dirty» meant *has content*, not *has changed* (2026-08-27)

الملف التدريسي reported itself dirty whenever the profile held any subject,
category or availability range:

    const dirty = loaded && (subjectIds.length > 0 || ... );

So every مؤطِّرة who already had a profile opened the dialog **already dirty**,
and closing it without touching a field asked her to confirm discarding work she
had not done — precisely the nag rule AY exists to prevent, produced by the
mechanism meant to prevent it.

Now the shared `isDirty(current, pristine)` against the record the form loaded,
captured in the fetch rather than in a render (the timing trap `form-dirty.ts`
documents). Selections are sorted before comparing, because a multi-select
returns ids in click order; availability ranges are deliberately not sorted.

**Why it survived this long, and what that changes about the checks.** On an
empty profile the broken computation and the correct one agree — and every test
and browser check opened an empty profile. The new browser check is therefore
placed *after* the one that saves content, and the new unit guard was proved by
reintroducing the defect and watching it fail. Recorded as **UX rule AY.1**.

Two incidental finds:

- `session-audience-dialog` hand-rolled the same comparison correctly; moved
  onto the shared helper. Its guard pinned the literal `[...chosen].sort().join`
  — a mechanism — so it failed on a correct change; **restated as the property**
  (dirty is computed against `initial` through `isDirty`) rather than deleted.
- `verify-teaching-profile.sh` had been looking for the nav label «إدارة
  المؤطِّرات», which **R105 renamed to «المؤطِّرات»**. Two checks had been failing
  quietly ever since, and the QA inventory still recorded 13/13. Now 14/14.

## §10 — the recorder became self-contained, and uncovered a seeding race (2026-08-27)

**The rule AX carry-forward.** مكتبة المحتوى's recorder took its scope from the
**page filter bar** and refused to open until those filters were set. That broke
two rules at once: **AX**, because the four fields deciding what the recording
*is* were outside the form creating it; and **A/F**, because a filter was acting
as a **precondition** rather than narrowing what is visible.

`ContentRecorderForm` now carries the same five controls through
`useContentScope` — **extracted** from `ContentUploadForm`, not copied, because
both surfaces create the same object under the same scope and the same §14.1
visibility rule. Recording is deliberately not gated on a complete scope: the
refusal moved to **save**, beside the button, since losing captured audio to an
unset filter is the worse failure.

`session-materials-dialog` was left exactly as it is. The platform audit records
it as *Borderline, Owner decision*, and changing it would pre-empt that.

### The defect this uncovered — a guard that outlived its mechanism

Three checks in `verify-content-visibility.sh` had been failing and looking like
fixture noise. They were one real defect: **مكتبة المحتوى's upload dialog lost the
Subject its page filter had set, every time.**

NEW D replaced the Subject *fetch* with a lookup and kept `loadingSubjects` as a
constant `false`, documented as *"today's answer is always yes"*. But rule 2 — *a
selection no longer offered is cleared* — used that flag to skip the field while
its list was in flight. The window did not close; it **moved**: `levelSubjects`
fills and `ready` flips true in the same commit, while `options` was memoised
during that render from the still-empty `subjects` state. The seeded Subject was
not in an empty list, so it was cleared.

Fixed by deriving the Subject list **during render** rather than writing it to
state in an effect — removing the window instead of re-guarding it. The unit
guard now pins the absence of `setSubjects(` too, because restoring the effect
would restore the defect. **24/24** in the browser, up from 21/24.

Also: the first attempt mounted the recorder form unconditionally inside its
`<Dialog>`, putting a second set of scope selectors in the document — anything
reading the page by label found the hidden empty ones. Both content dialogs now
mount their form only while open.

## NEW O — the account-deletion design, all 35 relationships classified (2026-08-27)

`docs/SRS-PROPOSAL-R111.md`. **Design only — no schema, no migration, no code**,
as TASKS.md item 13 requires.

The thirty-five foreign keys were enumerated **from the live database**, not from
the schema file: a `RESTRICT` that exists only in a Prisma model is not the thing
that refuses at runtime. The count matched the recorded 35, and the three
`CASCADE`s turned out to be exactly the three rows carrying no institutional
meaning — the schema already encodes the distinction this proposal makes
normative.

**The finding that decides the design: twenty-six of the thirty-five must survive
the account.** A row cannot be deleted with twenty-six live references pointing
at it, `CASCADE` is forbidden in terms, and nulling the columns would destroy the
link that makes the surviving record meaningful — *a grade with no student* is
corrupted, not preserved. So account deletion is the **de-identification of a
`user` row that continues to exist**, which collapses thirty-five deletion
decisions onto the columns of `user` itself plus nine satellite rows.

Two things the enumeration settled that were open:

- **The hidden-item tension** TASKS.md flagged is sharper than «orphaned». R109
  resolves `hidden` to *the responsible person + Super Admin*, so deleting a
  responsible principal would leave a hidden item visible to **nobody at all** —
  it would disappear, not merely lose an owner. BLOCK.
- **Staffing splits by time, not by table.** `session_staff`, `event_staff` and
  `exam_staff` are PRESERVE for past occurrences and BLOCK for future ones,
  because R43.4 and R91 already forbid rewriting who delivered a class.

**Four questions are open at §7 and implementation is blocked on them** — chiefly
whether a مؤطِّرة may self-delete at all, given the responsibilities §3.3 gives her
that a beneficiary does not have.

## NEW K/L — a Category and a Level carry a description (2026-08-27)

Ratified by the Document Owner. §7 defined both narrowly and the seed said so in
a deliberate comment; the Owner's canonical dataset supplies a description for
every one of them, so the narrow definition was the thing that was wrong.

- **Migration** `20260827160000_new_kl_category_level_description` adds a
  nullable `VARCHAR(500)` to `category` and `level`. **Nullable and staying so**:
  a row without a description is ordinary, not incomplete — the treatment
  Revision 35 gave the Branch contact fields and NEW I gave the second phone.
- **A column, not a `system_setting` row.** The alternative was the pattern §4.9
  uses for default visibility. Rejected: that pattern is for a *policy attached
  to* a Category, whereas this is an attribute *of* it — read on every list, and
  routing it through settings would make one screen issue two reads for one row.
- **Super Admin CRUD end-to-end**: validators (`entityDescription`, shared in
  `common.ts`), services, DTOs, controllers, OpenAPI, adapters, both forms and
  both tables. `''` normalises to `null` at the boundary, because *no
  description* is one state and not two; absent and `null` stay distinct, so
  clearing a description is not the same as not mentioning one.
- **The shared taxonomy form gained a documented variant, not a sibling.** A
  Subject has no description, so `withDescription` is one declared flag rather
  than a second copy of that file (rule C). The Subject contract records the
  absence deliberately: a Subject is a curriculum word and needs no gloss.
- **Not hardcoded in the frontend.** The canonical text is loaded by the
  reconciliation script and seeded for a fresh install; every screen reads it
  from the API. Level descriptions are **derived** as `المستوى N - برنامج X` from
  position and Category rather than listed, so the two cannot disagree — and
  that text is seeded data, not a format the API imposes.

## R111 ratified — the account-deletion decisions (2026-08-27)

All four questions answered and folded into `SRS-PROPOSAL-R111.md` §7.
Self-deletion for **every** user including مؤطِّرات; an administrator may delete on
someone's behalf on the **same 3-day window**; the tombstone reads **«حساب
محذوف»** everywhere; and **BLOCK refuses with an explanation** naming what must
be reassigned rather than reassigning in the same action — the explanation is the
feature, and reassignment keeps its own authorization and audit.

Implementation (#14) is unblocked. Still open and deliberately not taken here:
how long the preserved records are kept (OD-06), and the copy shown to the person
deleting.

## NEW G — حسابي says where she is, not only who she is (2026-08-27)

The page could show a beneficiary her own name and not her own Level. That is
the recurring shape UX rule P names — a complete capability with no reach — on
the one screen that is entirely about her.

`GET /profile` now carries three things it did not, loaded in the **same read**
because this is one screen answering one question and the authorization is the
simplest there is: the subject is the JWT `sub`, so no scope rule applies and
none was invented.

- **`enrolments`** — Category, Level, Branch and group, rendered as
  `{Category} — {Level}` (rule D), since Level names are not unique across
  Categories.
- **`circles`** — her §4.4c Subject circles.
- **`guardians`** — **a name and the relationship's status, and nothing else.**

### The privacy constraint, and where it is enforced

NEW G's rule is a list of exclusions, and exclusions decay silently: nothing
fails when a field is added, the screen just starts showing it. So the rule lives
in the **server projection** — the guardian's contact details are never *loaded*,
which is what keeps them from being one careless spread away from the response.
A client-side filter would have been a second copy of the rule, and on this
project the copy that drifts still passes its own tests.

The open question *«which guardian fields may she see»* is answered by the
constraint itself: it already excludes them by default and requires that a field
a business rule needs be **reported, not assumed**. The conservative reading
shipped; showing more is an additive decision against a screen that currently
discloses nothing extra.

### One defect caught while writing it

`parentLinks` is the links on which a person is the **parent** — the children she
is guardian *of*. Reading it here would have shown a parent her own children
under a heading saying they were her guardians. The correct relation is
`childLinks`, and the guard pins it, because the failure is entirely silent.

The guard was proved by reintroducing a guardian-phone leak into the projection
and watching it fail.

### Two more harnesses were failing quietly against renamed labels (2026-08-27)

The same class as `verify-teaching-profile`'s R105 label, found by running the
harnesses rather than trusting their recorded counts:

- `verify-portals.sh` looked for «إدخال الحفظ», which **R106 renamed to «إدخال
  حفظ المستفيدات»** — deliberately, to say *whose* memorisation is recorded.
  Restated, not deleted: the property is that the entry is reachable, not what it
  is called. Now 25/25.
- `verify-student-flows.sh` check 11 is **fixture-coupled and is recorded as
  such rather than papered over.** Checks 1–3 cancel the beneficiary's class and
  check 11 then needs a scheduled one. Its diagnostic previously said only
  `none-found`, which cannot distinguish *the calendar had no class* from *the
  details did not say it was one* — the first is a fixture fact, the second a
  defect. It now lists the month's chips, and they show **no class chip at all**.
  Closing it properly needs the scenario to seed a second class the harness does
  not cancel; that is real fixture work and is left open rather than claimed.

**The pattern is worth stating once: a recorded «last run» count is evidence only
of the day it was recorded.** Three harnesses have now been found green in the
documentation and failing in reality, each because a label was deliberately
renamed and the harness was not.

## Account administration is Super Admin's; the operational directory is not (2026-08-28)

Owner clarification, recorded as authoritative in `SRS-PROPOSAL-R111.md` §6a.

`/admin/users` had one role list — `['admin', 'super_admin']` — covering two
different things: **global account administration** (every person, their address,
their status, their roles, and the power to delete the account) and **picking a
person while doing operational work** (staffing, enrolling, rostering). The Owner
separated them.

- **`/admin/users` and every write beneath it are Super Admin's**, asserted in
  the service so the refusal reaches every caller — tests and jobs included.
  Hiding the menu entry is not the enforcement, and the HTTP tests prove the
  refusal with forged requests, which a menu test cannot.
- **`/admin/directory` is new** — Admin or Super Admin, answering *who may I
  staff, enrol or roster*. Exactly `id`, `name_arabic`, `nickname`, `roles`.

**The projection is the point, not a side effect.** Five operational screens were
receiving every user's **email, phone, account status and TD-15 version** in
order to render a list of names. They now receive none of it, and the exact key
set is pinned so a field added to the account projection cannot arrive here
unnoticed.

**The scope rule is shared, not copied.** Both surfaces run one unexported query
with no role assertion of its own, so they cannot drift on *which rows* a
branch-scoped Admin may see while differing on *what is returned about them* —
which is the only difference intended.

### A guard restated because the Owner changed the rule, not because code drifted

`user-management.http.integration.test.ts` asserted **404, never 403**, for a
branch Admin editing an out-of-scope user: §20 rule 17, because *"exists, but not
yours"* is itself a disclosure. That was right while an Admin could edit *some*
accounts.

An Admin is now refused the **capability** rather than the row, so **403 is the
non-disclosing answer, and uniformity is what makes it so** — the same status
comes back for an out-of-scope user, a user in the caller's own branch, and an id
that does not exist. The test now asserts exactly that: three very different
targets, one answer. The scope-based rule moved to `/admin/directory`, where a
branch-scoped Admin *is* authorized and out-of-scope people simply do not appear.

Two frontend guards were restated the same way, and say so in place.

## R111 — account deletion (2026-08-28)

Implements the six Owner decisions. `backend/src/services/account-deletion.service.ts`.

**Deletion is the de-identification of a row that continues to exist.** Twenty-six
of the thirty-five relationships referencing `"user"` must outlive the account,
thirty-two are `RESTRICT`, and `CASCADE` is forbidden — so the row cannot go, and
nulling the columns would destroy the link that makes the surviving record mean
anything.

- **`DELETE /profile`** — every authenticated user, no role gate. The subject is
  the JWT `sub`, so nobody can name someone else, and a role has never been a
  reason to be unable to leave. A role can **block**, never forbid.
- **`DELETE /admin/users/{id}`** — Super Admin only, same 3-day window.
  **`?permanent=true`** performs the de-identification now: the operation the
  window reaches, **not a stronger one**. Idempotent.
- **Blocks**, each naming what to move: `LAST_SUPER_ADMIN` (the platform's
  existing guard, applied rather than rewritten) and `RESPONSIBILITIES_ASSIGNED`
  with a `blocked_by` breakdown. Responsibilities split **by time, not table** —
  past staffing is preserved history (R43.4, R91), future staffing is an
  unfilled obligation.
- **Three days, and BR-15's ninety untouched.** `snapshot()` gained an optional
  window; every existing caller keeps what it had.

### Three things the implementation had to correct in itself

- **A fifth `account_status` value was drafted and reverted.** The schema already
  records the opposite decision at the enum: TD-1's Deleted state is
  `deleted_at IS NOT NULL` *"so a soft-deleted user has exactly one source of
  truth instead of two that can disagree"*. My own justification was the inverse
  of that reasoning and weaker. The migration was withdrawn and the enum value
  removed from the development database.
- **The TD-10 search shadows are trigger-maintained.** Clearing `name_arabic`
  while leaving `name_arabic_normalized` would keep a deleted person findable by
  search — the one place the omission shows on no screen. They follow the columns
  they shadow, so the correct assertion is that the **original** is gone from
  them, not that they are null. Setting them explicitly would have looked like
  the safeguard and been decoration.
- **The suite's teardown found its rows by name prefix**, which deletion breaks:
  a de-identified account is renamed «حساب محذوف» and stopped matching, so rows
  accumulated and would have changed the all-table isolation snapshot (P1.2). It
  now tracks ids, which never change.

**One construction is in the code for a reason I could not fully explain**, and
says so in place: the block check reads staffing rows and asks about their
parents by id, rather than using a nested relation filter. The nested form was
rejected at runtime inside the request path while the identical query succeeded
from a script against the same client in the same container. Rather than ship a
construction whose behaviour I could not account for, it is written the plainer
way — which also reads as what it means.

## NEW N — partners are data, and the landing page reads them (2026-08-28)

Ratified as **R113**; `docs/SRS.md` gains five TD-3 routes, one §14.1 node
(الشركاء, in الإدارة) and one TD-2 row, applied at the Document Owner's
instruction.

- **`Partner`** — `name`, `display_order`, `is_visible`, TD-5 soft delete,
  TD-15 version. Hand-written migration with a `display_order >= 0` check.
- **`GET /partners`** is public and is §5.1's only source, exactly as the branch
  directory is: a partner added in the back office appears with **no frontend
  change and no deployment**.
- **`/admin/partners`** — Super Admin only (OD-01's sub-decision), asserted in
  the service. New screen in الإدارة, placed after أنواع الجدولة on that
  section's own logic — the dependency chain, then the standalone nodes.

**A partner is a NAME.** No logo, URL, description or contact; the write schema
is `.strict()`, so a client sending a logo is **refused rather than silently
dropped** — a `201` after sending one would reasonably be read as its having been
recorded. Asserted at the boundary.

**`is_visible` is not `deleted_at`**, and the tests say so: a withheld partner is
absent from the public list and still present in the back office. Withholding one
while a relationship is renewed is an ordinary need and not the act of removing
the record.

**The section renders nothing at all when no partner is visible** — specified
behaviour, not a degraded one, and the reason it differs from the branch list
beside it: an empty branch list is a *fault* (the association has premises), while
having no partners is ordinary, so a heading over an empty area would report an
absence nobody asked about. `verify-partners.sh` proves the absence in a browser,
which no unit test can observe — a component test asks what the component
returns, never what the page looks like around it.

### The four names are NOT seeded, and that is the finished state

They are referred to as already provided and are **not recorded anywhere in this
repository** — `docs/`, the task log and the change log were searched. The brief
forbids inventing anything about a partner, and a name is the *whole* of this
entity, so `PARTNERS` in the seeder is an empty array with a line saying why.
**Owner decision, 2026-08-28: ship empty and enter them through the new screen** —
no deployment needed, and nothing is fabricated. Filling the array later affects
fresh installs only.

### One gap found and reported, not fixed

`/admin/scheduling-types` (R110) is in the navigation registry and in the
harness, and **§14.1's sitemap does not list it**. The registry guard did not
catch it because its expectation is hand-maintained — the exact failure its own
comment warns about: *"a test that pins one copy against another copy cannot
catch that; only re-reading §14.1 could."* Adding it is the Document Owner's
edit, and it is outside R113's approved scope.

## NEW P — the legal pages (2026-08-28)

`/privacy` and `/terms`, public and unauthenticated. They waited for R111 for the
stated reason: **a privacy policy cannot describe a retention model that has not
been decided**, and now it can.

**Every statement is traceable to something the code does.** The Google section
names the three scopes `oauth.ts` actually requests — `openid email profile` —
and says what they are used for; the deletion section describes R111 exactly:
sessions end at once, three days to change your mind, then the personal details
go and the educational, consent and safeguarding record stays. That last
sentence is the one the whole page exists for — **an interface promising deletion
while §4 retains the record is the one genuinely unacceptable outcome**, so the
policy says plainly what survives.

**Nothing legal is invented.** The legal entity, registration numbers,
addresses, CNDP references and governing law each render a visible
«⚠ معلومة مطلوبة من الجمعية» marker. A plausible-looking legal detail is worse
than an obviously missing one: the first is trusted and wrong, the second asks to
be filled in. `legal.test.ts` pins that — including that the retention period is
stated as **undecided**, because OD-06 says it is.

### Google's OAuth requirements, verified against Google's own documentation

Checked on 2026-08-28 against `developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/policies`
and `support.google.com/cloud/answer/13464321`, not against repository notes:

- the homepage must be on a **verified domain**, describe the app, and **link to
  both the privacy policy and the terms**;
- the privacy policy **should be hosted within the domain that hosts the
  homepage** and **linked from it so users can find it easily**;
- it must explain how the app **accesses, uses, stores and shares Google user
  data**;
- **verification is triggered by sensitive or restricted scopes.** The platform
  requests `openid email profile` only — all non-sensitive — so app verification
  is **not** required; brand/domain verification for the consent screen still is.

The footer carries both links on every public page, which is why the requirement
is met by one implementation rather than by a link the landing page remembers and
the others forget. **No verification was submitted**, as the brief directs.

Remaining before launch: the domain must be verified in Google Search Console
under a project owner, and the consent screen's privacy-policy URL must match the
deployed one. Both are deployment steps, not code.

