## R111 deletion, restoration and responsibility invariants are now real (2026-08-28)

An adversarial read of R111 found several independent false claims behind green tests.

- Permanent deletion changed only the composed display names. The separately stored Arabic
  and French name parts, notes, registration-request metadata, spoken identifier and old QR
  remained, as did refresh-session and quota rows. The operation now clears the complete
  person-side allow-list, rotates the first external QR correlate, removes every declared
  credential/planning satellite, and keeps the stable email lock rather than racing its
  deletion. The lock owns nothing; the two cleared ownership channels release the address.
- The recoverable Trash JSON kept the original name, phone and email after permanent
  de-identification. It is now deleted in the de-identification transaction, while the audit
  trail and institutional relationships remain. The regression creates a real family link,
  identity, refresh session and quota row and reads every one back.
- The interface promised three-day restoration while Trash labelled every User
  `CASCADE_RELATIONSHIPS` and refused it. R111's soft-delete phase actually removes none of
  those relationships, so User joins the tested restorable set; revoked sessions correctly
  stay revoked. The old test's claimed preserved grade did not create one, and its replacement
  now proves a real safeguarding relationship survives.
- The responsibility check queried a nonexistent generic `Event.date` at runtime and treated
  every non-deleted course assignment as live forever. It now uses the Event's actual bounded
  date fields and both effective-dated schedule/staff bounds, so future responsibility blocks
  while past delivery remains evidence.
- Session regeneration and schedule splitting also read those effective dates now. An ended
  former teacher is neither written back onto a later occurrence nor treated as a live staffing
  obligation on the successor schedule.
- Account deletion, every supported staffing mutation and future-Exam restoration now share
  deterministic User locks and active-account validation. This closes both directions of the
  race: no deleted account can be assigned a new obligation, and a committed assignment is
  visible to the deletion refusal. A binned future Exam can no longer be restored onto a
  supervisor deleted in the meantime. The platform-wide last-Super-Admin count is separately
  serialized on the stable `super_admin` Role row; locking two different User rows was not a
  global invariant.
- De-identification re-reads `deleted_at` after normalized-email and User locks. A concurrent
  Trash restoration can therefore win safely: the stale purge refuses `NOT_DELETED` instead of
  erasing the restored person. A converged retry neither rotates the QR twice nor writes a second
  `user.deidentify` audit fact, including when a legitimate original name already equals the
  tombstone label.
- The final adversarial pass found four stale-writer variants behind the same erasure boundary:
  teaching-profile/availability saves, safeguarding-profile upserts, notification inserts and
  upload initiation/publication had all resolved a live User before their write transaction, so
  they could recreate a deleted satellite or mint/publish new upload authority after the purge.
  They now take the governing User locks in deterministic UUID order and revalidate after locking.
  Forced concurrent regressions prove both serial winners converge on no planning, case-file,
  inbox or quota satellite attached to the tombstone.
- The broader verification falsified three old fixture claims. Restoring only a SystemSetting's
  JSON still changed its version/updater; deleting a database delta of consumed tokens could
  remove a real user's replay guard; and an audit-purge proof consumed matching ambient history.
  Fixtures now restore the whole logical setting row, own exact JTIs from issue time, and run the
  fixed-clock platform purge inside a rollback. The first full rerun then exposed a fifth
  onboarding suite previously masked by the broad cleanup (`consumed_token 0 → 14`); its exact
  14/14 regression now leaves the database snapshot unchanged.
- The first affected-suite rerun passed all 137 assertions but the all-table guard caught
  `audit_log 139 → 92`: fixed suite tags let a restarted process adopt and delete 47 audit rows
  attached to older interrupted fixtures. No assertion observed the loss. The six affected suites
  now use run-unique ownership prefixes; the identical 137-test rerun and the 58-test R111 rerun
  both leave every application-table digest unchanged.

The automatic end of the three-day window remains blocked rather than fabricated: R111 calls
for a pg-boss purge, but TD-7 has no such queue and the current SRS still carries pre-R111
"no account deletion" clauses/routes. The exact Document Owner reconciliation is recorded in
`TASKS.md`; manual immediate de-identification is complete, automatic expiry is not.

Verification on the final diff: focused real-stack R111/trash/exam/schedule **157/157**;
focused setting/registration/approval/email/audit isolation **87/87**; staff-registration
ownership **14/14**; backend unit **285/285**; complete real-stack integration **89 files,
1861 passed, 10 skipped**, with the before/after digest of every application table identical;
frontend **857/857**. Backend and frontend lint, exact typecheck and Production builds pass;
all 27 CI guards pass, documentation links are **883/883**, OpenAPI is current at **130 paths /
172 operations**, and `git diff --check` is clean.

## TD-14 no-PII logging boundary (2026-08-28)

The Production-readiness log audit found four ways personal or sensitive data could cross a
boundary whose comments claimed it was absent. Nginx trusted a public `X-Request-Id` verbatim and
logged the raw URI plus client address; Express logged `req.path`, including arbitrary unmatched
paths; both the HTTP error handler and failed job-runner startup copied raw exception messages;
and `user.create` duplicated the pre-provisioned mailbox into an indefinitely retained AuditLog
row. A caller could therefore place an email in two operational logs merely by requesting an
unknown path or setting one header, while driver/storage errors could disclose SQL, connection
strings or filename-derived object keys.

The edge now generates the correlation id and forwards only that id. Its structured record omits
URI and client address; its non-customisable error log is process-emergency only (`crit` has
request-context call sites and was rejected during adversarial review). Express accepts
only Nginx's 32-hex id or a UUID, logs the registered route template / `<unmatched>`, and emits a
fixed internal-error operator message. Job-start failure logging names the failed stage without
the exception text. Staff pre-provisioning records `identity_channel = pre_provisioned`, role,
scope and target User id, never the mailbox. Three behavior tests inject the same email-shaped
value through the header, matched/unmatched paths and exception message; the user-service
integration proves audit attribution without identity duplication. A new CI guard pins the Nginx,
runtime and AuditLog source boundaries, and `nginx -T`/`nginx -t` verify the repository-mounted
configuration under test.

One specification contradiction remains explicit rather than invented: TD-8 says
`auth.login`/`auth.login_denied` detail includes identity email, while TD-14 and §20 rule 18 say
never log email. Current code already follows the stricter rule and remains unchanged; `TASKS.md`
asks the Document Owner to remove that TD-8 field (recommended) or define a controlled exception
with access/retention basis. The launch checklist therefore remains open on that decision.

Verification: hostile-value middleware regressions and the complete backend unit suite pass
**288/288**; the real-PostgreSQL staff pre-provisioning integration passes **38/38** with an
identical all-table before/after snapshot. Backend lint, exact typecheck and Production build pass.
All **28** CI guards pass (including the new no-PII guard); documentation links are **883/883**;
OpenAPI is current at **130 paths / 172 operations**, with **0** implemented TD-3 endpoints
undocumented; and `git diff --check` is clean. `nginx -t` and the loaded `nginx -T` pass against
the repository-mounted config. A live hostile-header/path request receives a generated 32-hex id,
and its Nginx record contains only that id, method, status, byte count and timings.

## Durable audit-detail minimisation (2026-08-28)

The follow-on TD-14 audit found a broader retention leak behind the pre-provisioned mailbox:
domain services copied catalogue names, event titles, Trash labels and filename-derived object
keys into `AuditLog.detail`, whose non-authentication rows are retained indefinitely. Those
copies added no attribution beyond the target id, while R111 could erase a person's identity
from its governed row and still leave the same value embedded in unrelated audit JSON.

The sole audit repository write now recursively refuses identity/display property names and
exact storage locators before the surrounding domain transaction can commit. Catalogue actions
record target ids and changed field names. Content upload, replacement and deletion record a
deterministic SHA-256 identity of the exact bucket/key coordinate; exact locators remain only in
the content/Trash/pg-boss records that must execute the transition. Current upload retries
reconstruct the canonical key from the signed finalization id plus the accepted content digest,
while rows from older releases retain their read-only raw-key fallback. Real MinIO regressions
prove publish/retry/replace/delete still converge and that an email-shaped filename never enters
the new audit detail.

The guard intentionally does not sanitize reasons, notes or setting values. TD-8 explicitly
requires some of that evidence while TD-14 prohibits PII in logs; it also literally requires
identity email and raw storage keys in two audit-grid rows. The three smallest Owner/Document
Owner reconciliations are recorded in `TASKS.md`; current implementation follows the stricter
boundary for unambiguous system-copied values without inventing a policy for mandated free text.

The first complete integration sweep then exposed an independent falsely-green fixture. The
social-profile retention test invoked the real platform-wide audit purge with a 2099 horizon and
checked only that its safeguarding row survived; it passed while deleting **83** pre-existing
eligible authentication audit rows. That purge and its assertion now execute inside an
always-rolled-back transaction. The deleted rows were not fabricated or restored, and the one
concurrent refresh-token addition observed in the same digest was left as Owner activity rather
than misclassified as fixture leakage.

Verification: backend unit **298/298**; focused real PostgreSQL/MinIO integrations
**364/364** across 14 files, each with an identical all-table before/after digest; backend lint,
exact typecheck and Production build pass. All **28** CI guards pass; documentation links are
**883/883**; OpenAPI is current at **130 paths / 172 operations**, with **0** implemented TD-3
endpoints undocumented; and `git diff --check` passes. The complete real-stack catalog passed
**1,861 assertions with 10 skipped twice**. Its first wrapper comparison exposed the now-fixed
83-row destructive fixture. The post-fix comparison observed only concurrent non-test activity:
one `courseschedule.create`, its 23 non-test-tagged Sessions, and login/refresh/logout rows; no
test-tagged schedule or Session survived. Those Owner rows remain untouched. A third shared-state
sweep was refused on safety grounds, and the repository has no disposable full Nginx/API harness;
the focused corrected purge proof is independently isolation-clean **19/19**.
The direct-Prisma audit-write guard was also mutation-tested: an unreachable
`prisma.auditLog.create` in a domain service was detected at its exact line, then removed.

## User, group and scheduling-type corrections (2026-08-28)

Built on Codex's R111/TD-14/audit hardening; every guard it added stayed green.

### مجموعات المستويات — one question had two predicates

Deleting a group answered *«تسجيلات مستفيدات (2)»* while its roster read *«لا
توجد مستفيدات»*. Both counted `Enrollment` rows: the roster required a **live
student**, the deletion refusal — and the `member_count` column beside it — only
a live enrolment. The rows between them are **R111's and are correct**: account
deletion preserves the enrolment because the educational record outlives the
account, so a group whose members had deleted their accounts held live rows and
no beneficiaries. Measured, not inferred: the one group in the database showed
`blocker=2, roster=0`, both enrolments belonging to «حساب محذوف» tombstones.

`policies/enrolment-membership.ts` now holds the predicate once and all three
reads use it. The roster's answer is authoritative — a de-identified tombstone is
not a beneficiary — so a group whose members have all left can be closed, while
one holding a live student is refused exactly as before. Invested records still
block under their own name: a `Grade` is unaffected.

### A pristine form asked to discard nothing

تعديل المجموعة warned *«هناك تغييرات لم تُحفظ»* on an untouched form, and nothing
in the caller was wrong. The close button ran with `dirty` false and called
`onCancel`; the parent cleared the row; for the one render before the reset
effect, the still-loaded fields compared against an empty pristine and read as
dirty; and `Dialog`'s native `close` event **re-entered `requestClose` inside
that window**. `useUnsavedGuard` now derives `open && dirty` once — a form that is
not open holds no unsaved work — which fixes every always-mounted `FormDialog`
of that shape rather than one caller.

### المستخدمون

- **الأدوار merged into the edit form.** One form, one decision; the separate
  dialog and row action are gone.
- **A role is held once per account** (Owner decision). `UNIQUE (user_id,
  role_id, branch_id)` never prevented it, because **PostgreSQL treats NULLs as
  distinct** and `branch_id IS NULL` is the ordinary all-branches scope — the
  platform's own Super Admin held `super_admin` twice. A partial unique index on
  live rows now enforces it, the service refuses a repeated role with
  `DUPLICATE_ROLE`, and the dropdown offers only roles not already held.
  **Multi-branch scoping is withdrawn deliberately** (ratified): a role carries
  one scope. Moving a scope **revokes and re-grants** rather than rewriting the
  row — the first draft mutated `branch_id`, and the existing TD-5 regression
  caught that it destroyed *«who taught at this branch in March»*.
- **The person fields are registration's**, through the same `PersonFields`
  component and the same limits (`validators/person.ts`). The form had one
  «الاسم» box holding the **composed** name, so a staff member retyping it became
  the authority on how it reads — which §1.1 composes server-side to prevent —
  and the French name, sex and notes were uneditable.
- **A taken email says so.** `DUPLICATE` mapped to *«ابدئي تسجيل الدخول من
  جديد»*, advice that cannot work when the address itself is the problem. The
  server already distinguished `EMAIL_ALREADY_CLAIMED` under its lock; the client
  now does too.
- **«حساب بلا دخول» is not a product state** (Owner): every account is created
  through registration with a Google address. The label now names the one real
  case — an account pre-provisioned and not yet signed into.

### أنواع الجدولة — عطلة is its own structural kind

`محاضرة` was an activity and is a **class**; `عطلة` was an activity and is now
**`holiday`**, a new `SchedulingStructuralKind`; the missing generic `نشاط` was
added. Corrected **in place** — ids preserved, nothing recreated, and verified
first that no `Event` referenced any of them. A holiday carries **الفرع and
الفئة only**: Levels, groups and staff are refused at the write boundary
(`HOLIDAY_SHAPE`), not hidden by the form, so a forged request meets the same
rule. The fields a class has and an `Event` does not — القاعة, المادة, الحلقة,
نمط التدريس, طريقة الحضور — need no refusal: there is no column for them.

### The constraint caught a fixture, which is what it is for

The first full sweep failed one assertion: `consent-safeguarding` granted its
actor `admin` at a **second branch** to give it reach over both. That is the
multi-branch form the Owner withdrew, and the new index refused it. The fixture
now widens the existing assignment to the all-branches scope (`branch_id: null`
is *all branches*, never *none*), which is how that reach is expressed in the
current model — and which the test's own token already claimed.

### Two SRS conflicts surfaced rather than resolved silently

- **R80 point 6** said `sex` *«stays off every contract»*. The Owner amended it:
  المستخدمون is Super-Admin-only since R112, and a Super Admin reads and edits
  everything about an account. It is published on **that read alone** —
  `/admin/directory`, the Admin-reachable surface, still does not carry it.
  R80.3/R80.4 are untouched.
- **Minors and guardian emails.** The Owner states every account has an email and
  a minor signs in through their guardian's. §4.3 currently models a minor as a
  login-less row. That is a model change with its own slice; this batch changed
  the label only and did not touch §4.3.

## Users, enrolments, scheduling and partners (2026-08-28)

### الشركاء — حذف did nothing, literally

`classifyDeletion` takes the **caught error**; الشركاء passed it a **callback**:
`classifyDeletion(() => deletePartner(id, token))`. A function is neither `null`
nor an `ApiError`, so it fell through to `failed` — and `deletePartner` was
never invoked. Confirmed at both boundaries: **no DELETE reached nginx or the
API**, while the reader was told the deletion had failed. My own defect from the
NEW N slice.

The parameter is `unknown`, which a function satisfies, so the type checker had
nothing to say. `classifyDeletion` now **throws** on a callback rather than
returning a tidy `failed`: a caller that passed an operation has not deleted
anything, and reporting a neat failure hid that for a whole release. Every other
delete screen was checked; partners was the only misuse.

### الجدولة — permanently dirty on key ORDER

`isDirty` serialised both snapshots and was order-sensitive for object keys, on
the documented reasoning that both sides are built by *the same literal in the
same file*. الجدولة falsified it: its two snapshots are **separate literals**,
and `schedulingTypeId` sat second in one and twentieth in the other. Every value
matched — the instrumented comparison found **no differing field** — and the form
was dirty from the moment it opened, so إضافة عنصر and تعديل العنصر asked to
discard nothing.

`isDirty` now sorts object keys and **keeps array order significant**, because a
reordered list is a genuine change. That is the second distinct cause behind one
sentence; the first (a closing form's cleared pristine) was fixed earlier, and
the harness now covers both.

### المستخدمون

- **Every assigned role's scope is editable.** It rendered as text with only a
  Delete, so narrowing or widening meant removing the role and adding it back —
  losing the assignment's history for a change that is not a revocation. Saving
  revokes the old row and grants the new one, so *«who taught at this branch in
  March»* stays answerable, and one role still carries one scope.

### المستفيدات — one row per مستفيدة

The page listed **enrolments**, so a مستفيدة in two Levels appeared twice and one
in none did not appear at all — on the screen whose job is enrolling her. It now
lists students, each with every Level, group, branch and circle she holds.
`＋ تسجيل مستفيدة` is gone: تسجيل starts from her row, so the form no longer
opens by asking a question the row has answered. Branch membership stays
read-only here and is edited on تعديل بيانات المستخدم, as the Owner requires.

**Who counts as a student, stated rather than assumed.** The Owner's rule is
*every account holding the Student role*. R79.7's rule is the durable
`is_beneficiary` fact, and it exists because **role membership does not identify
a beneficiary** — a مؤطِّرة may study, and §4.3's minors hold no role at all.
Either alone drops people from the screen that places them, so the page shows the
**union**. Every Student-role account appears as required, and nobody already a
beneficiary disappears. **If the Owner wants the role alone, that is a one-line
change and this note is the flag.**

### الشركاء — الوصف, end to end

A nullable `VARCHAR(500)` on `Partner`, through the validators, service, both
DTOs, the form, the management table and the public landing section. `''`
normalises to `null`: *no description* is one state, not two. `＋ إضافة شريك`
moved into `AdminLayout`'s shared header slot, where the other eleven management
pages put it.

### A harness that borrowed another suite's fixture

`verify-unsaved-guard`'s pristine-close check seeded its group against `LIMIT 1`
of whatever branch existed. Run beside the integration sweep it took the branch
the recording suite was about to delete, and that suite's teardown hit RESTRICT
— five failures in a suite that had nothing to do with the change. The harness
now creates its **own** branch and group and removes both: a harness must own
every row it depends on, or it is a fixture for somebody else's test.

### A harness that pointed at a withdrawn control

`verify-unsaved-guard` drove `＋تسجيل مستفيدة` as its reference form. The Owner
removed that button, so the reference is now الشركاء — a plain pristine
`FormDialog` behind a header Add, which is what the check was ever about.

## The name parts, the silent حفظ, and a leak the tests could not see (2026-08-28)

### تعديل بيانات المستخدم opened empty and saved nothing

Two defects with **one** cause. Every account created before Revisions 40–41 —
and everything the seeds and most fixtures still write — carries only the
composed `name_arabic`; `first_name_arabic` and `last_name_arabic` are NULL.
The edit dialog requires both, so it opened with two required fields blank, and
`حفظ` then failed validation and **returned without a word**. The button looked
broken because, from the reader's side, it was.

- **`splitComposedName` derives the parts at read time** — `userDto` and the
  directory projection — splitting at the **first** space and returning `null`,
  never `''`, for what it cannot determine.
- **It never writes.** Revision 40 refused a backfill because a guessed part
  would become indistinguishable from a typed one; deriving for *display* keeps
  that refusal intact, because the guess is offered to somebody who knows the
  person's name and can correct it before it becomes a fact. An HTTP test
  asserts the stored row is **still NULL** after the read that derived from it.
- **The form now refuses out loud** (rule AH). A validation failure that returns
  silently is indistinguishable from a dead button.

Recorded in `docs/development/person-identity.md`, which is where the concept
already lived.

### الاسم الشخصي and الاسم العائلي are two columns, on all three tables

المستخدمون · المؤطِّرات · المستفيدات each showed one combined name. They now show
the two fields the platform actually stores, sorting still carried by the
composed name — §14.2's tables name the halves separately, and the parts are
*in* the composed name, so the directory projection discloses nothing new by
publishing them (its exact-key assertion was restated rather than relaxed).

### تسجيل — the branch was asked for and already known

The `المقر` dropdown is withdrawn. An Administrative Group carries its branch
(§4.4c), and a Level-only placement (R66) falls back to the مستفيدة's own role
assignment — so the value is **derived, not mirrored**, and the page-level
branch state and the `groupId → setBranchId` effect are gone with it. Branch
stays managed from تعديل بيانات المستخدم, as the Owner asked.

الحلقة selection was **already implemented** and became reachable once the level
gate above it was fixed; verified in a browser rather than assumed.

### A form born dirty, by a third mechanism

`＋تسجيل مستفيدة` asked to discard on an untouched close because its pristine
baseline compared `studentId` — a value the **row action sets when it opens**,
not a field anybody can change. New rule **AY.1b**: *a `dirty` baseline
enumerates what the reader can modify, not what the component holds.* Pinned in
`verify-unsaved-guard.mjs`, now 23/23, beside الجدولة and إضافة شريك — three
different mechanisms producing one sentence for the reader.

### A tag in a mutable column is not a handle

Found while verifying the above, in a file this batch never touched. Two tests
in `administrative-group.http` reproduce R111 by rewriting `nameArabic` to
«حساب محذوف» — after which the teardown's `startsWith(TAG)` matches nothing.
The suite passed 26/26 while **leaking two users per run**; only the all-table
snapshot guard could see it (`user 25 → 27`). Fixtures now record ids and the
teardown deletes the union, the shape `user-management.http` already used
against exactly this feature. Generalised in `testing.md`: **a fixture's handle
on its rows must live in a column no test writes.**

**Residue not cleaned:** 26 soft-deleted `حساب محذوف` rows from runs before the
fix remain in the local development database. All carry zero roles and zero
enrolments and are excluded from every read; one is held by an enrolment under
RESTRICT. Deleting them would mean unwinding audit and trash trails for no gain,
so they are reported rather than removed.

## حفظ that did nothing, and a refusal that arrived too late (2026-08-29)

### تسجيل مستفيدة — no request at all

Not a failing save: **no request left the browser**. The dialog opens from a
مستفيدة's row, so she is already chosen — and it then went looking for her
again, in a `beneficiaries_only` directory search of its own, to read her
branch. The page builds its rows from the **union** of that durable fact and the
Student role (R79.7 exists because role membership does not identify a
beneficiary), so a person on the page can be missing from the dialog's narrower
list. She was; the search returned zero rows; the branch resolved to `''`; and
that both disabled حفظ and made `submit` return on its first line.

- **The dialog now receives the row, not an id it must re-resolve** — new rule
  **BC**. Two lists holding two definitions of the same word is how they come to
  disagree, and the disagreement surfaced as a dead control rather than an error.
- **The branch is derived from what is already known:** the group's branch
  (§4.4c) → her role assignment → a branch she is already enrolled at (R66).
- **If none of them answers, the form says so.** حفظ is gated on the Level alone
  — the one required answer the reader can actually give.
- **The service's own reasons reach her in Arabic.** `GENDER_RESTRICTION` and
  `ALREADY_ENROLLED_IN_LEVEL` were arriving as a bare «تعذّر الحفظ»; an unmapped
  refusal now carries the server's code so it can be quoted.

Verified in a browser on the Owner's exact case — a Level plus **حفظ القرآن — 1**
— as two `201`s and a closed dialog.

### المؤطّرات وفتراتهن — the invariant was right, the moment was wrong

A class beginning 30 غشت 2026 with an assignment of 29 غشت → 29 غشت is
`STAFF_PERIOD_OUTSIDE_SCHEDULE`. **The invariant is untouched.** What changed is
that it is now said at entry: the pickers carry the schedule's bounds as native
`min`/`max`, and both date fields show the reason and go `aria-invalid` as it is
typed.

**The half that is easy to miss** — new rule **BB** — is that the *measure* can
move without the measured thing being touched: editing the class's start date
invalidates a staffing row nobody looked at. The marking is therefore derived on
every render from props, never from an effect or cached state, and the component
holds no `useState` and no `useEffect` at all. A browser harness drives exactly
that: type an invalid period, move the schedule, watch the untouched row clear.

`lib/staffing-period.ts` mirrors `withinScheduleLife` on the client. Admissible
only because it can never be permissive — the server refuses independently — and
pinned against the same boundaries, **including the anchor day, which the
backend policy had not covered on the lower side.**

### The two red errors in `production.ts`

`description does not exist in CategoryCreateInput` and `prisma.partner does not
exist` were **the editor's, not the code's**. Established rather than assumed:
the schema validates, the live database has `partner` and `category.description`,
and `npm run typecheck`, `lint` and `build` are all green — including
`prisma/seed`, which is inside the typecheck surface.

The client generates into `backend/src/generated/prisma`, which is **gitignored**
— a per-machine build artifact — and there was **no `postinstall` hook**. So a
fresh clone had no client at all, and a machine that pulled a schema change kept
serving the old shape to its language server. Added
`"postinstall": "prisma generate"` and proved it: deleting the generated
directory and running `npm install` alone restores it. Recorded in
`getting-started.md` beside the existing staleness entry, with the rule that
**when the CLI and the editor disagree, the CLI is right.**

### A harness that reported 4/4 while leaking

The new enrolment harness seeds its own branch, مستفيدة and role — and its
teardown named `teaching_group_member`, a table that does not exist. `|| true`
swallowed the error, so it passed while leaving its مستفيدة behind under
RESTRICT. Corrected to `student_teaching_group`, and its trap is now armed
**before** the first row is created: a seed step failing under `set -e` had
already exited once with rows it had made. Proven by counting rows either side —
`users 29→29`, `enrolments 5→5`.

## Name sorting, empty tables, and who an administrator may record for (2026-08-30)

### Both name parts sort, on all three tables

المستخدمون · المؤطِّرات · المستفيدات order by الاسم الشخصي or الاسم العائلي
independently. The trap: ordering by the stored parts would collect every
pre-Revision-40 row under NULL, because those revisions **refused a backfill**
and the displayed value comes from `splitComposedName` on read. That is an order
by *whether anybody has edited this person*.

Two `GENERATED ALWAYS … STORED` columns carry the same derivation in SQL,
indexed `ar-x-icu`. Nothing can write them, they are never projected into a DTO,
and naming one in `sort_by` is refused like any other non-contract field — all
three asserted. المستفيدات sorts client-side through the shared `sort-rows`
helper, which is R76.2's stated exception: its rows are assembled from three
reads and no endpoint could order them.

### An empty table keeps its columns — new rule BD

`DataTable` rendered the empty state *instead of* the table, losing the headers
(the page's only description of its data when there are no rows), the sort
controls, and the difference between «nothing here» and «nothing matches». The
message now spans the body in a `colSpan` cell, which is also what makes it
announce as part of its table. Loading and error are unchanged deliberately:
those are facts about the request, not about the data. **Fixed once on the
shared component** — twenty-odd pages inherit it.

### إدخال الحفظ offers every مستفيدة, not only the placed ones

The roster required `is_beneficiary` **and** an existing enrolment, so the one
person who most needs finding on that screen — the مستفيدة whose record is empty
— was invisible with nothing saying why. The population is now the same union
التسجيلات uses (Student role ∪ R79.7's durable fact), which is **not** «every
account»: parents, مؤطِّرات and administrators stay out, as the §C25 correction
established.

**The domain invariant is untouched.** `assertLevelCurriculum` still refuses
`LEVEL_NOT_ENROLLED` — progress is recorded against a Level's curriculum — and
the test asserts the widened list and the preserved refusal *together*, because
widening without keeping the refusal is precisely the bypass the Owner ruled
out. A branch-scoped Admin's reach is unchanged; it simply can no longer be read
from an enrolment alone, so her role assignment answers it for the unplaced.

The screen's «no curriculum» note now names both causes it can have and the page
that fixes each.

### نقاط الامتحانات — the population was already right; the silence was not

Investigated rather than assumed. The sheet's rows are the exam's audience —
students enrolled in its Level at its Branch — which **is** «where the exam
context permits», with no narrowing beyond it, so nothing was widened.

What was actually wrong: the only exam in the database is a pre-R58 sitting
carrying no branch and no subject. The service refuses it deliberately
(`EXAM_INCOMPLETE`; resolving Level-wide would put other branches' students on
the sheet) — and **that reason was mapped nowhere on the client**, so it arrived
as «تعذّر التحميل». An empty screen with no student and no explanation. It now
says what is wrong with the exam and which screen fixes it.

### Two fixture leaks the snapshot caught

`quran-entry` never deleted `user_branch_role`, which RESTRICTs the user delete;
it had never granted a role until the Owner's «all students» rule made a
Student-role account part of the population under test. Caught as `user 30 → 32`
