  **R56 is not superseded** (its stated condition for reversal was met).
  **Clause (9) records the Owner's later amendment of 2026-08-28** — محاضرة → `class`, عطلة →
  the new fourth `structural_kind` `holiday`, and نشاط added as the generic activity — which
  is migrated and live. That is a supersession that already existed; recording it is
  reconciliation, not reinterpretation.

Both proposals now carry **APPLIED** status lines and point at §0 as authoritative;
`docs/README.md`'s two rows follow. The placeholder note added yesterday saying 109 and 110
were unratified is removed.

### Partner description

§5.7 and the Revision-113 entry both said a partner carries **no description**. The Owner
added `Partner.description` on 2026-08-28 and confirmed it on 2026-08-30, so both places now
record a nullable `VARCHAR(500)` description, with the amendment marked and scoped: **logo,
URL and contact remain absent and are not authorised by it.**

### Verified against the implementation

R109: all four scheduling entities carry `visibility Visibility @default(public)`
(`RecurringCourseSchedule`, `Session`, `Event`, `Exam`), and `EducationalContent` keeps
`private` — §4.9 is a different concept and R109 does not touch it. R110: the entity carries
all five fields plus TD-5 soft delete, `Event.schedulingTypeId` is `onDelete: Restrict`, and
writes assert Super Admin.

### CONTRADICTION FOUND — reported, NOT fixed

**A fresh Production install would seed a scheduling-type catalogue of ONE row.**

Measured, not inferred, on a throwaway database: after `prisma migrate deploy`,
`scheduling_type` contains exactly **`نشاط`** — migration `20260828190100_holiday_catalogue`
inserts it unconditionally, and its two corrective `UPDATE`s match nothing on an empty table.
The production seed then reaches `initializedByPresence('scheduling_types', count)`, which
returns true for any `count > 0`, logs *«already initialized — the database is
authoritative»* and **skips all five rows**. Confirmed end to end: the catalogue finishes
with `count = 1`.

- **Existing databases are correct** (six rows, right kinds): the migration corrected them in
  place, which is what it was written for.
- **A fresh install is not.** It would lack حصة دراسية, اختبار, محاضرة, حفل and عطلة, so
  `الجدولة` would offer one type and no class could be scheduled.
- This now contradicts a **ratified** requirement: Revision 110(2) states the initial
  catalogue, and 110(1) that the seed *"establishes the initial state"*.
- Also noted while measuring: `SCHEDULING_TYPES` in `production.ts` still carries the
  **pre-amendment** kinds (محاضرة `activity`, عطلة `activity`) and no نشاط row, so even were
  the guard to let it run it would seed the superseded shape.

**Not changed**, per the Owner's instruction to report rather than silently alter behaviour.
Two candidate fixes for the Owner: make the seed reconcile by name rather than short-circuit
on presence, or move the نشاط insert out of the migration into the seed list. Separately,
`scripts/seed/verify-production-seed.sh` fails two **pre-existing** R107/R108 Subject
preflight assertions unrelated to this work, and its drill asserts nothing about the
scheduling catalogue — which is why this went unseen.

## The fresh-install scheduling-type catalogue, fixed (2026-08-30)

### What was wrong

A fresh Production install finished with **one** scheduling type. Migration
`20260828190100_holiday_catalogue` inserts **نشاط** unconditionally, and its two corrective
`UPDATE`s match nothing on an empty table — so the seed arrived at a one-row catalogue, and
`initializedByPresence` read that one row as *the catalogue is there*, marked it initialized
and skipped the other five. **No `class` row existed, so no class could be scheduled**, and
nothing anywhere reported a problem because no write path was ever reached.

### The rule that replaced it

**The marker says «initialized», never the row count.** A pre-marker installation is adopted
only when **every canonical name is already accounted for** — completeness, not presence,
which is what the old check was actually reaching for. When the catalogue is incomplete the
seed reconciles **by name**:

- creates a canonical type that has **no row at all**;
- **counts a soft-deleted row as present**, so a type an administrator retired is not
  resurrected on the next deploy — *seeded does not mean immutable*, read in the direction
  that costs something;
- **never renames, re-flags or reorders an existing row**: `attendance_required` and
  `display_order` are the Owner's once the row exists, which is the point of their being
  columns;
- **preserves «database authoritative after initialization»** (R110.1) exactly — once the
  marker is written, later runs return immediately.

The migration is untouched, as instructed.

`SCHEDULING_TYPES` now carries the ratified catalogue: **محاضرة `class`**, **عطلة
`holiday`**, **نشاط** added. Attendance flags and display orders are R110(2)'s verbatim and
نشاط's are the migration's own — **nothing is inferred**. The seed's postcondition now
requires all **four** structural kinds live, for the same reason it already required three.

### Coverage

Four states, in `production-seed.integration.test.ts`: a completely fresh database · the
partial state where only نشاط exists (the defect itself) · an already-initialized database
whose canonical row was re-flagged and reordered, which must survive untouched · a
soft-deleted canonical type, which must stay deleted.

`verify-production-seed.sh` now runs the **real seed entry point on the true
migrate-then-seed sequence** and asserts the catalogue **in SQL, before vitest** — so the
defect cannot hide behind an unrelated failure later in the drill.

### One consequence, reported

On a fresh install **نشاط keeps `display_order = 1`**, the value the migration gave it, and
so shares that position with حصة دراسية; BR-19 tie-breaks by name. The seed does not correct
it because that would overwrite an existing row's editable metadata, which the instruction
forbids. Reordering is a supported administrative action.

## R107/R108 preflight failures — a REAL defect, reported not fixed

The two long-standing failures in the seed drill are **not stale assertions**. They encode
SRS Revision 107(3): *"A conflicting live marker or duplicate live حفظ القرآن rows makes the
seed fail loudly."*

**Measured, not inferred.** On a disposable database seeded once (marker
`seed.initialized.subjects` present), inserting a second live حفظ القرآن and re-running the
seed prints `subjects: already initialized — the database is authoritative` and **exits 0**.
The ambiguity preflight sits *inside* the transaction that runs only after the
`initializedByPresence` guard, so on any already-initialized installation it never executes.

- **The tests are right; the implementation contradicts a ratified requirement.**
- The fix is one move — run the preflight before the initialization guard — but it changes
  **Production deploy behaviour**: a database in an ambiguous Subject state would begin
  failing every deploy, which is precisely what R107(3) intends and is not a change to make
  unasked.
- **Left untouched, and the assertions were not weakened**, per the instruction to report
  rather than weaken. The drill therefore still exits non-zero for these two, while the new
  SQL catalogue assertion runs and passes before them.

## Staging deployment — NOT performed

`docker-compose.staging.yml` is present and **validated** (`docker compose … --profile
production config` succeeds), so the commit is staging-ready. **The deployment itself was not
performed**: this repository and machine hold **no Staging host, no credentials and no deploy
script** — `scripts/deploy/` contains only `enable-tls.sh`, CI builds but does not publish or
deploy, and no Staging target exists in `.env.example` or the workflow. R104 also requires
Staging to be production-shaped with its own TLS and synthetic data, which a local bring-up
would not be. Running the overlay on this machine would collide with the development stack's
ports and volumes and would not be a Staging deploy in any meaningful sense, so it was not
attempted.

## R107(3)'s loud failure restored — the marker is no longer a licence to stop looking (2026-08-30)

### The defect

SRS Revision 107(3): *"A conflicting live marker or duplicate live حفظ القرآن rows makes the
seed fail loudly."* The ambiguity preflight sat **inside** the transaction that
`initializedByPresence('subjects', …)` returns before ever reaching — so on any
already-initialized installation, which is every real one after its first deploy, a second
live حفظ القرآن row or a marker owned by another Subject produced
`subjects: already initialized` and **exit 0**.

### The fix

The check moved **ahead of the initialization guard**, where *"fail loudly"* stops being
conditional on how many times the platform has been deployed. It is read-only and **decides
nothing** — it does not delete a duplicate, merge two rows, rename either or choose between
them, because guessing among Owner-managed rows is exactly what R107 refuses. It reports the
ambiguity by name and stops.

Extracted as `assertSubjectsUnambiguous(db)` and called from **both** places: before the
guard, so the refusal is unconditional, and inside the transaction, so the write path keeps
its own guarantee rather than inheriting one from a read taken earlier outside any
transaction. One statement of the rule, two call sites — a second copy is a second copy that
can drift.

### Proven on disposable databases, not argued

- **Initialized + duplicate** — marker `seed.initialized.subjects` present, a second live
  حفظ القرآن inserted: the seed **exits 1** with *«requires exactly one live حفظ القرآن
  Subject; found 2»*, and the Subject rows are exactly as they were. Nothing merged, nothing
  removed.
- **Initialized + healthy** — the same database with the duplicate removed: **exits 0**, and
  `count` and `max(updated_at)` are byte-identical before and after. Running a read-only
  check on every invocation does not disturb *«database authoritative after initialization»*.

Both are now regression tests: the healthy case asserts a full snapshot equality, and the
duplicate case first asserts the marker is present, so it can never again pass by accident on
an uninitialized database.

### A test that had to start cleaning up after itself

The conflicting-marker test left the database ambiguous for its neighbours and relied on
`afterAll`. That was survivable only while the preflight was unreachable; now that the seed
refuses ambiguity on **every** invocation, it made every later test in the file fail on a
state this one introduced. It restores its own rows in a `finally` (P1.2) — **the assertions
are unchanged and nothing was weakened**.

### Unchanged, deliberately

The scheduling-type reconciliation from `1631c80`/`4dd739a` is untouched — verified by diff.
**نشاط keeps `display_order = 1`**: it is existing editable catalogue data, and no
authoritative requirement gives it another position, so this task does not reorder it.

The whole Production seed drill now exits **0** (12/12 in the seed file, then 318 and 175 in
the downstream suites), where it had been red on these two assertions.

## Manual-UAT batch — 2026-09-02

Seven findings from Localhost UAT against `d57bedb5`. The lifecycle architecture and its
R59 retention rules are unchanged; so are R115/R116/R117.

### 1 · Trash purge refusals — correct, but unactionable

The three reported failures are **not** FK defects and none was bypassed:

- **Level** — blocked by `LevelSubject` rows that are themselves soft-deleted but whose
  ownership the parent's snapshot does not record. These entries predate the exact-id
  snapshots, so purge deliberately deletes no guessed child and lets PostgreSQL refuse. That
  is the audit's `INCOMPLETE_SNAPSHOT` behaviour working.
- **Category** — blocked by a `Level` that is itself in Trash. Ordered purge: the Level goes
  first. It cannot, for the reason above, so the Category is transitively stuck.
- **AdministrativeGroup** — blocked by a soft-deleted `RecurringCourseSchedule` **that never
  materialized a Session**. This is **outstanding Owner decision 1** in
  `audit-2026-09-01-trash-lifecycle.md` and is NOT decided here.

What was genuinely wrong is that the refusal said only *«something still references this
record»*. The server now translates the constraint PostgreSQL actually returned into a domain
blocker and the screen names it in Arabic with the next action. This is a translation of an
answer already given — not the predictive blocker list the module rules out — and an unmapped
constraint keeps the old sentence, so it can become less helpful but never wrong. The raw
constraint stays in the envelope and never reaches a screen (rule M).

### 2 · الجدول الزمني filters

`/calendar`'s parameter builder set three filters and **dropped `type` and `subject_id`**,
which the page computed and the server accepts. The page never read `subjectId` at all, so it
was missing from the effect's dependencies too. `/me/calendar`'s builder had the mirror fault
— four parameters set twice. Two hand-maintained copies of one list caused both; there is now
one builder.

### 3 · مكتبة المحتوى editing

New `PATCH /content/{id}` for title, Level, Subject and visibility. `.strict()`, so a request
carrying a filename or key is refused rather than appearing to have replaced an object.
**B-02 shapes the visibility half**: `private` and `hidden` share the private bucket and swap
a column, while any change crossing `public` copies → verifies → commits → deletes the source.
Skipping the move would leave a row claiming to be private with its bytes still anonymously
reachable. Consent still wins (`CONSENT_FORCED_PRIVATE`). No notification, per R116(9).

### 4 · Level description

The edit form collected `الوصف`, the adapter carried it, the API accepted it — the page's
`save()` named only `name` and `gender_restriction`. Every edit sent a payload without the
field and reported success over a server that changed nothing.

### 5 · Monday first

Already correct: one constant, one computation, one grid consumer, and `weekdaysShort`
beginning with الاثنين. Pinned by regression, including the Sunday-start month that reads 0
under a Sunday-first grid and 6 under this one. **Native `<input type="date">` popups follow
the browser's locale and cannot be configured from the page** — see `DateField`'s own
docstring on why the native control is kept.

### 6 · Event dates — NOT REPRODUCIBLE

Traced end to end and measured at every layer: form → payload → `PATCH /events/{id}` 200 →
database row → `expandEvent` projection → `/calendar` → the rendered الجدول الزمني page. A
weekly activity edited to 14 Sept / 9 Nov produced exactly nine occurrences and the page
showed them; a `none` activity moved to 7 Sept showed on 7 Sept. Nothing was changed, because
nothing was found wrong. The probe did show that a **«إشعار المعنيين» dialog opens after a
successful save** (R82/R83's optional announcement), which is the most plausible thing to have
read as *«it saved but nothing happened»*.

### 7 · Recurrence default

`مرة واحدة` is now the creation default — **for the kinds that can be it**. `allowsOnce` is
false for a class because *the database refuses that recurrence on a schedule*, so defaulting
blindly put the form state out of step with its own control. The default is also re-derived
when the kind changes, since the kind is chosen after the form opens; that also closes a
latent fault in the other direction, where switching activity → class left a value the server
would reject.

## Lifecycle closure and test isolation — 2026-09-02

### Three suites stopped requiring a pristine development database

All three asserted global emptiness of a shared table and failed on legitimate data — 12
published Hijri months for 1448, and a Platform Owner who has signed in. None was testing
that.

- **Platform Owner** — `identities).toEqual([])` stops being a statement about bootstrap the
  first time anybody logs in. The property is that bootstrap **fabricates no Google binding**,
  so an identity created in the bootstrap transaction would carry the account's own creation
  instant while one bound by a real sign-in is strictly later. That is now asserted.
- **Hijri empty coverage** — `coverage` answers *how far does the official calendar reach* and
  has no per-year form, so the suite made the empty case by requiring an empty table. It now
  owns the visible set for one **deliberately rolled-back transaction**; the empty branch is
  exercised for real and nothing is written.
- **Hijri draft coverage** — asserted as *not this suite's draft* rather than globally null.

No legitimate data deleted, no assertion weakened, no production behaviour changed.

### Owner lifecycle decisions (SRS Revision 118)

**A · Schedule purge is a fact about the row.** Never materialized a Session → purgeable; one
Session, live or tombstoned → permanent, `MATERIALIZED_HISTORY`. Asserted twice: in the list,
so no interface offers an impossible action, and inside the purge transaction, where it is
authoritative because a materialization job may have run between the two. Staffing rows are
owned and go with it; Sessions are never in the plan, since their existence is what forbids it.

**B · Retained history is a lens, not a move.** `view=actionable|retained|all`, defaulting to
actionable. **No row is moved, hidden, altered or deleted.** Reuses the existing Trash rather
than adding an archive subsystem.

**C · A terminal rejected FamilyLink may be removed.** The operational row goes; the audit is
the evidence, written before the delete in the same transaction. `pending` and `approved` are
refused by name — one still owes an answer, the other is authority and is revoked, not
removed.

### Localhost Trash after the decisions

**49 rows: 48 actionable, 1 retained.** The retained row is a schedule that materialized
Sessions. Fourteen schedules became purgeable, which is what releases the
AdministrativeGroup blocker through ordered purge — proven in the suite, **not performed on
live records**: no bulk cleanup was authorised and provenance of individual UAT rows is not
established here.

### Not changed

The Level and Category entries still refuse on legacy `LevelSubject` tombstones whose
ownership no snapshot records — deliberate no-guess behaviour from `d57bedb5`, unchanged. The
R59.4 quarantine-destruction question remains open.

## Content edit refusal and a Localhost fixture leak — 2026-09-02

### CONSENT_FORCED_PRIVATE was the invariant working, not a metadata bug

Traced end to end against the deployed `PATCH /content/{id}`, measured at every layer.
**Metadata-only edits on consent-forced-private content already succeed** — title, Level and
Subject each return 204, proven by API and through the real dialog. The guard fires on one
condition only: a payload carrying `visibility: "public"` for an item whose consent flag is
set and whose stored visibility is not public.

The frontend sends `visibility` **only when it differs** from the row's, so the field reaches
the server only when somebody actually chose a new tier. The refusal was therefore correct:
the reader selected «عام» on an item consent has locked private.

**The real defect is that the dialog offered that choice at all**, and then reported the
refusal as a bare «تعذّر الحفظ.». The page reads the **anonymous** `/library` endpoint, which
does not publish `consent_forced_private` — safeguarding-adjacent metadata that must not
become public to satisfy a back-office convenience (§16.2). So the client cannot know in
advance.

Fixed where it can be: the moment the server says consent keeps the item private, the
visibility control is replaced by the effective value **as a fact with the rule beside it**
(rule AF, not a disabled select that implies it may one day be chosen), the reader's other
edits stay in the form, and every coded refusal now has its own Arabic sentence —
`OBJECT_MISSING`, `STORAGE_MOVE_FAILED`, `CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION`, `UNKNOWN_LEVEL`,
`UNKNOWN_SUBJECT` all previously collapsed into «تعذّر الحفظ.».

**No privacy policy was invented and no domain rule changed.** Refusing the whole request
rather than applying the metadata and silently dropping the visibility is the platform's
established convention — a field the server will not honour is refused, never ignored, so a
response never appears to confirm what did not happen.

### The `[content-test:*]` selectors — a Localhost-only leak, with its cause

**Staging is clean: zero `content-test` rows.** Localhost holds **12**: four Categories, four
Levels and four Subjects, one family per crashed suite run, each Level still holding one
content row.

The mechanism, established rather than assumed: this suite's own edit test renamed a content
row to a title **without** the TAG, so teardown's `title startsWith TAG` missed it; the
orphan then blocked the Level delete on `educational_content_level_id_fkey`; `clear()` threw
at that line, and the Subject and Category deletions after it never ran.

**A tag in a mutable column is not a handle** — the lesson `testing.md` already records, hit
again in the suite that renames. Teardown now collects content by the **union of the tag and
the ids it recorded at creation**, so a rename cannot strand a row. Proven: a fresh run adds
nothing (4 families before, 4 after, zero leftover content rows).

The 12 existing rows are **reported, not deleted**: no authorization to remove them was
given, and none is required for the fix.

## «هذا تسجيل حصة» became editable — 2026-09-02

`PATCH /content/{id}` gains `origin`, R99.12's existing marker and the same field the upload
form already sets — no second boolean for a concept that had one. The edit dialog offers it
through `CheckboxField` with the upload form's own label and hint, so one concept keeps one
control vocabulary, and it is sent only when it actually changed.

**A plain column with no storage meaning**: correcting the marker moves no object and
re-uploads nothing, which the regression asserts on bucket, key and size, in both directions.
Authorization, consent, optimistic locking and the `.strict()` schema are untouched — the
CONSENT_FORCED_PRIVATE behaviour from 1a56aa9 is deliberately unchanged.

`origin` also joins the library projection, because the dialog has to seed its control from
the item's current value. It states what the file IS, not consent state.

## «عطلة» became creatable, and the calendar learned the catalogue — SRS R119 Part A, 2026-09-02

R110(9) made `holiday` a structural kind of its own, and it reached the enum, the migration,
the seed, the write boundary and the label map. **The create form's option list alone still
offered three**, so a Super Admin could see a عطلة row in `أنواع الجدولة` and had no way to
create another — the complete-capability-with-no-reach shape (rule P), seventh instance.

`الجدول الزمني → النوع` offered `session | event | exam`: **the storage taxonomy**, which
cannot express the association's own vocabulary. Three catalogue rows are stored as an
`Event`, so `type=event` returned نشاط, حفل *and* عطلة together and a holiday could not be
asked for at all; «حصة دراسية» and «محاضرة» are likewise one `class`.

**Only `Event` recorded a catalogue row.** `RecurringCourseSchedule` and `Exam` now carry the
same nullable, `RESTRICT` column, set through the picker the form already rendered and
validated by the same rule — `assertTypeOfKind`, which `assertActivityType` became a wrapper
over rather than a second copy of.

`GET /calendar?scheduling_type_id=` resolves the type's `structural_kind` **first** — which
decides *which of the three sources can hold such a row at all*, so a class type never queries
the `Event` table — and then narrows within that source by id. That ordering is what lets two
types of one kind be filtered apart. `type` remains accepted, so a link saved earlier still
works. The options come from `GET /calendar/bootstrap`, which every calendar already fetches,
through one shared module: the public and personal calendars held two copies of the same
hard-coded array.

**No legacy row was backfilled, and this is the load-bearing decision.** Every schedule and
sitting created before this records no type; such a row matches no type filter and stays
visible under «الكل». A `structural_kind` fallback is the tempting bug and is wrong twice
over — it would make one untyped class answer to «حصة دراسية» *and* to «محاضرة», two
contradictory claims from a row that records neither, which is the name-matching §4.4b forbids
arrived at from the other direction. The column is editable so somebody who *knows* what a row
was can say so.

A عطلة is marked **from the row**: each occurrence carries `scheduling_type_id`,
`scheduling_type_name` and `structural_kind`, and the chip renders an outline plus the type's
own word beside the title (rule AV). Matching «عطلة» as a string would break the moment the
administration renames the row — the one thing the catalogue exists to let them do.

Migration `20260902160000_scheduling_type_on_schedule_and_exam`.

## The legal consent wording became versioned, immutable evidence — SRS R119 Part B, 2026-09-02

**The defect was an absent relationship, not a bug in a function.** The Arabic wording lived
in `frontend/src/i18n/ar.ts`; the version lived in the `legal.consent_text_version`
`SystemSetting`, a free-text box; a `ConsentRecord` stored **only that string**. Editing the
i18n text without bumping the setting made every record since silently misstate what was
agreed to; bumping the setting without editing the text made the records claim a version that
never existed. **Both drifts passed every test**, because nothing asserted a relationship that
nothing enforced — and the platform could not answer *«what exactly did this person agree
to»* from its own data at all.

`LegalConsentText` makes it a foreign key: exact Arabic wording, a unique human-readable
`version_label`, a SHA-256 digest, `draft | active | superseded`, and creation and activation
provenance. Four invariants, each enforced where it can actually hold:

* **Immutable once in force** — in the service, because *has this been used* is a question
  about other tables a CHECK cannot ask. New wording is a new version, refused with
  `CONSENT_TEXT_IMMUTABLE`. A **superseded** version is exactly as frozen as the live one.
* **Exactly one active version — in the DATABASE**, a partial unique index over
  `status = 'active'`. A service check alone is a race, and this is the invariant whose
  violation means somebody could be recorded against wording nobody put in force. Asserted by
  a direct write that bypasses every service check.
* **Nothing is destroyed.** No delete verb; `RESTRICT` on both references.
* **Fail closed, unchanged.** No active version → `503` /
  `CONSENT_TEXT_VERSION_NOT_CONFIGURED`, as before.

**Displayed X is recorded X, by construction.** `GET /registration/consent-text` (anonymous —
the notice is reached before any account exists) returns the id and the text **in one
response**, so there is no *«frontend text X, separately fetched version Y»* to race; the
submission carries that id and the server refuses a mismatch with `CONSENT_TEXT_SUPERSEDED`.
Refusing rather than accepting the stale version is deliberate: a wording taken out of force
may have been withdrawn *because it was wrong*. Filling the blank server-side with *whatever
is active* is the obvious convenience and is exactly the race the parameter closes — so an
absent id is `VALIDATION_FAILED`, asserted.

**Legacy evidence is preserved honestly and never fabricated.** Pre-R119 records keep their
version string and carry `consent_text_id = NULL`. **No `LegalConsentText` was manufactured
for a historical string**: the wording those people saw was never stored, and attaching them
to a row containing today's text would assert they read those words, which nobody can prove.
Nothing existing was altered, restamped or discarded.

`legal.consent_text_version` is retired from `WRITABLE_SETTINGS`, which is now empty — so
there are not two editable answers to *which wording is in force*. **The stored row is left in
place**: it is the only record of which label was last in force before the cutover, and being
unreadable and unwritable it cannot drift. `setting.integration.test.ts` keeps an exact-key-set
assertion that fails if it ever comes back.

`إعدادات المنصة` now manages versions (rule **BE**, new): what is in force with its exact
wording and effective date, the history read-only and visible without a click, and a create →
write → label → review → **activate** flow with activation a separate act. Immutability is
**shown before it is enforced** — each version carries how many consents name it, and a used
one says in words that new wording means a new version, rather than a greyed-out «تعديل»
(rule AF). No hash or UUID is put in front of an administrator.

**i18n split.** Only the accepted statement moved: the checkbox now renders the stored wording
verbatim, and the Law 09-08 explanation — headings, link, plain-language body, buttons — stays
in `ar.ts`, because it is interface, not the statement being accepted. The three keys that
composed the sentence around an inline button are gone: templating around legal text is how a
notice ends up saying something nobody approved.

TD-8 gains `legalconsenttext.create`, `.update` and `.activate`, carrying the **digest, never
the body** (audit rows are purged on their own schedule) and **ids, never labels** —
`assertMinimizedDetail` refuses a copied label, correctly, since the version rows are permanent
and the label is one join away.

**The test-isolation leak (B10) is fixed, and it was an ordering mistake rather than a missing
restore.** `email-ownership.integration.test.ts` called the restore as the **last** statement
of an `afterAll` that deleted five fixture rows first, so any one of them throwing skipped it
and left `email-owner-test-v1` behind. Restoration now runs **first**, with fixture teardown in
the `finally`, in that suite and every other. `test-support/legal-consent-text.ts` replaces the
setting helper: it never deletes a wording somebody else installed, and leaves its own row
`superseded` rather than deleting it when consent evidence still points at it. No legitimate
Localhost data was removed.

Development fixtures use wording that **says in Arabic that it is development text with no
legal value** — R119 made the body the thing a person actually reads, so a placeholder that
looked like a notice would be displayed as one. Production is seeded with nothing, and §2.3
keeps approving the real wording an Owner compliance task.

Migration `20260902180000_versioned_legal_consent_text`.

## The registration consent wording collapsed by default — 2026-09-02

R119 put the exact `LegalConsentText` beside the checkbox, and on the real form the notice is
long enough to dominate the page: the rest of the registration became hard to scan. It is now
behind an **inline disclosure** — the checkbox carries the consent's name, a line of help says
to read the wording before submitting, and «قراءة نص الموافقة كاملاً» reveals it in place.

**Presentation only.** The wording revealed and the version id submitted come from the same
`ActiveConsentText` the page holds; opening or closing touches no consent state, and the
schema, recording, activation and concurrency behaviour are untouched. A source guard pins the
one-source property, because a rendering test would pass equally with two independent sources —
which is the race R119 exists to close.

**Inline rather than a modal**: the applicant is deciding about this text right here, and a
dialog would take the checkbox off screen at the moment they need to compare the two. The
Law 09-08 explanation stays a modal, because it is background rather than the thing agreed to.

The region stays in the DOM and is hidden with `hidden`, so `aria-controls` names something
real in both states — **and nothing sets `display` on it** (rule AG: `[hidden]`'s only defence
is the UA's `display: none`, which an author rule of any specificity outranks). Measured in a
browser rather than asserted from CSS.

**The `<legend>` gap was real and was not a `gap`.** `.register-form__group` is `display: grid`
and a `<legend>` is not a grid item — the fieldset lays it out by its own algorithm, so the
grid's `gap` never applied to it. It now carries an explicit `margin-block-end`; measured at
32px, against 20px of incidental leading before. **Reported honestly: a literal overlap could
not be reproduced in Chrome**, so if «الموافقات» still collides with the line under it on the
Owner's browser, that is a separate rendering issue and a screenshot is needed.

New browser harness `scripts/dev/browser/verify-consent-disclosure.sh` — 19/19, including a
character-for-character comparison of the revealed wording against what
`GET /registration/consent-text` serves, and a 360px check. `verify-registration.sh` still
passes 41/41 plus 12/12 database assertions, so the submission path is unchanged.

## StudentSocialProfile removed; what a lesson recording IS — SRS R120, 2026-09-02

**The association does not collect this data, and the platform must not be able to.**
`StudentSocialProfile` held a child's **health condition**, family situation, home address,
siblings count and both parents' names and professions, behind the strictest authorization in
the system. **No product surface ever collected any of it** — not registration, not «تسجيل
طفل», not the beneficiary profile, and no parent, مؤطِّرة or administrative screen. The
frontend contained no reference to it at all.

**An unused capability is still a capability**, and that is the whole reasoning: an endpoint
nobody calls is a live ability to collect health data about a minor, and an empty column is a
declared purpose. Neither is withdrawn by not being used, so the Owner removed the feature
rather than disabling it — model, table, both routes, service, controller, authorization path,
the `socialprofile.view`/`socialprofile.update` audit actions, and the tests.

**The drop is guarded rather than trusted.** `20260902200000_drop_student_social_profile`
refuses to run against a non-empty table and reports the row count, because the DROP is
irreversible and the table was the only place that data ever lived — a human must decide the
fate of any row before the capability goes. Proven against the case it exists for on a
disposable database: **refuses with 1 synthetic row, drops when empty, idempotent on re-run.**
Verified read-only first: **Localhost 0 rows, Staging 0 rows**; Production is not deployed.

Tagged `contract-phase` (TD-6b) with the reason there is no expand or migrate step: there is
nothing to migrate, and a transitional column or renamed successor would preserve exactly the
capability being withdrawn.

**Policy recorded with the decision:** the platform does not collect categories of personal
data that are not necessary for the association's current operational purposes. This is a
technical fact about the software; **no legal conclusion about which CNDP regime applies is
stated or implied** — that assessment is the Owner's and their counsel's.

The dated compliance documents that argued for this now carry a resolution banner: the
2026-08-11 personal-data audit's §A.2 is **resolved by removal**, and the two companion
documents' proposals to *narrow* the case file are moot, since the Owner took the stronger
decision. They are retained as the reasoning trail, not as an inventory.

TD-2's two rows, TD-8's two action types and TD-3's two endpoints are **struck rather than
deleted**, so each registry records that a capability was withdrawn and not silently dropped.
Revision 28 is marked superseded in its entirety rather than rewritten.

## What a recording IS — a factual correction, same revision

A recording is **a مؤطِّرة recording herself giving or explaining a lesson**: her own voice in
the MVP, her own video post-MVP. **The platform does not record a classroom and does not
record beneficiaries**, and teacher-authored lesson material must not be described as
classroom capture or as inherently containing a student's voice or image.

**No technical safeguard prevents a microphone from capturing somebody else, and none is
claimed.** BR-2's consent gate is the answer to that possibility — it governs publication and
access, not capture — and is **unchanged by this correction**. Event and party media is a
separate scenario, intended for publication only where consent exists from identifiable people
or their parent/legal representative; **no implementation is created here.**

Internal names (`SessionRecording`, the `session-recording` queue, `recordings-staging`) are
kept deliberately: what needed correcting was the product meaning, not identifiers.

## `User.notes` removed; Google scope minimised; Category names reconciled — SRS R121, 2026-09-02

### A · Generic free text is withdrawn

`User.notes` was 2 000 characters of unbounded free text rendered as «ملاحظات (اختياري)» on the
**public registration form**. An applicant could volunteer a health condition, a custody
arrangement or a judicial matter into a platform that deliberately collects none of them; it
was also staff-editable and displayed to approvers. **No requirement anywhere stated what it
was for or who had to read it.**

R62.1 had already excluded `notes` from the child shape in exactly these words — *"free text
about a child, with no stated purpose and no reader, is where a diagnosis or a custody
arrangement gets written in good faith"* — and the Owner now extends that to every person.
Hiding the control would not have withdrawn the capability: the column would still accept
2 000 characters through the API, and an empty column is still a declared purpose.

**Migration `20260902220000_drop_user_notes` refuses to run while any row holds a non-blank
value** and reports how many, because a dropped column cannot be recovered. Blank-but-not-null
counts as no value, deliberately — refusing on whitespace would block a deployment over an
artefact rather than over somebody's words. Proven on a disposable database in three states:
**refuses with a real value; succeeds with only NULL and blanks; a fresh full history ends with
no column.** Verified read-only first: **Localhost 73 users / 0 values, Staging 14 users / 0
values.**

**The principle, recorded so this is a rule and not one deletion:** the platform does not
provide generic free-text collection fields without a defined operational purpose; a
personal-data field must have a specific, documented purpose.

**Deliberately untouched** — the bounded free-text fields that DO have one:
`ChildApplication.internal_note` (R62.8), `FamilyLink.decision_reason`,
`Session.cancellation_reason`, and the `notes` key on the §5.2 Session page projection, which
is a different field on a different entity.

### B · Google OAuth asks for `openid email`

`profile` was requested and never read: the verifier extracts `sub` and `email` and discards
the rest, so the platform was asking Google for a name, a picture and a locale in order to
throw them away. Nothing downstream moved when it went.

PKCE, `state`, the sealed flow cookie, token validation, `email_verified`, the subject binding
and the account-chooser prompt are unchanged, as is the rule that no Google token is persisted
and no Google API is called after login. **The public privacy page is corrected in the same
change** — it told applicants the platform asks for their name and picture. A policy naming a
scope the application does not request is as wrong as one omitting a scope it does, and
`legal.test.ts` asserts the page and `oauth.ts` agree.

### C · The Category names are the association's own

**`المرأة`, `اليافعات`, `الطفل` are authoritative** (Owner, 2026-09-02) and are not renamed.

**Revision 27 is not reversed.** Its purpose was to make the sex restriction machine-readable:
before it, *«teens are girls only»* lived **only in the feminine plural of the category name**,
where no query could read it. R27 moved that onto `Level.gender_restriction`, paired it with
`User.sex`, and **that half stands untouched**. The rename that travelled with it was a
consequence of the normalisation, not a product decision — and this repository then recorded
the renamed forms as the seeded reality in several places.

**No migration and no data change was created.** Both installations already hold the Owner's
names with no duplicates, and a fresh install seeds them correctly because the R27 rename is
guarded by the legacy name and finds nothing on an empty table.

**Two live defects caused by the stale assumption are fixed.** The library's `CATEGORY_ORDER`
matched the sex-neutral names, so **not one of the three matched a real row**, every category
ranked equal-last, and the adult→teen→child progression the constant exists to impose was
silently absent. And the public landing page named two stages the association does not use.

**A known upgrade hazard is recorded rather than fixed**: on an installation carrying the
legacy rows before 2026-07-28, the R27 migration renames them and the §15.1 seed — which
matches by name — would create the Owner's names as *new* rows. No such installation is known;
if one appears it is a data reconciliation and an Owner decision, not a silent seed change.

**No legal conclusion** is drawn here about these names, about `User.sex`, about Quran progress
or about the Google transfer. Those remain open compliance questions.

## An enrolment belongs to a semester — SRS R122, 2026-09-03

**The defect.** An `Enrollment` was treated as *current* for exactly one reason: nobody had
soft-deleted it. That conflated two different facts. `deleted_at IS NULL` says *a human never
ended this enrolment*; it says nothing about whether the semester it belonged to has finished.
The unique index `(student_id, level_id)` among live rows made the conflation structural — the
same student could never hold two live enrolments in one Level, so a second semester in the
same Level could only be recorded by overwriting the first. The history a future attestation
depends on («كنت أدرس عندكم وأريد شهادة تثبت المستوى الذي وصلت إليه») was being destroyed one
year at a time, and nothing in the system said so.

**The distinction the Owner named, and which the model now holds.** An *academic year* is a
calendar span the association runs. A *studies year* — the pedagogical tier a student has
reached — is a different concept, and one academic year can contain more than one of them for
the same person. The Owner's case: enrol in الفصل 2 of the first studies year; at the start of
the next academic year enrol in الفصل 1 of the **first** studies year *and* الفصل 1 of the
**second**; then in الفصل 2 of that same academic year enrol in الفصل 2 of the second studies
year. Four live rows for one student, two of them in the same academic year at different
Levels, and none of them overwriting another.

**No new entity for the studies year.** `Level` already **is** it: a Category orders its
Levels by `display_order`, and progression is moving to the next. Inventing a `StudiesYear`
would have created a second answer to *what level has she reached*, and the two would drift.
The evidence for this is in the live data, not an assumption.

**`AcademicPeriod` is new** — a semester inside an `AcademicYear`, `(year, sequence)` unique,
with a start date and an **inclusive** end date, because «الفصل ينتهي يوم كذا» means through
the end of that day. `Enrollment.academic_period_id` names one, and the live-row unique index
becomes `(student_id, level_id, academic_period_id)`. BR-21 is unchanged in substance and
narrowed in scope: one group per Level, **within a period**.

**Currency is derived, never stored.** Whether an enrolment is running is read from its
period's dates on every request. A stored status column was rejected: it would need a job to
maintain, and a job that fails leaves a row lying about itself. `deleted_at` keeps its single
meaning — *ended early by a person* — and the two states read differently on screen: an ended
enrolment is gone from the list, a finished one is present and reads **منتهٍ**.

**The migration fabricates nothing.** `academic_period_id` is nullable and **no historical row
is backfilled**: the association's past semester boundaries are not recorded anywhere, and a
guessed period would be indistinguishable a year later from one that actually ran. Existing
rows read **غير مسجَّل**, which is the honest answer. The column is required at the *write*
boundary instead, so nothing new can be created without one. The seed creates **no periods**
either, for the same reason.

**Approval fails closed.** Approving an applicant or a child application enrols into the period
covering today; with none open the request is refused with `NO_CURRENT_ACADEMIC_PERIOD` rather
than enrolling into an unrecorded semester. Opening the periods is now part of starting an
academic year.

**Overlap is refused in the service, not the database.** Two periods of one year may not cover
the same day (`ACADEMIC_PERIOD_OVERLAP`). A PostgreSQL exclusion constraint over a date range
needs the `btree_gist` extension and this deployment installs none, so the service check is the
only enforcement — recorded in [Database](architecture/database.md) so nobody assumes a
constraint is behind it.

**Authorization matches its siblings.** Period writes are Super Admin through the same
`scope.isSuperAdmin` check the rest of curriculum reference data uses. TD-12 freshness was
tried first and removed: it is scoped to approvals, consent overrides, pass/fail overrides,
user management and settings, and applying it to one reference-data write alone would have
made it behave differently from every control beside it on the same screen.

**The screen was built with the model, not after it.** `/admin/academic-periods` (الفصول
الدراسية, الإدارة, Super Admin) lists every period, filters by year *optionally*, and creates
and edits them. Shipping the required field without it would have been this project's
recurring defect in its purest form — a capability with no reach — and the consequence would
not have been cosmetic: with no period open, approval refuses every applicant. There is **no
delete**: a semester that ran is a fact, enrolments point at it under `RESTRICT`, and a
destructive action beside rows that must never take it is a trap rather than a feature.

**Two test-fixture defects were found and fixed while threading the column through.** A shared
fixture that swept its whole label band deleted a concurrently-running suite's period; and a
random label from an 800-wide band collided inside a single file within one run. Both are
recorded under [Testing](development/testing.md) — the fixes are ownership tracking and one
year per test file with a counter, so uniqueness is structural rather than probabilistic.

**Unrelated documentation drift fixed:** `identity-and-access.md` still described
`StudentSocialProfile` reads as a permission row and as a TD-12 freshness surface, three
revisions after R120 removed the table.

## Attendance — the register the association keeps on paper — SRS R123 (proposed), 2026-09-03

**The Owner commissioned this build; the SRS has not been amended.** §20 rule 16
still names attendance as a postponed feature and §4.7 still says *"nothing in
this section is built"*. That is now a code/specification conflict, and rule 20
requires it be reported rather than resolved silently: the wording is drafted in
[`SRS-PROPOSAL-R123.md`](SRS-PROPOSAL-R123.md) and **awaits Owner ratification**.
No agent edited `docs/SRS.md`.

**What attendance MEANS is a property of the type; who may MARK is a property of
the class.** `SchedulingType.attendance_required` — a boolean — collapsed two
questions the Owner states separately, and a عطلة and an optional نشاط were both
"not required" while needing completely different behaviour. It becomes
`attendance_mode`: `disabled` · `optional` · `required`. Separately,
`RecurringCourseSchedule.attendance_marking` and `Event.attendance_marking` say
`staff_only` or `self_or_staff` — حصة دراسية is the type of both a women's class
each woman signs herself into and a children's class only the مؤطِّرة may mark,
so this could never live on the type.

**A row IS the presence.** No absence table, no status column, no snapshot.
Present is a live `Attendance` row; not marked is the absence of one. Creating an
absent row per expected person per occurrence would record, thousands of times,
that nothing happened — and §4.7 makes attendance informational, gating nothing
(BR-11), which is what makes the missing row safe.

**Three occurrence carriers, no fourth abstraction.** A `Session`, an `Event`
**with its date**, or an `Exam`; exactly one, enforced by
`attendance_one_occurrence_check` — the idiom `Notification` already uses. The
date is stored because for a recurring نشاط it is **half the occurrence's
identity**: one Event row expanded over many dates, so a record keyed only by
`event_id` would make «حضرت يوم 15 يونيو» mean «حضرت كل أسبوع». Idempotency is a
partial unique index with `NULLS NOT DISTINCT` — without that, PostgreSQL treats
every NULL occurrence column as unique and the index permits unlimited
duplicates, which is exactly the double-tap on «تسجيل حضوري».

**The expected roster is a query against the OCCURRENCE's date** (§20 rule 22,
R122). `audienceWhere` and `eventAudienceWhere` gained an **explicitly required**
`on: Date | null`, so every caller states which of two questions it is asking —
*who is in this class* (period-blind: the consent gate, the calendar,
notification recipients, unchanged) or *who was expected on this day*
(attendance). A field each caller must FILL IN cannot go missing the way a
behaviour each caller must opt INTO does (rule AE).

**Enrolment decides EXPECTED, never ALLOWED.** Any live beneficiary may be
marked, including one not enrolled in that class; the sheet flags her
`beyond_roster` — the note the paper sheet takes in the margin. Finding her needs
a search, and `GET /admin/directory` is Admin-only, so a **narrower question**
was added instead of widening it: `…/attendance/candidates` answers beneficiaries
at the occurrence's own branch, excluding those already marked. Handing every
مؤطِّرة the whole people-picker to solve one sheet's problem is the widening rule
O forbids.

**A minor never self-marks, and never sees the control.** Three server
conditions: the occurrence is `self_or_staff`, the caller is the subject, and
**every Category she is enrolled in carries `self_attendance_allowed`** — a new
column, because the platform had no machine-readable way to say which Categories
are adults (§4.4b forbids matching the name; R62.7 forbids `schooling_stage`
gating anything). It holds **even when a schedule says `self_or_staff`**. The
capability is published on `GET /me` beside `teaches_quran` and for the same
reason, so the control can be hidden rather than discovered by pressing a button
that can only fail. The scheduling form withholds `self_or_staff` from the outset
where the Level's Category forbids it, and says which it is.

**Vacations and parties are refused server-side on every path** — reading,
marking, removing **and configuring** — with `ATTENDANCE_NOT_AVAILABLE`. So is an
occurrence whose type was never recorded: *we do not know* is answered by
refusing, not by guessing the permissive branch.

**A Session carrying attendance is protected from schedule regeneration** (§20
rule 24) by `HAS_ATTENDANCE` — added as a **built-in** rather than a contributed
rule. The registry's own doc-comment uses attendance as the contribution example,
and the dependency argument is real; but the file already reads `session_content`
so that direction was never pure, and the stronger property is the one the file
states two paragraphs up: *a protection that can be switched off by forgetting to
call a bootstrap function is not a protection* — and `resetContributedRules` can
switch a contributed one off inside a test.

**Authorization invents nothing.** A Session is marked by whoever staffs *that
occurrence on its date* (R91), an Event by whoever may edit it (R71), an Exam by
its supervisor or an Admin in the branch. TD-12 freshness was deliberately **not**
added: its catalogue is approvals, overrides, user management and settings, and
marking a register is ordinary operational work.

**Migration mapping, stated exactly.** `holiday → disabled` is **structural** — no
name is read. Everything else: `true → required`, `false → optional`. The seeded
**حفل** row is set `disabled` by name **once, in the migration**, guarded to a row
still of kind `activity` and still carrying that name; the same one-time
classification sets المرأة's `self_attendance_allowed`. **Nothing at runtime ever
matches a name.** Both are one click to change on أنواع الجدولة afterwards, which
is why the proposal asks the Owner to confirm rather than assume them.

**No analytics, no reports, no percentages. No QR, geolocation, biometrics,
signatures, photos, device identifiers or IP recording.** Audit detail is ids and
a date only, mechanically enforced by the audit repository's minimisation guard
and asserted directly, because a register is about minors often enough that «no
name reached the log» must be a test rather than a convention.

## The online assessment builder — SRS R124 (proposed), 2026-09-04

**Nothing here is a new domain.** `exam.mode = 'online'` has existed since R58
and was refused at the service level with `ONLINE_NOT_AVAILABLE`, because — in
that service's own words — *"`physical` carries a place and staff and no
questions; `online` will carry questions and no place"*. This builds the half
that was declared and postponed. The Owner commissioned it; **`docs/SRS.md` is
untouched** and the wording is drafted in
[`SRS-PROPOSAL-R124.md`](SRS-PROPOSAL-R124.md) awaiting ratification.

**No second assessment entity, and that was the whole decision.** `Grade` is
keyed `(exam_id, student_id)` and already carries the 20-point scale, the
draft/published split, the grade sheet, the student's results screen and the
parent visibility rule. An `Assessment` table beside it would have forked all
five, and the Owner's *«reuse the existing Grade model»* would have been
impossible to honour. What makes a quick test not *«forced awkwardly into a
formal Exam»* is the **target**, not a second model.

**Five targets, one resolver.** A Level · an Administrative Group · a **Session**
· a Teaching Group · **one named beneficiary**. R58 stored the narrower sitting
as a non-null `administrative_group_id` and read NULL as *the whole Level*; with
three more arms that inference stops being decidable, so `target_kind` is stored
and `exam_target_check` refuses any disagreement between the arm and the columns.
Every consumer — the student's list, the paper, the inbox, **and the grade sheet**
— composes one `examAudienceWhere`. That last one matters: the sheet branched on
`administrativeGroupId` being null, and left alone it would have resolved a quick
test's marks to the whole Level.

**Eligibility is derived; a submission is a fact.** Nobody is copied into an
assignment table (§20 rule 22). *Who may start now* resolves from live enrolment
against the `AcademicPeriod` covering **the assessment's own date** (R122); *who
answered* is a row nothing later touches. A beneficiary who leaves the Level
keeps her submission, keeps it on her own list, and loses only the ability to
start a new one — **a submission is its own permission to read**.

**R124 narrows only what R124 introduces.** `examAudienceWhere` takes an explicit
`on: Date | null`, and a **physical** sitting passes `null`: those have been
resolved period-blind since R58, and narrowing them would have silently dropped
every pre-R122 enrolment off a grade sheet that has always shown her.

**The two `jsonb` blobs are gone.** `exam.questions` and
`student_exam_submission.answers` could not express three things this feature
requires: **stable explicit ordering** (an array's index IS its order, so any
edit reorders by accident), **an answer that references the option it chose** (a
blob names a string, so rewording an option silently rewrites what a student
said), and **a database that refuses to delete an answered question**. Both were
snapshotted into `Trash` before being dropped — nothing was discarded, including
the one development fixture holding sample questions in an auto-scoring shape v1
deliberately does not have.

**The paper freezes on the first submission**, and that is why no versioning
scheme exists. A question whose wording, order or options changed after an answer
was given makes that answer mean something the student never said. A draft in
progress freezes nothing: she has answered nothing anybody has read.

**SAVE is not SUBMIT.** حفظ leaves a draft; إرسال is confirmed in Arabic and
final for her. **Nothing autosaves and nothing autosubmits** — an assessment that
submitted itself because a phone locked would be a mark nobody chose to hand in.
Completeness is required only on submit, or a save would be useless.

**Every response type is checked server-side against the question's own kind**,
and each mistake gets its own reason. The sharpest is `UNKNOWN_OPTION`: an option
belonging to another question would attach an answer to a choice she was never
shown.

**Two defects found while building, both fixed.** `assertExamInTeacherScope` was
handed an empty string for a branchless online assessment and PostgreSQL raised a
cast error (P2007) that would have surfaced as a 500 — it now narrows the
question honestly to *does she teach anybody in this Level*. And
`createPhysicalExam` had to learn to write `target_kind`, or every existing
`POST /exams` with a group would have failed the new CHECK.

**Grading is manual and unchanged**: the same sheet, the same 20-point scale, the
same `SystemSetting` passing basis, the same publication act. **No automatic
scoring, no analytics, no reports, no percentages.** No branching, file uploads,
images, rich text, timers, proctoring, geolocation, random pools or negative
marking.

**A student's answer never reaches `AuditLog`, `Trash`, a notification or a
log.** Question text and option labels are kept out on the same rule — *what did
the paper ask* is not a question the audit trail exists to answer — and the
property is asserted directly rather than left as a convention.

## R123 and R124 ratified into the SRS — 2026-09-04

The Document Owner ratified both revisions. `docs/SRS.md` is amended; the two
proposal documents are marked **APPLIED** and kept as the drafting record, which
is the convention R109 and R110 established.

**What moved into the authoritative text.** Revision 123 and Revision 124 are
recorded in §0 with the rest. §4.7 is **replaced in full** — it no longer says
*"nothing in this section is built in the MVP"*. §4.6's *"`online` is declared
and refused until it is planned as its own feature"* is superseded, and its
question-type and auto-grading clauses are **narrowed to what v1 actually does**:
four kinds, manual marking, no MCQ auto-scoring. §20 rule 16 no longer names
attendance or the exam builder among the postponed features — **its sentence
«a specification written ahead of its build is not permission to build it» stays**
and now attaches to session announcements, which is the case it still describes.
§10.1's Attendance System roadmap bullet is retired in place. §7 gains
`Attendance`, `ExamQuestion`, `ExamQuestionOption`, `StudentExamAnswer`,
`StudentExamAnswerOption` and `Category.self_attendance_allowed`.

**Two Owner sub-decisions are now normative rather than open.** حفل stays
`attendance_mode = disabled` and **اختبار is `required`** — and the Owner added an
invariant the proposals did not state: **exam attendance, exam submission and
exam grade are three independent facts**, with no gating between them in either
direction. Attendance with no submission, and a submission with no attendance,
are both legitimate. §4.7 states it and R123 clause (10) records it.

**R110's superseded clauses are annotated in place, not rewritten.** Clause (4)'s
`attendance_required` boolean and clause (6)'s عطلة flag carry a note saying
Revision 123 replaced the boolean with the three-state mode, and why. The
historical wording stays readable — a revision log that edits its own past is a
log nobody can trust.

**Documentation-only.** No schema, service, contract or test changed, so the
executable evidence from `b979393` — backend 320 unit / 2071 integration, frontend
1004, all CI guards — remains valid for this HEAD.

## R124 migration — semantic audit, and a mandatory production preflight — 2026-09-04

Read-only audit of `20260904090000_r124_assessment_builder`, clause by clause,
because it does two things the compatibility policy normally forbids in one file:
it **drops two `jsonb` columns** and it **writes a value no old column proves**.

**`target_kind` is derived, not invented.** R58 stored the narrower sitting as a
non-null `administrative_group_id` and read `NULL` as *the whole Level* — its own
schema comment says so in those words. `NULL → level`,
`NOT NULL → administrative_group` re-encodes that inference exactly. The
inference had to become explicit because with three more target arms, *which
target is this* stopped being decidable from which columns happen to be null.

**`status = 'published'` is a choice, and the audit establishes it is inert.**
`is_published` existed from the first migration and **no application code ever
wrote or read it**. What makes the choice safe rather than merely convenient is
that **every reader of `exam.status` is scoped `mode = 'online'`** — they are all
in `assessment.service.ts` — so a physical sitting's status is consulted by
nothing. `published` is the conservative direction if that ever changes: a future
unscoped reader would show an arranged sitting rather than hide one.
`createPhysicalExam` writes the same value, so legacy and new rows agree.

**Trash preservation is a safety net, not a plan.** A non-empty blob is
snapshotted before the drop, but the snapshot carries the ordinary 90-day
`purge_after` like every other. Depending on it would be depending on a window.

**Therefore a mandatory production preflight, recorded in three places** — the
deployment runbook (the SQL), the database architecture page (the reasoning) and
the deployment-readiness blocker register (the gate). Three counts, all expected
`0`: a non-empty question blob, any `student_exam_submission` row, any
`mode = 'online'` exam. If any is non-zero the migration **does not run**: those
rows are evidence of something the application did not do — no submission
endpoint ever existed, and `online` was refused from R58 until R124 — and what to
keep is an Owner decision, not an operator's.

**No code was changed.** The migration is not rewritten: it was found safe under
a precondition, and the precondition is now enforced by procedure rather than
assumed.

## R122/R123/R124 cross-feature audit, and the one defect it found — 2026-09-04

**One demonstrable defect, fixed.** R123 resolved an exam's attendance roster
with its own two-arm rule — *named group, else the whole Level* — which was R58's
entire targeting model and correct when it was written. R124 then gave `Exam`
three further arms and a canonical `examAudienceWhere`, and the second answer
survived: an exam targeting **one beneficiary** would have opened its attendance
sheet on **everybody in the Level**.

**No route produces such a row today** — `/assessments` records no scheduling
type, so an online assessment has no sheet at all, and `/exams` writes only the
two old arms. It is fixed anyway, because *unreachable* is not *impossible* and a
second answer to *who sits this exam* is the defect whether or not something
currently reaches it (§4.4c). Reproduced first with a failing test that builds
the row directly, then fixed by calling the canonical resolver.

**Everything else the audit checked held.**

*Audience logic* — every call site in the codebase (`online-class`,
`consent-reevaluation`, `grade`, `attendance`, `assessment`, `session`,
`notification`, `quran`) composes `roster-resolution.ts`. After this fix there is
**no second implementation of any arm** anywhere.

*R122* — eligibility resolves against the period covering the **occurrence or
assessment date**, never today's; a `NULL` `academic_period_id` matches no period
filter, so unclassified history never becomes current; `deleted_at` keeps its one
meaning and natural expiry is not a deletion; a historical enrolment stays
readable.

*R123* — the expected roster uses the occurrence date; a recurring `Event`'s date
is half the attendance key, so two dates are two sheets; marking somebody beyond
the roster writes an `Attendance` row and touches no `Enrollment`; attendance
writes no `Grade`; self check-in is refused for a Category without
`self_attendance_allowed` even when an occurrence says `self_or_staff`.

*R124* — a Session target resolves through the class's own audience, so it cannot
leak to unrelated students; a Level target carries the period rule; a
TeachingGroup target requires seat membership *and* a live enrolment in that
Level for the period; an individual target is exactly one id; a submission
survives later enrolment changes and remains its own permission to read; the
first submission freezes the paper; grade publication is `Grade.status` and is
untouched by assessment publication.

*Exam attendance ≠ submission ≠ grade* — verified as three independent writes
with no read of one gating another, which is now normative in §4.7.

*Physical exams* — unchanged by R124 except where the ratified revision says so:
`status` is inert for them, and the grade sheet still resolves them period-blind.

## The online assessment's grading path is now proved, not argued — 2026-09-04

R124's tests wrote `prisma.grade.create` directly, which proves the *visibility*
rule and nothing about whether an online assessment can actually be graded. That
mattered because R124 **widened `loadForGrading`**: an online paper has neither a
branch nor a Subject, and the pre-R58 guard that refuses both had to be narrowed
to physical sittings. A path changed that way deserves a test rather than an
argument.

Three assertions through the real service: the sheet opens on the assessment's
own audience with its own maximum (the Level's students, resolved through the one
exam-audience rule — not the branch-bound arm an online paper has no branch for);
a draft mark stays invisible and publishing reveals it, so **publishing the
assessment is still not publishing the grade**; and grading writes **no attendance
row** and does not move the submission — the three facts stay independent (§4.7).

**It found a real teardown gap immediately.** `publishGrades` notifies (R116), and
`notification.exam_id` is `RESTRICT`, so the suite could not delete its own exams.
The hand-written `grade.create` never sent a notification, which is exactly the
kind of thing a test that skips the real path cannot see.

