## An online assessment leaked into the sittings list — 2026-09-04

`GET /exams` filtered `deleted_at` and the caller's scope, and **not `mode`**, so
R124's papers appeared on الجدولة beside real sittings — for a Super Admin and an
all-branches Admin. A branch-scoped Admin never saw them, because
`branch_id IN (…)` excludes NULL and an online row has no branch; the leak
reached exactly the callers with no branch filter, which is why it was invisible
in the branch-scoped tests.

**Why it is a defect and not a cosmetic difference.** Every row on that screen is
an *arrangement* — a branch, a room, a clock window — and an online assessment
has none of them by construction (`exam_online_has_no_room_check` forbids a
branch). It rendered as a sitting somebody had forgotten to finish, and clicking
edit answered `ONLINE_NOT_AVAILABLE`: a refusal the reader did nothing to earn.

Reproduced first with a failing HTTP test that creates the row directly, then
fixed by filtering `mode: 'physical'` — which is what the route has always meant
and what the ratified §4.6 split now says in terms: this endpoint schedules a
sitting, `/assessments` writes a paper. OpenAPI updated to say so.

exam.http + exam + teacher-exam-scope + assessment integration: **70/70**.

## The independence invariant is now tested, not asserted — 2026-09-04

R123 clause (10), added by the Owner at ratification, says exam attendance, exam
submission and exam grade are **three independent facts** with no gating between
them in either direction. It was true by construction and proved by nothing.

Four assertions on a real physical sitting — the only kind with a register, and
the case the Owner named (*«اختبار: attendance REQUIRED»*):

* attendance is recorded with **no submission and no grade**;
* she is **graded without ever having submitted**, and the register does not move;
* somebody with **no attendance row at all** is graded — and BR-7's absent-zero
  row for him was initialised **from the audience, not from the register**, which
  is the invariant seen from the other side;
* **withdrawing the attendance mark leaves the published grade standing**.

The third case corrected the test rather than the code: `bob` already had BR-7's
draft row, so the stale `version: 0` was refused. The platform was right and the
test's assumption was wrong — and restating it made the assertion stronger,
because *where the absent-zero row came from* is the more interesting fact.

Two teardown gaps surfaced and were closed: `attendance.exam_id` and the suite's
own rooms and scheduling types. Both are the FKs doing their job.

## The assessment builder, verified in a browser — and it found two things — 2026-09-04

R124 shipped without a browser harness, unlike R123. `verify-assessments.sh`
closes that, and it earned its keep on the first run twice over.

**A real defect: the teaching portal was rendering the back office's sidebar.**
`/teacher/assessments` routed straight at the back-office *page*, which owns its
own `AdminLayout` — so a مؤطِّرة opening the builder got the administration's
navigation and none of her own. **No unit test could have caught it**: both
frames render perfectly well alone, and the module guards only assert that the
node exists and the router has a case.

The fix is R70.1's rule applied again, the way `/teacher/exams` already applies
it: the builder's **body** becomes `AssessmentsView`, each portal passes its own
chrome, and neither owns a frame the other has to inherit. The property is now
pinned in CI as *the teaching page imports no `AdminLayout`* — asserted as an
import rather than a word, because the comment above the component names the
defect and must stay free to.

**A harness precision bug, in the harness.** The submit check asked whether the
page still offered «إرسال الإجابات» and found the **confirmation dialog's own
confirm button**, which carries the same label. Scoped to outside any `<dialog>`.

Five checks: the builder creates and publishes through its own API; a مؤطِّرة
reaches بناء الاختبارات and gets the builder rather than a pending placeholder;
the published paper is on the beneficiary's list; حفظ saves a **draft**; and
إرسال **asks first, then locks every control and stops offering itself**.
**5/5.** Frontend 1004/1004, build green.

## A branch-scoped Admin could name any beneficiary in the association — 2026-09-04

Found by probing R124's individual target rather than by a failure. An online
paper carries no branch (`exam_online_has_no_room_check` forbids one), and
`assertMayAuthor` read that as *nothing to assert*:

```ts
if (exam.branchId !== null) { assertCanActOnBranch(...); }   // else: no check
```

So an Admin scoped to one branch could create a paper **addressed by name** to a
beneficiary at any other branch — and, because authoring brings the submissions
inbox with it, **read that student's submitted answers**. TD-2 scopes an Admin to
her branches; a paper having no room of its own does not widen that.

**Fixed narrowly and deliberately.** The named individual is checked against the
branches she is actually enrolled at, answering `NOT_FOUND` rather than `403`
(§20 rule 17 — a 403 would confirm which beneficiaries exist elsewhere). **The
other arms are not checked this way**, and that is a decision rather than an
oversight: a Level-wide online paper is cross-branch *by construction* —
`examAudienceWhere`'s `level` arm resolves the Level across branches precisely
because the paper is sat nowhere — so whether a branch-scoped Admin may author
one is a **policy question for the Owner**, recorded below and not decided here.

**The probe also found the teacher arm is stricter than expected, and that is
recorded as it is rather than as it was assumed.** A `student` target names no
group, so `assertExamInTeacherScope` asks *do you teach this whole Level* — and a
مؤطِّرة staffing one Administrative Group inside it does not. She is refused for a
student she does not teach **and for one she does**. The refusal is safe; the
second half is a **usability gap needing an Owner decision**, because widening it
means defining what *«her own»* means for a target that carries no group.

assessment + exam.http + attendance + grade.http: **127/127**.

## Two Owner decisions, implemented — SRS Revision 125, 2026-09-05

**A Level target does not override branch authorization.** A Level spans
branches, and an online paper carries none of its own, so a branch-scoped Admin
could author one that reached every student in a Level wherever they studied.
The rule is now the Owner's, **stated over the audience rather than the target's
shape**: she may create, use or publish a target only when everybody it resolves
to is inside her branches. One check for all five arms, composed from
`examAudienceWhere` — §4.4c's single definition of *who is this for* — so **no
second source of truth for branch membership** is introduced; enrolment is the
branch fact (§20 rule 22). A beneficiary enrolled at two branches, one of them
hers, is somebody she already administers and does not make a target escape.

**It is re-asked at publish**, which is the Owner's word: the audience is
resolved and never stored, so a Level entirely at her branch when she drafted may
have gained a second branch since, and publishing is the moment it reaches
people. `403` with `TARGET_OUTSIDE_BRANCH_SCOPE`, naming how many escape. **A
Super Admin is unaffected**, and no other Admin permission moved.

**A مؤطِّرة addresses THIS student, not a whole Level.** A `student` target names
no group, so the old path degenerated to *do you teach the entire Level* and
refused her for a student she teaches every week. It is now `studentsTaughtBy` —
§4.4c's canonical derivation, the same one behind Quran logging on *her own
students*, exam authoring and sensitive social data — **reused rather than
restated**. She gains no association-wide lookup: not a student outside her
teaching, not another branch's beneficiary, and not anybody merely because she
knows a UUID. Out of scope answers `404`, never `403`, or the refusal becomes a
way to discover who exists (§20 rule 17).

**The picker is built, and it is not the boundary.** `GET /assessments/targets`
answers *what may I address* per caller, staff-only, capped and searchable, with
labels composed server-side. A مؤطِّرة sees her own students and the occurrences
she staffs; an Admin sees what stays inside her branches and **is not offered a
Level whose audience escapes them** — computed as *which Levels hold an enrolment
at an unreachable branch*, one query rather than one per Level. The builder
composes the existing `SearchInput` and `SelectField`; **no second generic
picker**. Every refusal is made again on the write.

**One real bug found while testing.** `reachableBranches(scopes, ['admin'])`
returns an **empty array** for a مؤطِّرة, not `null` — she holds no admin
assignment — and an empty array used as a filter matches nobody, so every arm of
the picker silently returned an empty list for her. `null` is *every branch* and
`[]` is *none*; a caller with no branch scope of that kind must ask a different
question, and hers is `studentsTaughtBy`.

**Tests: 56/56** on the assessment suite, covering all fourteen required proofs.
Browser **6/6**, including the picker offering a مؤطِّرة her own student by name
with no UUID box anywhere in the dialog.

**Follow-up the same day — the Level list is scoped too.** R125 withholds a Level
whose audience escapes a branch-scoped Admin's branches, but the create form took
its Level from the ordinary scope selector, which lists every Level. She was
therefore still *offered* one and refused at save — a control leading to a
refusal she did nothing to earn, which is the inverse of rule P and the reason
the picker exists at all. When the target **is** the Level, the Level now comes
from the scoped list; on the other arms it stays the ordinary selector, because
there the Level is not the audience — a group at her own branch inside a Level
that spans two is perfectly legitimate, and withholding that Level would block a
real case.

## R126 — student educational evidence forbids deleting an assessment (2026-09-03)

**Owner decision, taken from the read-only preflight of the same day.**
`DELETE /exams/{id}` is a **soft** delete — an `UPDATE` — so not one of the six
`RESTRICT` foreign keys pointing at `exam` ever fired, and nine materially
different situations behaved identically: a bare draft, a published sitting
nobody sat, a saved student draft, a final submission, a draft mark, a published
mark, an attended sitting, a paper with questions, a paper with answers. All
nine were tombstoned by one click, and every reader's `deleted_at IS NULL`
filter then withdrew the paper, the marks and the register together.
`readPublishedGrades` hides a withdrawn sitting's marks **deliberately** — *"the
sitting was withdrawn (R59), and the mark went with it"* — which is right for a
cancelled sitting and wrong for a delivered one, and nothing told the two apart.

**The line is student educational evidence, and only that.** Any
`StudentExamSubmission` in any state, or any `Grade` in any status, refuses the
deletion; either alone is sufficient. State does not soften it — an
`in_progress` submission is work somebody did and a `draft` Grade is a mark
somebody awarded — and neither table carries `deleted_at`, so the question is a
plain count. The refusal is a `STATE_CONFLICT` carrying
`STUDENT_EVIDENCE_EXISTS` and **the two counts and nothing else**: a number makes
it actionable where a name would put a beneficiary in an error message (TD-14).

**Publication does not block, and attendance does not block.** Publishing
creates no student record — a published paper nobody sat is still a plan, which
is R118 clause (1)'s rule for schedules applied to the same shape of question.
R123 makes attendance a fact about the **occurrence** rather than about
achievement, and blocking on it would make a cancelled sitting undeletable
because one person was marked present at it. Both directions are asserted: a
guard that only ever refuses is as wrong as one that never does.

**The guard is the transaction's first statement.** A refusal therefore rolls
back before the cancellation notification, the staff tombstones, the `Exam`
tombstone, the Trash snapshot and the audit row — a blocked delete must leave
nothing behind suggesting one happened, and the suite asserts each of those five
absences. It is asserted **inside** the transaction rather than on an optimistic
pre-read, because a submission may arrive between the two.

**No `cancelled` state was introduced and grade visibility was not touched.**
Both were considered and both are separate decisions.

**R59 clause (3)'s rationale is corrected, not rewritten.** It admitted `Exam`
to the restorable set because *"its deletion cascades to exactly one child
table, `ExamStaff`"* — true in July 2026, false since R123 attached `attendance`
and R124 attached four more. §0 keeps the historical sentence, because erasing it
would erase the fact that the premise expired; `docs/SRS-PROPOSAL-R126.md` states
the current reasoning as its own clause. Restorability now holds for a
**narrower row and a better reason**: the deletable class is exactly the exams
carrying no student evidence, and for such a row the cascade really is
`ExamStaff` alone. Questions and options may exist on a deletable exam, are not
part of the cascade, and their `RESTRICT` keys are what stop a **permanent
purge** — `purgeEntry` turns PostgreSQL's refusal into `DEPENDENTS_EXIST`, which
is unchanged and is pinned by a test.

**The screen names what holds the assessment.** «تعذّر الحذف» alone is true and
unactionable — the administrator reads it and clicks the same button again — so
the scheduling page now reads the typed reason and reports the counts, following
the Trash screen's `DEPENDENTS_EXIST` precedent. The two placeholders are pinned
by a contract test, because dropping one while editing the Arabic is neither a
type error nor visible to a render test that never triggers a 409.

**Tests: 14/14** in a new `exam-deletion.integration.test.ts` against a real
schema, plus 14/14 on the scheduling contract guard. The suite is the audit the
preflight asked for: both permissive cases, four blocking cases, both
non-blocking dependents, the combined case, four authorization cases (including
a Super Admin meeting the same refusal — evidence is not a permission) and the
FK-safe purge.

## R127 — save-and-resume is the only response policy (2026-09-03)

**Owner decision.** R124's ratified §4.6 says *«a student saves an incomplete
draft and returns to it»* **unconditionally**; §17 said *«single_submission
exams skip resume»*. Both were in force. The Owner resolved it in R124's
favour: SAVE ≠ SUBMIT, a saved response stays resumable, and Final Submit is
explicit and final. There is one policy.

**What `access_policy` actually was.** Present since the initial migration,
`NOT NULL DEFAULT 'save_and_resume'`, from the R56/R58 online-exam design — the
same family in which `online` was declared and then refused. Audited at
`652ce3f`: one writer (the dev seed fixture), one reader (`accessPolicy: true`
in `ASSESSMENT_SELECT`, **selected and never branched on**), no write boundary,
a `SINGLE_SUBMISSION_FINAL` code with **no thrower for its whole life**, no UI,
and a frontend guard pinning it off the wire. R124's drafting record calls it one
of four things *"dormant and now used"* — true of the other three, not of this
one, and R127 corrects that.

**Removed, not documented as dormant.** *An unused capability is still a
capability* (R111 clause 2): an empty alternative is a declared behaviour a
future maintainer implements by guessing its semantics from the enum name. §18
made it worse by listing *"single_submission vs save_and_resume enforced"* as an
**acceptance criterion** for behaviour that never existed — so §18 could not be
satisfied by any implementation.

**The migration refuses rather than assumes.** Every row carries a value, so
*"is the column empty"* is not the question it was for `user.notes`. The question
is whether anybody recorded the **withdrawn** intention, so
`20260904100000_drop_exam_access_policy` counts `single_submission` rows, refuses
with the count if any exist, and lets default rows through without comment — a
default is what every row got for free, not a decision somebody made. It is a
TD-6b **contract-phase** drop with no expand and no migrate step, deliberately: a
transitional column would preserve exactly the capability being withdrawn.

**Verified in both directions.** Localhost read-only before writing: 1 exam,
1 `save_and_resume`, 0 `single_submission`. Then `migrate deploy` against the
**existing** database, and the **full chain against a fresh disposable
database** — after each, neither the column nor the enum type exists. Staging was
not queried and Production is not deployed; the guard is what makes the check
authoritative wherever it runs. The initial migration is untouched: the column's
history stays in the chain where it happened.

**Two guards restated rather than deleted.** `errors.test.ts` already closed the
catalogue by exact key set, which refuses a re-addition with a diff and no
reason; it now also pins `CAPACITY_FULL` and `SINGLE_SUBMISSION_FINAL` as retired
**by name**, with the shared reason — an unraisable code invites somebody to find
a use for it (TD-3.8). The scheduling form's wire guard keeps `access_policy` in
its list: the property it now protects is *a withdrawn field does not come back
through this form*, which is exactly what dropping the assertion would let
through silently.

**Tests: 56/56** on the assessment suite — including the three properties the
decision turns on, all pre-existing and reused rather than rewritten: a partial
draft saves as `in_progress` (16), reopening finds exactly what was written (17),
and a submitted response refuses further edits with `ALREADY_SUBMITTED` (18–19).
Plus 8/8 on the error catalogue and 9/9 on the scheduling-exam wire guard.

## R128 — a rejected family link is soft-deleted with its decision (2026-09-03)

**Owner decision.** `FamilyLinkStatus` is `pending | approved | rejected`, and
the TD-6 partial unique index keeps one live row per `(student, parent)`. A
**revoked** link is soft-deleted, so it leaves that index and the pair can be
requested again — §4.3 says so in terms. A **rejected** link was never
soft-deleted, so it stayed **live** and occupied the index forever.

**The weaker outcome was therefore the more permanent one.** An adult refused
because the relationship was recorded wrongly could never submit a corrected
request for the same child, while an adult whose *approved* authority was
deliberately revoked could. The only way out was an administrator invoking a
destructive route — a **deletion performed to enable a correction**.

**One shape with two endings now.** A rejection writes, atomically: the status,
the decision instant, the decider, the reason, `deleted_at`/`deleted_by` stamped
with **that same instant**, a Trash snapshot carrying the decision itself, and a
`familylink.reject` audit row with both ids, the reason and no name (TD-14). A
corrected request is a **NEW `pending` row** with its own id; the old refusal is
never reopened, reused or overwritten, and no `rejected → pending` transition
exists. Live uniqueness is untouched — a second pending request and a request
against an approved link are both still refused.

**R118.3 is superseded, and its machinery is withdrawn.** That revision gave the
stuck row a special exit; this one removes the reason it was stuck. `DELETE
/admin/family-links/{id}/rejected`, the `familylink.purge_rejected` audit action
and the `NOT_TERMINAL_REJECTED` code — whose only thrower it was — are gone from
TD-3, the router, the controller, the service, the OpenAPI document and the
tests. Nothing is lost: `PURGEABLE` already carried `FamilyLink`, so the ordinary
Trash lifecycle performs exactly what the special route performed, under BR-15's
ninety days, with one implementation instead of two. §0's R118 text is **not**
edited — it records what was decided on 2026-09-02 — and the audit document that
proposed it carries a superseding note rather than a rewrite.

**One implementation, because a rule stated twice drifts.** The registration
bundle and the standalone queue item both wrote the same four-field update
inline; they now share `decideLink`, which owns the status write, the rejection's
tombstone and snapshot, and the audit row for both arms. The bundle path
therefore **gains** a per-link audit row it never had — it wrote `user.reject`
alone, so a link's decision was attributable only through its parent, and §20
rule 11 requires a destructive step to carry its own evidence.

**The safeguarding property is proved, not assumed.** `FamilyLink` is absent from
the Trash restore plan and blocked as `CASCADE_RELATIONSHIPS`, so a generic
restore is refused by name however senior the caller — a rejected link can never
come back as live authority. The test asserts the refusal *and* that the row is
still tombstoned afterwards.

**The migration does not stamp today.** `20260904110000_soft_delete_rejected_family_links`
tombstones each legacy row with **its own decision instant** — the deletion is
the decision, and `now()` would claim the refusal happened today — carries
`decided_by` across as `deleted_by`, falls back to `created_at` where no decision
instant exists so no edge case stays live, changes no status, destroys nothing,
and writes a Trash entry per row while skipping rows that already have one.
Verified read-only first: Localhost 3 links (1 pending, 1 approved, 1 rejected,
all live). Proved against the existing database and a fresh disposable one.
Staging was not queried; Production is not deployed.

**Tests: 32/32** on the family-link suite — the decision's four recorded fields
and the tombstone sharing the decision's instant; no authority through
`resolveActingStudent`; the Trash snapshot carrying the reason; the audit row
with ids and no name; **the corrected request succeeding with a new id while the
old rejection stays historical**; both live-uniqueness refusals; revoke refusing a
rejected link; first-wins on a second decision; the restore refusal; an approval
still leaving live authority with its own audit row; and the obsolete route
absent from both the registry and the router. Plus 49/49 with the approval suite
and 58/58 on child-application and registration.

## R129 — a platform account is not association membership (2026-09-03)

**Owner decision, and an audit that found the model already right and
unstated.** Three services carried this distinction between them: R62 leaves
`mustEnrol` empty for an applicant who arrived with child applications, R79.3
writes `User.isBeneficiary` from `admitted` — which *is* the set the approval
enrolled — and the `student` role is granted from that same set. A guardian is
therefore excluded from all three **together, for one reason**. Good design,
invisible: nothing asserted it end to end, and the failure would have been silent
and severe — a guardian in a beneficiary list, or handed a personal educational
record she never had.

**No schema, route or wire contract changed.** What was missing was the
statement, the tests and the name.

**Guardian-only is now a named model.** Fatima authenticates in order to
register and manage Sara; she needs the ordinary `User` + `UserIdentity`
machinery for authentication, authorization, consent and `FamilyLink`, and that
is all it means. **No `GuardianAccount` table and no second authentication
architecture** — a parallel account type would fork authentication, consent,
deletion and audit for a distinction the existing model already expresses.

**Her email is hers, and an earlier over-absolute reading is superseded.** The
address on a guardian's authenticated identity **may also serve as that same
guardian's contact address**; `contact_email` must not be added merely to copy
the same string, because a second column holding one value is a second source of
truth and on this project every duplicate has drifted. What it must never mean is
*«Sara authenticates as fatima@example.com»* — the address is never copied into
the child's identity, the child's `preProvisionedEmail`, `ChildApplication` or
`FamilyLink`. A child created through the minor flow has **no email at all**, and
R62.9 makes that the definition of a minor. When messaging exists, a message
concerning a child will resolve the **live approved guardian** and read her
current address rather than a stored copy. There is still no email, SMS or push
provider.

**The generic `email` projection keeps its wire name.** `profile.service.ts` and
`user.service.ts` both project `identities[0]?.email ?? preProvisionedEmail` as
`email`, and the audit asked whether that reads as contact information: **the
screen labels it «بريد Google»**, so it already reads as the login address.
Renaming it would break clients to fix a label that is not wrong, so the
invariant is documented instead — `email` is the account's **authentication**
address, never a per-person contact field and never another person's.

**A guardian who joins the association keeps her account.** She submits the
ordinary process and the beneficiary state attaches to the row she already has;
her `FamilyLink`s stay intact. The mechanism needed nothing added — all three
facts are written against a `userId` and none is created by registration — so the
gap is a *screen*, not a model, and that screen is separate work.

**Tests: 38/38** on the registration suite, six of them new: approving a guardian
admits her to nothing; she holds no student, teacher, admin or super-admin role;
she is absent from the beneficiaries list; she carries no Grade, Attendance,
submission, Quran log or teaching-group seat; her email reaches neither the
child's identity nor the child's pre-provisioned address and exactly one account
claims it; and the upgrade attaches to the same `User` with her family links
surviving.

**New page:** `docs/development/account-and-membership.md`, indexed from
`docs/README.md`. `docs/SRS-PROPOSAL-R129.md` awaits application. Two stale
`TASKS.md` items are closed, including one that recorded the *opposite* email
model — *a minor signs in through her guardian's address* — which this decision
supersedes.

## R130 — a full date of birth for every beneficiary (2026-09-03)

**Owner decision.** Every beneficiary carries one — adult women, teenagers and
children alike — for four stated purposes: minor/adult status, age-appropriate
placement, registration and placement decisions, and eligibility for the
controlled transition to a self-managed account.

**The question follows ADMISSION, not authentication.** On the adult path the
applicant *is* the beneficiary, so she is asked. Every child on a family request
is asked, per child, because two siblings are two people. A **guardian** is not
asked — R129 admits her to nothing — and a **staff request is refused** if it
sends one, rather than having it ignored: accepting it would collect a
beneficiary's personal datum from somebody who is not one.

**Two columns, one fact.** `User.birth_date` is the durable answer, beside `sex`,
which is the other fact captured about the person themselves.
`ChildApplication.birth_date` is the SUBMITTED answer, because the child `User`
does not exist until approval (R62) — and approval **copies** the same calendar
date onto the row it creates rather than recomputing it, so the evidence and the
authority cannot disagree. Both are `DATE`, never a timestamp: an instant puts a
person's birthday a day earlier for half the world.

**One module owns the rule.** `lib/birth-date.ts` holds the parse, the real
calendar check, the future bound, the plausibility floor and the eighteen-year
predicate. Four boundaries need the same answer and one has a safeguarding
consequence. `new Date('2010-02-31')` is the 3rd of March **silently**, which is
why this does not reuse the shared `calendarDate` validator — the components are
read back. The browser runs a deliberately **thinner** pre-check, so it can only
be quieter than the server, never in conflict with it.

**No age is stored, and the test asserts it against `information_schema`** rather
than against a memory of the schema. An age is wrong the day after it is written.

**Eighteen establishes eligibility and triggers nothing.** No birthday job, no
automatic family-link revocation, no automatic identity binding, no role change.
The property is the **absence** of machinery, so it is guarded by asserting that
no job source names the column — such a thing could only exist to act on a
birthday.

**Legacy rows, stated honestly.** Read-only first: Localhost 73 users (18 live),
25 beneficiaries, **0 with a birth date**, 4 child applications. Nothing is
fabricated and nothing is inferred — not from a Category, a schooling stage, an
enrolment or a row's creation date — and no sentinel is used, because a sentinel
is indistinguishable from a recorded fact a year later. The column is therefore
nullable with the requirement at the **write boundary**, exactly as R122 did for
`enrollment.academic_period_id`. **The `NOT NULL` contraction is not performed
and cannot honestly be performed until every live beneficiary has a real date** —
recorded as a pending sub-task rather than claimed as complete.

**Completion uses the surface that already exists.** `PATCH /admin/users/{id}`,
Super Admin only since R112, accepts `birth_date` under the same TD-15 version
check and TD-12 freshness assertion as every other field. No new route, no new
role. **Completion, never correction**: a change to a recorded date is refused as
`BIRTH_DATE_ALREADY_RECORDED`, on the R80.3 pattern and for the R80.3 reason.
Re-sending the *same* date is idempotent, because a form that hydrates the field
and saves an unrelated change resends it.

**Privacy.** The audit row names the **field, never the value** — asserted, not
assumed. Published on the Super-Admin-only `/admin/users` read and nowhere else:
not `/admin/directory`, not any beneficiary list, the same exact narrowing R80.6
applies to `sex`. Never logged, never an identifier, never accompanied by an age.

**One shared control gained a documented variant** rather than a second
implementation (rule C): `TextField` accepts `type="date"`, whose native value is
already `YYYY-MM-DD` — exactly what the TD-11 boundary expects, with no parsing
on either side. `PersonFields` gained a **required** `collectBirthDate` prop, not
a defaulted one: rule AE warns that a behaviour each caller must opt into will be
missing somewhere, and the two callers here want opposite answers, so TypeScript
refuses a caller that has not decided.

**A defect the types caught while it was being written.** `ChildApplicationInput`
gained `birthDate` as **required**, and the compiler immediately named the second
write boundary — the student-area `POST /child-applications` — which would
otherwise have accepted a child with no date. Before that, a mapped object
literal dropped the field silently and it reached the database as NULL with
nothing failing.

**Tests: 48/48** registration (12 new), **45/45** user service (5 new), **26/26**
child-application, **10/10** the birth-date unit suite, **16/16** the users wire
contract. Frontend typecheck, lint and build clean; fresh migration chain and the
existing database both verified; OpenAPI regenerated and reconciled against the
live router.

## Minor → adult account transition — direction recorded, workflow deliberately not built (2026-09-03)

**The Owner's direction is settled and the workflow is blocked on a safeguarding
decision, so it is recorded rather than improvised.**

Settled: a former minor who needs her own login has a **verified Google identity
bound to her existing beneficiary `User`** — never a second account, so
`Enrollment`, `Grade`, Quran progress, attendance and assessments stay on one
person. Eligibility comes from date of birth at 18 (R130), **nothing happens on a
birthday**, and once the account is self-managed she exercises her own rights: a
historical `FamilyLink` makes nobody the owner of an adult's account.

**The binding mechanism already exists and needs no work.** §4.1b resolves a
sign-in by verified email, finds the account carrying that `pre_provisioned_email`
and binds the real Google subject to it, creating no second row.

**The blocker is exact, and it is a refusal that exists on purpose.** No
operation points an *existing* account at an address: `preProvision` only
creates, and `PATCH /admin/users/{id}` refuses `pre_provisioned_email` by name
because *"it authorises claiming an account (§7 R15)"*. The missing piece is
therefore not a screen but an answer to **who attests that this Google address
belongs to the young woman herself** — and the question is sharp because of who
is available to ask. A minor has no login and cannot request it; the only party
who can act for her is her guardian, who is precisely the party an anti-takeover
control must guard against. Letting a guardian nominate the address would let her
bind a second account of her own to her daughter's record and inherit the entire
educational history.

Staff pre-provisioning does not have this problem — a Super Admin has the address
from the person, out of band, and can say who confirmed it. The equivalent
trusted channel for a former beneficiary is a safeguarding decision about how the
association identifies people, not an engineering choice.

**Recorded with an explicit prohibition:** do not add a generic *set the
pre-provisioned email on an existing account* capability to close the gap. It
would reopen that claim path for every account on the platform in order to serve
one workflow nobody has specified.

## R131 — two deletion requests, ten-year retention, and the end of R111 ↔ R122 (2026-09-03)

**Policy and architecture only. Nothing is implemented and no deletion behaviour
changed.** The Owner's instruction was to ratify the policy first and write no
destructive automation until the cross-domain map is coherent — so this is the
map and the policy, and the purge job it would authorise is explicitly not
written.

**The map is measured, not remembered.** Re-enumerated from the live schema:
**42 foreign keys reference `user.id`**, against R111's 35 — 34 of R111's survive
(`student_social_profile` went with R120) and **8 were added since**, by R115,
R116, R119, R123 and R125. Each of the eight is classified with its reason, and
`docs/development/personal-data-map.md` **extends R111 §3 rather than restating
it**: R111 owns the base classification, the new page owns the delta and the
Option B column. The enumeration SQL is on the page, because the next person must
re-run it rather than trust the number.

**One judgment call, made by applying the Owner's own rule.**
`notification.subject_user_id` is a message about her sitting in **somebody
else's** inbox. R111 deletes `notification.user_id` because the person no longer
has an inbox; this one is another person's record, and *do not guess when
deletion would destroy another person's legitimate record* decides it —
PRESERVE, rendering as «حساب محذوف». Narrowing it is the Owner's to make.

**Ten years after the last educational activity, DERIVED.** A dedicated
`last_activity_at` column would be a fact nobody updates consistently, and a
retention clock driven by a stale column deletes the wrong records — so the
boundary is the latest of the canonical facts the platform already keeps: the
enrolment's `AcademicPeriod` end (R122), an attendance occurrence date (R123),
the date of the exam a Grade is against, a submission's instant, a Quran entry.
**It is the association's own purpose-based policy and is never to be described
as prescribed or approved by the CNDP.** Quran progression is treated as
educational history **for product purposes**, with no claim made about its legal
classification.

**Two requests that must never be presented as one.** Option A closes the
account and keeps the minimal archive; Option B requests deletion of the
educational record too, is **Super Admin only**, and is a request rather than a
browser-side cascade. Option B's screen must warn **before** confirmation that
the association may afterwards be unable to prove a previous level or issue an
attestation; the destructive option is never preselected and Option A is never
implied to destroy history. **Neither may promise "zero rows anywhere"** —
narrowly necessary evidence survives under its own rules — and equally **no
educational content may be kept under the pretext of audit** once full deletion
is approved. TD-8 already records fields and ids rather than values, so the audit
trail is not a hiding place.

**The R111 ↔ R122 contradiction is resolved.** R122 promised a future
attestation while R111 cleared every field that could match a returning person to
her record — including `referenceCode` — and neither cited the other. The Owner's
decision: **the code survives Option A** as protected pseudonymous data (the
archive is *not* anonymous merely because the login is gone), **is deleted by
Option B**, and **is never a back door** into history that Option B removed. Not
yet implemented: `deIdentifyAccount` still clears it, and that is Option A work.

**Guardian cleanup is one controlled operation, never a cascade.** When the last
child-management purpose is deliberately removed, a guardian-only account is
closed through the established machinery — but only after atomically verifying
she is not a beneficiary, staff, an administrator, holder of another live link,
of a pending application, or of any other current purpose. Removing one of
several children leaves her; a rejected link (R128) beside a still-pending
application leaves her.

**Applications: twelve months, a maximum and not indefinite retention** — long
enough to carry the decision and its reason through the academic cycle and to
prevent an accidental re-acceptance within it.

**Backups are described honestly.** A deletion reaches the live system;
finite-lifetime encrypted backups may hold an older copy until they expire, and
nothing may promise byte-level erasure from backups already written. A restore
must not silently resurrect deleted data — recorded as operational work, with no
infrastructure deployed.

**The active `LegalConsentText` is untouched.** The six wording changes R131
requires are enumerated in `TASKS.md` for the Document Owner to draft, review and
activate.

## Email-lock keying — ratified design, blocked on one operational precondition (2026-09-03)

**The Owner authorised implementing this in the same session only if six
conditions held. Condition 5 does not, so this is the design and the migration is
not written.**

`normalized_email_lock` is a **concurrency primitive**, not a directory: the
invariant *one live account per email* spans `user_identity.email` and
`user.pre_provisioned_email`, either row may be absent, and same-table uniqueness
therefore cannot close the check-then-insert race. The defect it exists for is
real — `POST /admin/users` once answered `201` for an address that already had a
live active identity. The problem is that its primary key is the **raw lowercased
address**, and after a permanent de-identification that row survives, ownerless,
with no purpose statement or retention rule.

**The design.** `HMAC-SHA-256(EMAIL_LOCK_KEY, "bodour.email-lock.v1|" +
normalized)`, hex, as the primary key. **HMAC rather than bare `SHA-256(email)`**
because the space of real addresses is small and enumerable — an unkeyed digest
is the address in a thin disguise — and the domain-separation prefix means a
digest here can never equal one computed elsewhere under the same key. The
canonical input is the address after the `trim()`/`toLowerCase()` every ownership
path already applies and **nothing else**: dot-stripping or plus-tag removal
would change *which addresses collide*, which is an authentication decision, not
a storage one.

**A new required secret, not a reused one.** Borrowing `JWT_SIGNING_KEY` would
couple routine session-key rotation to the integrity of every email lock. Making
the new variable optional would be worse still: a raw-email fallback yields two
key spaces and silently breaks the invariant wherever it fell back.

**Rotation, secret loss and migration are one answer: truncate and continue.** A
digest is a lookup key, so re-keying needs the plaintext this design stops
storing — there is deliberately no dual-key lookup and no computed backfill. It
is safe because the rows are **disposable by nature**: they carry no ownership
and no history, ownership lives in the two authoritative channels, and any
address still in use re-establishes its lock on the next claim. A collision would
cost liveness, not correctness, since the authoritative re-read still consults
both channels. Transaction semantics and lock ordering are unchanged, so no new
deadlock class appears. `deIdentifyAccount` locks by digest and still does **not**
delete lock rows — that reasoning was always right.

**The blocker, exactly.** `EMAIL_LOCK_KEY` must join `REQUIRED_ENV_VARS`, and a
missing required variable throws `MissingRequiredEnvError` at boot by design
(TD-13 gives secrets no defaults). Shipping the code first would break the next
Staging deploy — a mutation this session is forbidden to make, with a secret it
must not invent. **The order is: install the secret in Localhost and Staging,
then implement.** Everything else — design, migration shape, unchanged auth
behaviour, and eight named tests including the original race and an
`information_schema` assertion that no raw address remains — is ready for one
pass.

## Test isolation — a suite was destroying audit rows it did not own (2026-09-03)

**Found by the all-table isolation guard during the final gate**, as `audit_log`
losing two pre-existing rows across a full run.

`refresh-token.integration.test.ts` cleaned up with

```ts
await prisma.auditLog.deleteMany({ where: { targetEntity: "User", actorUserId: null } });
```

— **no ownership term at all.** System-written audit rows carry
`actor_user_id = NULL`, so they cannot be found through the tagged actor the rest
of that cleanup uses, and the predicate was widened until it matched them. What
it actually matched was *every* system-written User audit row in the database,
whoever wrote it and whenever. In Local Development that quietly destroyed real
audit history on every run; pointed at a populated database it would destroy
audit evidence, which is the one record §7's attribution invariant says must
survive everything else.

**The fix scopes by target instead**, resolving the suite's own tagged user ids
first — the same shape the earlier P1.2 isolation fix used for staffing — and
deletes nothing when that list is empty, which is the correct first-run
behaviour rather than a reason to widen the predicate again.

**Proved against the defect rather than assumed.** An unowned system-written
probe row was inserted, then: with the fix it survives (1 → 1); with the old
predicate temporarily restored it is destroyed (1 → 0). A cleanup guard that has
never been shown to catch its own defect is not protection.

Also corrected in the same pass: `children.test.ts`'s child factory predated
R130 and built children with no birth date, so five of its cases failed once the
requirement landed. The fixture now carries one, with the rule named beside it.

**The other half of the drift was not a defect.** The HTTP suites in the same run
were failing against an `api` image built before R126–R130; rebuilding it from
current source is a documented precondition of those files, and the gate was
re-run against the rebuilt image.

## The guardian was being asked for a date of birth (2026-09-03)

**Found by the browser harness, and a source test could not have found it.**

R130's date control went into `NameFields` — the block `PersonFields` shares
with the **guardian** — so a woman registering her daughter was asked for her
own date of birth, which R129 says must never happen. The `collectBirthDate`
gate on `PersonFields` looked correct while being **dead**: the field arrived
from the shared block whatever the gate said.

Both callers compiled and both rendered. What showed it was **counting the
controls on a real page**: three date inputs for two children.

**The harness now asserts the shape rather than the count.** It reports the
`<legend>` of the fieldset each date field sits in, so a regression reads
`["بيانات ولي الأمر", …]` — naming *whose* form grew a field — instead of
`3 ≠ 2`. A count would have told a future reader that something was wrong; this
tells them what.

**One diagnostic trap cost most of the time and is worth recording**: `docker
compose up -d --build nginx` rebuilt the image and **did not recreate the
container**, so the browser kept serving the previous bundle and the fix looked
ineffective. The tell was comparing the served asset hash with a local
`vite build` — `index-C5lNaLiI.js` against `index-H7wkzRVs.js`. `--force-recreate`
is what makes a frontend change reach the harness.

**verify-registration: 44/44** — the two dates in the two children's fieldsets,
none in the guardian's, and the registration itself `201`.

## R132 — a beneficiary claims her own account at 18 (2026-09-03)

**The blocker recorded that morning is closed, and the refusal that created it
still stands.** A former minor is a login-less `User` (R62.9) holding her whole
educational history, and nothing could give her a login: no operation points an
EXISTING account at an address, because `PATCH /admin/users/{id}` refuses
`pre_provisioned_email` *"because it authorises claiming an account"*. That
refusal is correct and is untouched — R132 adds the one controlled path whose
absence it was protecting, not a general capability.

**Three facts, and none of them binds alone.** Google OAuth — the ordinary flow,
unchanged, PKCE and state protections intact — proves somebody controls *this*
Google identity. Her **reference code** names *which* beneficiary is claimed, and
grants nothing on its own (R62.5), which is exactly why it is safe to quote down
a telephone and why quoting it cannot take anybody's account. A **Super Admin**
then performs the association-side identity match, using the recognition the
association already practises. **Only the third binds** — and no CIN, no
identity-document scan and no invented automated identity proofing was
introduced to eliminate the human step.

**The guardian is excluded by construction, which was the whole blocker.** She
cannot choose the address, type it as the authoritative identity, attest
ownership, bind it, approve the transition or receive any credential, and her own
address never becomes the beneficiary's. The beneficiary proves control herself.

**Approval binds to the EXISTING `User`.** No second account, ever: enrolments,
grades, Quran progression, attendance, submissions, the reference code and every
relationship stay on the one id — asserted by counting users across the flow and
by reading her enrolment back afterwards.

**Age is eligibility and never a trigger.** R130's date decides whether she may
*ask*; nothing happens on a birthday. Boundary-tested at exactly 18 and one day
short, with a guard asserting no job source names the column.

**Reuse rather than new machinery.** The OAuth callback, the single-use
onboarding token carrying the VERIFIED email and subject (§20 rule 9), the
`ConsumedToken` replay guard, the `Email → User` lock order, the audit
conventions and the existing field primitives are all reused unchanged. The
public entry point is a **fourth mode on `/register`**, not a fourth page.

**The refusals are mostly uniform, on purpose.** Every condition about the
claimed person — unknown code, under 18, no recorded date, already has a login,
suspended, deleted, already claimed — answers identically, because distinguishing
them would report whether `BA-XXXXX` exists and whether that person is a minor
(§20 rule 17). Only conditions about the caller's **own** identity are named,
since those disclose nothing she does not control.

**At approval every precondition is re-read inside the transaction**, because a
claim may have waited days. An existing identity is never overwritten and never
reassigned; both ownership channels and `(provider, subject)` are re-checked; a
beneficiary who became ineligible fails **closed and by name**, so an
administrator learns the claim is stale rather than that the button is broken.

**Rejection follows R128's shape** — recorded, then withdrawn from the live set —
so a refusal never blocks the corrected request, and a retry is a NEW claim with
its own history rather than the old one reopened.

**Authority after the transition, without deleting history.** The child-context
resolver now refuses a `FamilyLink` whose student holds an active login identity
— **the same fact R62.9 already uses** to define a minor, read in one more place
instead of duplicated into a flag that could disagree with it. The link row
survives as relationship evidence; what ends is the current authority. The
control case is tested too: a 19-year-old who never transitioned still has her
guardian, because the rule turns on the **login**, not the age.

**Privacy.** The audit carries ids only — no OAuth token, no PKCE verifier, no
raw state, no Google subject, no address, no reference code, no birth date,
asserted rather than assumed. The review surface publishes who is claimed, which
record and which address becomes the login; the Google subject is absent from the
contract itself, and the birth date is absent because it decided eligibility
before the row existed.

**Tests: 28/28** in a new suite covering every numbered requirement; plus 117/117
across the family-link, registration, child-application and auth suites, and
42/42 on child-context and Quran, which the middleware change touches. Frontend
1013/1013, typecheck, lint and build clean. Fourteen CI guards green, fresh
migration chain verified, OpenAPI reconciled at 166 paths.

### R132 follow-up — two defects the browser found (2026-09-03)

`verify-self-managed-claim.sh` drives the whole journey and asserts the central
invariant from the database: **`claims=1 pending=1 identities=0`** — a pending
claim exists and nothing is bound. Writing it found two defects in code that
typechecked, linted and passed every unit test.

**A silent dead end.** The claim arm's early return in `validate` sat *after*
`person(state.applicant, …)`, so a form whose name fields are not even rendered
was permanently invalid: `valid` stayed false and the submit button did nothing
at all — no error, no request, no explanation. Now returned before, and pinned by
four cases.

**The wrong success message.** A claim rendered the *registration* wording,
telling her an application had been received and would be decided. That is true
of a different thing; she asked to be given a login on a record that already
exists, and what she needs to read is that nothing has changed yet.

Also corrected while verifying: the arm rendered the entire registration body —
name, branch, stage, consents — which would have asked her to restate what the
association already holds. The claim panel is now the only thing that arm shows.

**13/13 browser checks**, including that the Super Admin can find the claim (rule
P, on the most takeover-sensitive decision the platform offers) and that the
Google provider subject appears nowhere in the page's text or markup.

## R126–R132 applied to the authoritative SRS (2026-09-03)

**Specification reconciliation, on the Document Owner's instruction.** `SRS.md`
is immutable to an implementing agent (§20 rule 20) and revisions are drafted as
proposals for the Owner to apply; the Owner directed this application of the
seven already-ratified decisions. **No product code changed.**

**§0 gains seven revision entries** (126–132), newest first. Each states what was
decided and what it supersedes; **no historical §0 paragraph was rewritten** —
R58's, R59's and R118's stand as the record of what was decided when, with the
corrections carried as the new revisions' own clauses, which is the convention
R126 and R128 each specified.

**Sections reconciled.** §4.1 gains the account-versus-membership model, the
email semantics, the date-of-birth requirement, the self-managed claim and the
reference-code invariant; §4.3 gains the rejection lifecycle and the
post-transition authority rule; §4.6 gains the deletion rule and loses the access
policy; §4.10 gains §4.10a (retention and the two deletion requests); §5, §7,
§17, §18, TD-1, TD-2, TD-3 and TD-8 follow.

**Contradictions found and closed.** The withdrawn assessment policy was still
described in **seven** current normative places — §4.6, §5, §7, §17 twice, TD-3
and a §18 acceptance criterion that named behaviour which never existed. Beyond
the seven revisions, the sweep found **R124's manual-marking decision unapplied
in five places**: TD-1's submission machine still transitioned
`submitted → auto_graded (MCQ engine, immediate)`, and §5, §9, §17 and TD-3 still
described MCQ auto-grading. These were reconciled to R124's own ratified text
rather than decided afresh; the enum values are described honestly as retained
but never written, because the marking engine is postponed rather than cancelled.

**Three things deliberately stated rather than smoothed over.** §4.10a opens with
a status line saying **no destructive automation exists** and that the deletion
the platform performs is still R111's; §4.1 records that the `NOT NULL`
contraction on the birth date **has not been performed** and cannot be until
every live beneficiary carries a real date; and the ten-year retention is stated
as **the association's own purpose-based policy**, explicitly not as a CNDP
prescription — the only other CNDP reference in the document is §3's data
residency claim, which is about residency and is untouched.

**The reference-code invariant is now normative**: possessing, knowing or
supplying one is not authentication, not proof of identity, not proof of
Google-account ownership, not authorization, not entitlement to access or to a
transition, and not sufficient evidence for an approval. It is a locator.

**Proposal lifecycle.** All seven proposals move from *RATIFIED — awaiting
application* to **APPLIED**, following the R109/R110/R123/R124 convention: the
files are kept as the drafting record and `SRS.md` becomes authoritative. The
handbook index and `TASKS.md` no longer claim any of them awaits application.

**Not touched, deliberately:** `EMAIL_LOCK_KEY` (still blocked on its secret),
the active `LegalConsentText`, `notification.subject_user_id`'s provisional
classification, the adult-Category marker, and R59.4 — each recorded as open
rather than decided here.

## R131 Option A — account closure keeps the reference code (2026-09-04)

**Option A only.** Option B is not implemented, no retention automation was
written, and the R131 map's remaining reconciliations are untouched.

**The correction.** R122 committed the association to answering *«كنت أدرس عندكم
وأريد شهادة تثبت المستوى الذي وصلت إليه»* years later; R111's de-identification
cleared every field that could match a returning person to her preserved record —
**including `reference_code`** — and neither cited the other. R131 resolved it:
**the code survives Option A** as part of the protected minimal educational
archive. `deIdentifyAccount` no longer clears it.

**One line was not enough, which is the interesting part.** `hadIdentitySurface`
decides *both* whether `qr_ref` rotates *and* whether a `user.deidentify` audit
row is written, and it counted `reference_code`. Removing only the assignment
would have left that predicate permanently true — so every retry of an
**idempotent** job would rotate the QR again and write another audit row, making
one operation look like repeated human decisions. The field had to leave the
predicate in the same change, and a test now pins it.

**Preserving the code opens no way back in, and that is structural rather than
promised.** A reference code is a **locator** (R62.5, R132): the one surface that
takes one resolves a beneficiary `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`, and a closed account
is by definition soft-deleted — so quoting the code of a closed account gets the
same uniform refusal as a code that never existed. It is also never reissued:
`allocateReferenceCode` counts rows regardless of `deleted_at`, so the value stays
taken and no future beneficiary receives it.

**Three security-sensitive cases were checked and already hold, and are now
asserted rather than assumed.** A **pending self-managed claim** approved after
closure fails closed (`BENEFICIARY_INELIGIBLE`), so a closed account cannot
acquire a login through a stale claim. A **guardian-only** closure touches no
child record and does not make the guardian a beneficiary. And closing a
**self-managed adult's** account does not hand her back to a former guardian —
worth an explicit test because closure *deletes her `UserIdentity`*, which
satisfies the very clause (*an account with no active login identity*) that ended
the guardian's authority; what still refuses him is that the resolver requires a
live student. It holds by a second clause rather than by the one expressing the
intent, so it is pinned.

**DOB is untouched, deliberately.** R131's map does not classify the birth date
for Option A, and the current operation does not clear it. **No decision was made
here** — the test asserts the existing behaviour so a future change is a
deliberate one, and the unresolved classification is recorded rather than guessed.

**The wording now distinguishes closure from deletion.** «حذف الحساب» became
«إغلاق الحساب» on both surfaces, and the copy states that the educational record,
the reference code and the consent records remain **for the association's own
retention purpose**, that this is **not** a request to delete everything, and that
full deletion is a separate request to the administration. Four guards assert the
copy says the record remains, never promises total erasure, **claims no external
legal mandate for the period**, and does not offer the unimplemented Option B.

**Two pre-existing test defects surfaced and were fixed rather than hidden.** The
R132 suite asserted `selfManagedClaim.count()` with **no predicate** — a global
count that passed only while one file created claims; it is now scoped. And the
new suite's fixture used a fixed reference-code sequence, which collides with any
row an interrupted run left behind now that closure *keeps* the code — the unique
index was doing its job and the fixture was wrong.

**Tests: 13/13** new Option A invariants, **99/99** across the closure,
self-managed-claim, family-link, auth and child-context suites. The correction was
**proved against the defect**: restoring the old `referenceCode: null` fails
exactly the preservation and idempotency tests, and nothing else.

## Durable self-managed authority (2026-09-04)

**Owner decision, and a latent security defect Option A had just created.**

R132 expressed *"she manages her own account"* as **an account with no active
login identity** — §4.3's structural test for a minor, reused so there would be
one definition rather than two. The reasoning was right and **the fact was
wrong**: Option A deliberately deletes `UserIdentity`, so from the moment account
closure shipped, a self-managed adult who closed her account satisfied *"no
active login"* again.

**Nothing actually broke, and that is the uncomfortable part.** The child-context
resolver separately requires a live student, and a closed account is
soft-deleted, so the guardian stayed refused — by a clause that has nothing to do
with authority. **Authority surviving by coincidence is authority that will not
survive the next change.** The test that proves the fix therefore clears the
tombstone, so only the durable fact can refuse him; run against the old rule,
**that one test fails and no other does**, which is exactly the claim being made.

**The model, now stated in three words each.** `DOB ≥ 18` → eligibility.
Approved claim → durable authority. `UserIdentity` → authentication mechanism.
Option A → removes authentication, removes no authority.

**Derived, not stored.** An approved `SelfManagedClaim` *is* the durable fact:
the row exists exactly when a Super Admin approved the transition, it is never
soft-deleted (only a rejected claim is), and it carries the decider and the
instant. A `User.self_managed_at` column beside it would be a second answer to
one question — the failure R79.3 and R128 each name — and the two could disagree
after a restore or a hand-written fix. **Legacy accounts are correct by
construction**: no approved claim exists for anybody who never transitioned, and
nothing consults age, role or credential.

**Three paths read it, and the third was the dangerous one.** The child-context
resolver and the child-linking candidate search both mis-read the fact; the
**linking write boundary** did too, and there it *grants* authority — a closed
self-managed adult has no login, so `ACCOUNT_HAS_LOGIN` would have let her be
linked as a child, **giving a guardian authority over an adult who had taken it
away**. Refused now as `ACCOUNT_SELF_MANAGED`.

**Schema:** one additive index on `(beneficiary_id, status)`, because the
derivation runs on every child-scoped request and the existing indexes cover
`status` alone and PENDING rows only. No column, constraint, enum or row changed;
nothing backfilled and no status fabricated.

**Tests: 11/11** new policy invariants covering all ten required cases — plus the
distinguishing test and a control proving the rule did not simply widen to refuse
everybody — and **126/126** across the six suites that touch authority, closure,
linking and family links.

**Three test fixtures encoded the OLD definition** and were corrected rather than
worked around: two suites built a "self-managed adult" by giving her a login,
which is the very reading this decision retires, and two cleanups did not know
about `SelfManagedClaim`'s two `Restrict` foreign keys.

## R131 Option B — the request/review control plane (2026-09-04)

**The control plane only. Nothing deletes, and every surface says so.**

Option A closes the account and keeps the minimal educational archive. Option B
asks for the archive itself to go, which **may make a future attestation
impossible** — so §4.10a makes it a request a Super Admin reviews, never a
cascade a browser can start. Destructive execution is a separate step, gated on
the cross-domain classifications §4.10a leaves open, and is deliberately not
implemented: **approval records a decision and deletes nothing**, and both the
API response and the audit row carry `executed: false` so no reader can mistake
one for the other. The suite asserts it the same way — after approval the
enrolment is still there and the account is untouched.

**The authority rule is why Section 2 had to come first.** §4.10a gives two
bases: a person may always ask about herself, and an adult may ask for a minor
only while holding a **live approved `FamilyLink`**. The prohibition — *a former
guardian of a self-managed adult has no basis* — was **not expressible** while
authority was read from the presence of a login identity, because Option A
deletes it: a closed self-managed adult would have looked like a minor again and
her former guardian could have asked for her educational record to be destroyed.
The service reads `isSelfManaged`, and the test that proves it is the one where
the link row survives and the request is still refused.

**The basis is recorded, not re-derived.** A relationship can change while a
request waits, and the reviewer needs to know what was true when it was made.

**Refusals about somebody else are uniform.** A stranger, a revoked guardian, a
pending link and an id that does not exist all answer `404` alike — whether
another person manages her own account is not a fact this endpoint may disclose.

**A table of its own, deliberately.** Reusing `SelfManagedClaim` or `FamilyLink`
because their columns look similar would put two lifecycles in one table, which
is how a destructive verb reaches the wrong row (R128). One live request per
subject is asserted in the service rather than by a partial unique index: a
refusal is soft-deleted precisely so she may ask again, and the constraint is
about pending work rather than history.

**The queue shows who and on what basis — and not the educational record.** A
reviewer deciding whether it may be destroyed has no need to read it, and a queue
that displayed it would be one more copy of the thing at issue. The audit carries
ids and the basis; no name, no date, no educational fact.

**Arabic copy states four things** and is guarded: this is a different request
from account closure; it may make an attestation impossible; a review follows and
**nothing is deleted yet**; and encrypted backups keep an older copy until they
expire, so **immediate erasure everywhere cannot be promised**. One guard had to
be rewritten during the work — a naive *"must not contain «محو فوري»"* matched
the very sentence that exists to deny it, so the assertion now checks the
negation rather than the phrase.

**Schema:** one new table with two enums and two indexes. **Tests: 14/14** new,
38/38 across the closure, authority and request suites. OpenAPI at 170 paths,
reconciled; TD-3 updated.

## The guardian-only cleanup guard, and a gap in my own reconciliation (2026-09-04)

**R131's §4.3 clause was ratified and never reached `SRS.md`.** The
reconciliation pass carried §4.10a's retention and deletion clauses and missed
the guardian-only one entirely. Corrected: §4.3 now states the rule, the seven
atomic conditions, the load-bearing distinctions and the implementation status.

**The guard is built; no trigger is wired.** `policies/guardian-purpose.ts`
answers *does this account still have a reason to exist?* and returns **every**
purpose it finds rather than the first — an operator reading it before an
irreversible act needs the whole picture. Beyond §4.3's seven conditions it adds
two the platform gained since: a **self-managed adult** is never a guardian-only
account (she took it over deliberately; holding no links now is not a reason to
take it back), and an **undecided full-deletion request** preserves both its
subject and its requester, because closing either would decide it by removal.

**The predicate is inclusive on purpose.** A missed purpose closes an account
that should have lived; a spurious one merely leaves an account alive. Those are
not symmetric, so the doubt runs one way.

**What is NOT wired, and why that is the honest outcome.** §4.3 says the decision
*belongs to the deliberate deletion operation* — and three candidates differ
materially: revoking the last approved link (soft, immediate, common), purging
that link from Trash (genuinely permanent, but housekeeping that may come much
later), and fully deleting the last child under Option B (not implemented).
Closing somebody's account is severe, so attaching it to whichever operation
happened to be picked would be inventing policy. Recorded as a precise decision.

**Tests: 11/11**, including §4.3's own named case — a rejected link beside a
still-pending child application preserves the account — and the control that a
report lists every purpose rather than stopping at the first.
